Titre$ Stalpers /Identification of wood-inhabiting Aphyllophorales in pure culture/1978
com$ Adaptation et corrections GF 03/2015
1,0$ Source : Studies in Mycology No. 16  Identification of wood-inhabiting Aphyllophorales in pure culture  J.A. STALPERS 

1,0$ Abstract: A descriptive analytical key is given for c. 550 spp. of wood-inhabiting Aphyllophorales classified in 15 families. Each sp. is characterized by a numerical code referring to 96 characters or circumscribed parts of characters. The species codes can be transferred to punch cards and thus the system can easily be used as a synoptic key. The distribution of clamp connexions and the occurrence of laccase, skeletal hyphae, interlocking hyphae and cuticular cells are used as main differentiating characters. Seven groups are distinguished which are keyed out separately. About 1100 strs. were tested for the presence of enzymes; the reactions with alpha -naphthol (on laccase), p-cresol (on tyrosinase) and pyrogallol and H2O2 (on peroxidase) were useful for taxonomic purposes. The taxonomic position of some spp. much alike in culture but classified in different groups is discussed.ADDITIONAL ABSTRACT:A descriptive analytical key is given for c. 550 spp. of wood-inhabiting Aphyllophorales classified in 15 families. Each sp. is characterized by a numerical code referring to 96 characters or circumscribed parts of characters. The species codes can be transferred to punch cards and thus the system can easily be used as a synoptic key. The distribution of clamp connexions and the occurrence of laccase, skeletal hyphae, interlocking hyphae and cuticular cells are used as main differentiating characters. Seven groups are distinguished which are keyed out separately. About 1100 strs. were tested for the presence of enzymes; the reactions with alpha -naphthol (on laccase), p-cresol (on tyrosinase) and pyrogallol and H2O2 (on peroxidase) were useful for taxonomic purposes. The taxonomic position of some spp. much alike in culture but classified in different groups is discussed.ADDITIONAL ABSTRACT:A descriptive analytical key to 550 species in 15 families that cause decay in living or dead trees, stored wood and timber.
Up to 1 more results found for ""Identification of wood-inhabiting Aphyllophorales in pure culture""

