1,cs1$a.  Clamps absent from the very margin; marginal hyphae usually rather wide (about 37 um), with simple septa (which may be rare), branching with narrower hyphae which possess clamps. In other parts of the colony some wide hyphae with clampless septa may be present $Key A$s"Key A Species with clamps present but absent from the marginal hyphae."
1,2$b.  Clamps present of absent, but not absent only at the very margin     †  †
2,3$a.  Clamps present at all primary septa  3
2,6$b.  Clamps absent or not present at all primary septa 
3,4$a.  Branched or unbranched skeletal hyphae, cuticular cells or interlocking hyphae present  
3,5$b.  Branched and unbranched skeletal hyphae, cuticular cells and interlocking hyphae absent  
4,cs2$a.  Laccase present $Key B$s"Key B.  Clamps present at all septa. Skeletal and/or binding hyphae and/or cuticular cells and/or interlocking hyphae present. Laccase present.   "
4,cs3$b.  Laccase absent $Key C$s" Key C. Species with clamps at all septa. Skeletal and/or binding hyphae and/or cuticular cells, laccase absent"
5,cs4$a.  Laccase present  $Key D$s"Key D. Clamps present at all septa.Skeletal binding hyphae cuticular cells absent. Laccase present"
5,cs5$b.  Laccase absent  $Key E $s"Key E. Species with clamps ay all septa without skeletal hyphae, binding hyphae and cuiulr cells. Laccase absent."
6,cs6$a.  Multiple clamps present  $Key F $s"Key F. Species with multiple clamp connections"
