cs1,0$Key A Species with clamps present but absent from the marginal hyphae.

1,2$a. Laccase and peroxidase absent
1,8$b Laccase and peroxidase present

2,0$a. (1) Chlamydospores and arthroconidia absent. Aerial mycelium absent or very low, yellowish to pink, turning pale reddish in KOH. Hydnoid basidiocarps present within 6 weeks. $See Sarcodontia setosa under 14a.

2,3$b. Chlamydospores and/or arthroconidia present. Basidiocarps when present poroid. ///Growth rate typically 25-45 mm, growth on ChA often faster than on MEA. Advancing zone appressed to submerged, hyphae rather distant to distant. Odour sweet or absent. Aerial hyphae typically with clamps, but these may be lacking at some or all septa. (These species easily revert to the haploid condition). Skeletal hyphae may be present. Arthroconidia may originate from clamped and clampless hyphae. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, with thickened walls, most abundant in dikaryotic parts. Submerged hyphae with more septa lacking clamps.

3,4$a. (2) Colony becoming yellowish or orange-yellow, but never brown (except basidiocarp)
3,7$b. Colony becoming distinctly brown
4,5$a. (3) Arthroconidia absent
4,6$b Arthroconidia present

5,0$a. (4) Growth rate on ChA 20-30 mm. Skeletal hyphae straight, aseptate, unbranched, 2-3.5 µm, wall up to 0.7 µm thick. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, (13-)15-20(-30) x 7.5-12 µm, wall up to 2(-5) µm thick. Japan ///Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to thinly cottony, white to Straw Yellow or Amber Yellow, finally cottony, Light Orange Yellow to sometimes Apricot Yellow. Odour sweet. Reaction with KOH weak or absent. Marginal hyphae (2-)2.5-4.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 2.5 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, with granular contents, sometimes yellowish. Yellowish resinous material on the hyphae. Submerged hyphae up to 9 µm wide, often somewhat irregular, wider hyphae often without clamps. [p. 44]///Code: (4), (8), (9), 11, 13, (14), 17, 21, 30, 31, 35, 36, (39), 40, (42), 45, (46), 48, (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), 58, 83, 85, 89. $Tyromyces sambuceus (Lloyd) Imaz†

5,0$b. Growth rate on ChA 50-70 mm. Skeletal hyphae absent. Chlamydospores globose to ovoid, 7-15 µm diam or 9-14 x 7-11 µm, wall up to 1 µm thick. N.-America.///Mat appressed or minutely downy to sparsely floccose; around the inoculum velvety. Colony Buff Yellow to Apricot Yellow. Odour sweet. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5(-6) µm wide, somewhat irregular, often with terminal swellings (young chlamydospores?). Aerial hyphae with thin or slightly thickened walls, 2-4.5 µm wide.///Code: (6),(7), 11, 13, 14, (16), 17, (19), (26), 31, 35, 36, (39), 40, 45, (48), 52, 53, (54), (75), 80, 82, 83, 85. $Poria aurea Peck.†

6,0$a. (4) Skeletal hyphae present, hyaline to yellowish, 2-3.5 µm wide, sometimes rare. Basidiocarps formed within 6 weeks.///Growth rate 25-5 mm. Mat at first downy to low cottony or woolly, white to Warm Buff, Buff Yellow or Light Ochraceous Buff, becoming densely woolly or locally farinaceous-felty, Pale Orange Yellow. Basidiocarps with brown tinges. Reverse unchanged or slightly bleached, later sometimes darker. Odour variable. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-4.5 µm wide ; rarely narrow thick-walled hyphae present. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 10-22(-28) x 6-11 µm. North-temperate.///Ref.: Snell, 1922; Cartwright, 1931; Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1971; Bezemer, 1973.///Code: (2), (4), (7), (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, (18), 21, 22, (25), (30), 31, (32), 35, (36), (37), (38), (39), 40, 42, 45, 46, 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, (67), 83, 84, 85, 88, (89), (90), (93), (95), (96), 98, 100 $Gloeophyllum trabeum (Pers. ex Fr.) Murr. 
6,0,n$Syn.: Lenzites thermophila Falck†

6,0$b. Skeletal hyphae absent. No basidiocarps formed within 6 weeks. ///Growth rate 20-45 mm. Mat cottony to woolly, whitish to Cream Buff or Honey Yellow, sometimes with whitish farinaceous zones. Reverse unchanged. Odour sweet. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2.4-5 µm wide. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 9-22 x 6-11 µm. N.-America. [p. 45]///Ref.: Nobles, 1948.///Code: (4), (7), (8), (9), 11, (13), (14), (17), (18), 21, 22, 30, 31, 35, 36, (39), 40, (42), 45, (50), 52, 53, (75), 83, 84, 85, 90, (93), (95), (96), 98, 100. $Gloeophyllum protractum (Fr.) Imaz. 
6,0,n$Syn.: Trametes americana Overh†


7,0$a. (3) Mat at first appressed to downy or low woolly, white to Colonial Buff, Clay Color or Mustard Yellow, becoming densely cottony-woolly to felty, Ochraceous Tawny, Buckthorn Brown or Sayall Brown. Odour faint. (Sub)tropical. ///Growth rate 25-35 mm. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Aerial mycelium 1.2-2.5 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae hyaline to yellowish, 1-2.5 µm wide. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 9-16 x 6-8 µm. Arthroconidia absent.///Code: (4), (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), 17, (19), (21), 22, 25, 30, 31, 34, 35, (38), (39), 40, 42, (44), (45), 46, (48), 50, 51, 2, 53, 67, 83, 85, 89. $Gloeophyllum striatum (Sw. ex Fr.) Murr.

7,0$b.* Mat at first appressed to low woolly, white or cream to Colonial Buff or Honey Yellow, becoming densely woolly or cottony-woolly, locally Sudan Brown. Odour faint. Europe, Asia. ///Growth rate 30-45 mm. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-4 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae yellowish, 2-4 µm wide. Chlamydospores rare or absent, ellipsoid, 9-17 x 6-9 µm. Arthroconidia rare in diploid mycelium, abundant in haploid mycelium///Ref.: David, 1968///Code: 2, (4), (8), (9), 13, (14), (15), (17), (21), 22, 30, 31, 34, 35, (39), 40, 42, (44), (45), 46, (50), 52, 53, (83), 84, (85), 88, 90, (93), (95), (96), 98, 100. $Gloeophyllum abietinum (Bull. ex Fr.) P. Karst.

7,0$c.* Mat at first appressed to farinaceous or granulose, white to Warm Buff, becoming subfelty and Honey Yellow to Raw Sienna or Sudan Brown, sometimes locally woolly, Light Orange Yellow to Cadmium Yellow. Basidiocarps often formed within 6 weeks. Odour faint. North-temperate.///Growth rate 15-40 mm. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5(-5.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae hyaline-yellowish, (1-)2-4(-5) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, sometimes with short [p. 46] contorted branches. Often resinous material between hyphae. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 7-18 x 5-10 µm. Arthroconidia present.///Ref.: Snell, 1922; Robak, 1942; Nobles, 1948; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; David, 1968; van der Westhuizen, 1971.///Code: (2), (4), (7), (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), (15), (17), 18, (21), 22, 24, (25), (26), 30, 31, 34, 35, (37), (38), (39), 40, 42, (44), (45), (46), 48, 50, (51), 52, 53, (54), (61), 67, (75), 83, 84, 85, (89), (90), (93), (95), (96), 98, 100. $Gloeophyllum sepiarium (Wulf. ex Fr.) P. Karst.

7,0$d.* Mat at first appressed to farinaceous, whitish, locally becoming downy, thinly woolly or subfelty, Warm Buff to Honey Yellow, locally with orange or brown tinges. Basidiocarps may be formed within 6 weeks. Odour strong, sweet. Temperate. ///Growth rate 30-50 mm. Marginal hyphae (1-)2-4.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1-4 µm wide, hyaline to yellowish, thin- to thick-walled. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 10-18 x 6-10 µm. On Picea.///Ref.: Robak, 1942; David, 1968.///Code: 2, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, (17), 18, (21), 22, (24), (25), 30, 31, (32), (34), 35, 36, (38), (39), 40, 42, (44), (45), 48, 50, (51), 52, 53, (54), (67), 83, 84, 85, (88), 90, (93), (95), (96), 98, 100. $Gloeophyllum odoratum (Wulf. ex Fr.) Imaz.

8,9$a. (1) Blastoconidia present, formed on denticles or narrow stalks. ///Advancing zone appressed, rarely raised, even or slightly bayed, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed to downy, becoming low woolly or cottony-woolly and finally farinaceous to floccose, with yellowish, salmon or olivaceous tinges.
8,12$b. Blastoconidia absent
9,10$a. (8) On angiosperms
9,11$b. On gymnosperms

10,0$a. (9) Growth rate 1-5-40 mm. Colony becoming Deep Co lonial Buff or Chamois to Tawny Olive, turning purplish in KOH. Conidia globose to ovoid, 24(-5) µm diam. Chlamydospores absent. ///Basidiocarps often formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae 2-5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.54 µm wide. Conidia often abundant close to the advancing zone, [p. 47] but often rare or absent in older parts. Cosmopolitan Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Fallahyan, 1964; David, 1969a. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Fallahyan, 1964; David, 1969a.///Code: (1), 2, 3, 4, (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), 18, 22, (24), (25), (30), 31, 35, (39), 40, 42, (44), (45), (50), 52, 53, 86, 89, (95), (96), 97, 100. Code: (1), 2, 3, 4, (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), 18, 22, (24), (25), (30), 31, 35, (39), 40, 42, (44), (45), (50), 52, 53, 86, 89, (95), (96), 97, 100. $Hapalopilus rutilans (Pers. ex Fr.) P. Karst. 
10,0,n$Syn.: Polyporus nidulans Fr 

10,0$b. Growth rate 60->70 mm. Colony becoming Naphthalene Yellow, Straw Yellow or Martius Yellow to Sulphur Yellow or Citron Yellow, not reacting with KOH. Conidia globose to subglobose, (2-)3-6.5(-11) µm diam, thick-walled when mature. Chlamydospores often present, globose to subglobose, (6-)7-15(-20) µm diam ///Basidiocarps rarely formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae 2-7 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1-5 µm wide, with thin to thickened walls. Conidiogenous hyphae with remaining denticles often fragmenting into arthroconidium-like structures. Submerged hyphae sometimes thick-walled. North-temperate.///Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Fallahyan, 1964; David, 1969a; Siepmann, 1971. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Fallahyan, 1964; David, 1969a; Siepmann, 1971.///Code.: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (15), 17, 18, (19), (21), 22, 30, 31, 35, (39), 40, 44, 45, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), (78), (83), (84), 85, 86, (88), 89, (93), (95), (96), 97, 100. Code.: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (15), 17, 18, (19), (21), 22, 30, 31, 35, (39), 40, 44, 45, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), (78), (83), (84), 85, 86, (88), 89, (93), (95), (96), 97, 100. $Tyromyces fissilis (Berk. & Curt.) Donk 
10,0,n$Syn.: Polyporus albosordescens Rom./// Hapalopilus croceus (Pers ex Fr.) Donk may also show this type of conidia (3.57(10) µm diam) as mentioned by Fallahyan (1964). In CBS 315.29, however, they only appeared after 6 weeks, while Davidson et al. (1942) and David (1969) did not mention them at all.

11,0$a. (9) Colony Orange Buff to Deep Chrome becoming Salmon Buff, Apricot Buff or Salmon Color, turning purplish with KOH. Conidia globose to subglobose, 9-15 µm diam, thick-walled when mature. ///Growth rate 30-65 mm. Marginal hyphae 3-7 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, often covered with brownish material. Chlamydospores rare, 6-15 µm diam. Submerged hyphae 1.5-7 µm wide. North-temperate.///Ref.: David, 1969///Code: 1, 2, 3, 4, (7), (8), 11, 13, 14, 17, 21, (24), (25), (26), 30, 31, (32), 35, (39), 40, 42, 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, 58, (75), (80), (82), 83, 85, 86, (88), 90, (93), 95, 97, 100. [p. 48] $Hapalopilus salmonicolor (Berk. & Curt.) Pouzar 
11,0,n$Syn.: Polyporus aurantiacus Rostk.

11,0$b. Colony becoming Massicot Yellow. No reaction with KOH. Conidia globose to subglobose, (2-)3-7 µm diam, thick-walled when mature. ///Growth rate 60->70 mm. Marginal hyphae 2-9 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5(-7) µm wide, some with resinous contents. Submerged hyphae 2-7.5 µm wide. Chlamydospores 7-20 µm diam, sometimes rare. N.-America.///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, (19), 21, 22, 30, 31, 35, (39), 40, 42, 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, 55, (60), (61), (78), 83, 85, 86, 90. $Tyromyces amarus (Hedgec.) Lowe
12,13$a. (8) Chlamydospores absent
12,19$b. Chlamydospores present
13,14$a. (12) Odour strong, sweet
13,16$b. Odour faint or absent

14,0$a. (13) Large hyphal cells (often longer than 500 m) with resinous contents present, often seceding, sulpho-negative. Mat at first downy or farinaceous to thinly woolly, becoming floccose to (sub)felty, rarely cottony-woolly, white. Odour suggesting anisaldehyde. Marginal hyphae (3-)4-7(-9) µm wide. Basidiocarp not formed within 6 weeks. On gymnosperms. ///Growth rate 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to bayed, hyphae distant. Reverse bleached. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5(-5.5) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, sometimes encrusted. Needle-like crystals present. North-temperate.///Ref.: Nobles, 1948.///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (15), 17, (18), (21), 22, 24, (25), 30, 36, 37, (39), 40, 42, (44), 45, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), 60, (61), (73), (80), 82, 83, 90, (93). $Ischnoderma benzoinum (Wahl) P. Karst 
14,0,n$ : Nobles (1948) mentions the presence of chlamydospores in some strains. She also mentions gymnosperms and angiosperms as substrate for her isolates. All CBSisolates are from gymnosperms and not one has chlamydospores. Since I. benzoinum and I. resinosum are now considered distinct species and one of the distinguishing characters is the restriction to gymnosperms in I. benzoinum and [p. 49] angiosperms in I. resinosum (Jahn, 1973), it may be possible that the chlamydosporic strains represent I. resinosum. Otherwise, (85) should be added to the code of I. benzoinum.

14,15$b. Large cells with resinous material absent. Mat appressed, becoming thinly downy or farinaceous to thinly woolly, with yellowish tinges, turning reddish in KOH. Odour not suggesting benzaldehyde. Marginal hyphae up to 5(-6.5) µm wide. After 6 weeks hydnoid basidiocarps typically present. On angiosperms. ///Aerial hyphae often encrusted with yellowish crystals, which dissolve giving a reddish colour in KOH. Submerged hyphae often constricted at the septa. North-temperate.

15,0$a. (14) Growth rate 20-40 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Colony at first hyaline to cream, becoming Sulphur Yellow. Reverse unchanged, rarely bleached. Marginal hyphae 2.5-4(-6) µm wide. Aerial hyphae mostly 1.5-5(-6.5) µm wide, with thin to slightly thickened walls. Submerged mycelium sometimes with thickened walls. Mainly on Malus. North-temperate///Code: (1), (3), 4, (8), (9), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, (18), 22, (26), (30), 31, 35, 36, (37), (39), 40, 44, 45, (48), 50, 52, 53, (54), 57, (58), 82, 83, 88, 89. $Sarcodontia setosa (Pers.) Donk.

15,0$b. Growth rate 50->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to typically bayed, hyphae rather distant. Colony hyaline to cream, becoming Barythra Yellow to Pinard Yellow. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 2-5(-6.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-3(-5) µm wide with thin or firm, rarely thickened, walls. Submerged hyphae often with swellings up to 8(-11) µm wide. On various angiosperms. North-temperate///Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. $Mycoacia uda (Fr.) Donk.
16,0$a. (13) Growth rate not exceeding 8 mm on ChA; growth. on MEA distinctly slower or absent. $See Skeletocutis amorpha, Key B under 92h. †
16,17$b. Growth rate at least 40 mm on MEA and ChA. Advancing zone appressed or submerged, hyphae distant or rather distant.
17,0$a (16) Basidiocarps typically present within 6 weeks, hydnoid. ///Growth rate 40-65 mm. Advancing zone even to typically bayed. Mat at first appressed, locally becoming downy to thinly woolly, whitish to Straw Yellow, Amber Yellow or Colonial Buff. Reverse at first bleached, later often darker. Marginal hyphae 2-7(-8) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide, often encrusted. Submerged hyphae 2-7(-9) µm wide.///Ref.: Boidin, 1958a.///Code: 1, (2), 3, (7), (11), 13, (14), (15), (16), 17, (18), 22, (24), 30, (31), 35, (37), (38), (39), 40, 42, 44, 45, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (78), 82, 83, 88, 89. $Mycoacia aurea (Fr.) J. Erikss. & Ryv.
17,0,n$Syn.: Mycoacia stenodon (Pers.) Donk.

17,18$b. Basidiocarp when present even to warted. ///Growth rate 50->70 mm. Mat at first appressed to downy or thinly woolly to cottony-woolly, remaining so or becoming locally floccose or pellicular, rarely velvety, at first white, later often with yellowish, orange or brownish tinges
18,0$a. (17) Japan. Colony at first white to Straw Yellow or Mustard Yellow, becoming Raw Sienna to Sudan Brown, turning purplish in KOH. ///Reverse bleached, after 6 weeks locally darker. Marginal hyphae 2.5-8(-9) µm wide, sometimes with knob-like projections or short branches. Aerial hyphae 1.5-3(-3.5) µm wide, with thin to thickened walls, often covered with brownish crystals. Submerged hyphae 1.5-7(-8.5) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled (up to 3 m).///Code: 1, 3, 4, (6), (11), 13, 14, 17, (21), 22, 24, 25, 26, 30, 31, 34, 35, (37), (38), (39), 40, (42), 44, 45, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, 57, 82, 83. † $Cystidiophorus castaneus (Lloyd) Imaz.† 
18,0$b. North-temperate. Colony white to cream, locally sometimes becoming yellowish or pale orange, not reacting with KOH. ///Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal hyphae (2-)3-6(-7.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1.5-)2-5 µm wide, with thin to sometimes thickened walls, sometimes encrusted. Submerged hyphae with thin to thick walls (thick-walled hyphae occasionally lacking), up to 5(-6) µm wide, sometimes irregular. [p. 51]///Ref.: K¸hner and Yen, 1947; Boidin, 1958a; Lombard et al., 1975; Ginns, 1976.///Code: 1, (2), 3, (5), (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, (18), (19), (21), 22, (24), 30, 31, (34), (35), (37), (39), 40, 42, 44, 45, (48), 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), (80), 82, 83, (88), 89, (93), (95), (96), 97, 100. $Phlebia livida (Pers. ex Fr.) Bres./ Phlebia rufa M.P. Christ. 
18,0,n$  : Phlebia rufa sometimes forms gloeocystidia and/or arthroconidia. Thus (73) and (84) should be added to its code†
19,20$a. (12) Gloeocystidia or hyphae with staining resinous material (5-9 µm diam., often longer than 500 µm) present
19,22$b. Gloeocystidia absent
20,0$a. (19) Odour strong, suggesting anisaldehyde. Arthroconidia absent. Basidiocarp not formed within 6 weeks. $See Ischnoderma, under 13a .
20,21$b Odour absent or faint, not suggesting anisaldehyde. Arthroconidia present or not. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks
21,0$a. (20) Gloeocystidia clavate, 25-40(-60) x 4-7.5(-10) µm, containing oildrops or granular material, sometimes covered with resinous material. Plate covered in 2 weeks. Arthroconidia typically present and abundant, formed. by fragmenting of dendroid aerial hyphae. Basidiocarps even to warted.///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat appressed or downy to cottony, becoming densely cottony or floccose-cottony, whitish to cream, sometimes becoming Seashell Pink or Capucine Buff. Reverse unchanged or bleached. Marginal hyphae 2-7(-10) µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, some narrower ones with much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, often with resinous contents or oil drops, some encrusted. Chlamydospores ellipsoid to subglobose, 5-10 µm diam or 6-11 x 5-9 µm. Submerged hyphae often with irregular swellings. North-temperate, rarely subtropical, Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Ginns, 1976.///Code: 1, (2), 3, (6), (11), 13, (14), 17, (19), 21, (22), 30, 31, (33), (37), (39), 40, 42, 44, (45), (48), 50,52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (58), 60, (61), (72), (73), (75), (80), 82, 83, 84, 85, (88), 89, (93), (95), (96), 97. [p. 52] $Phlebia radiata Fr. 
21,0,n$Syn.: Phlebia aurantiaca (Sow.) P. Karst.†

21,0$b. Gloeocystidia very long, exceeding 60 µm. Growth rate 6-35 mm. Arthroconidia absent. Basidiocarps hydnoid to coralloid. $See Hericium, key D under 19a
22,23$a. (19) Colonies with yellowish, orange, pinkish, vinaceous. or reddish tinges, often turning reddish or purplish with KOH
22,30$b. Colonies white or pale cream, not reacting with KOH
23,0$a. (22) Arthroconidia present. $See Phlebia radiata under 21a
23,24$b Arthroconidia absent
24,25$a (23) Plate covered in 2 weeks. Colony never orange, red or vinaceous
24,26$b. Growth rate not exceeding 60 mm or colony reddish, orange or vinaceous
25,0$a. (24) Colony locally becoming Massicot Yellow to Barytra Yellow or Primuline Yellow, turning purplish in KOH. Chlamydospores globose, 6-10 µm diam ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat appressed to downy or thinly cottony, later low cottony-woolly to subfelty or velvety. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal hyphae 2-6(-8.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, often covered with crystalline material. Submerged hyphae up to 9 µm wide. North-America. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Lombard et al., 1975.///Code: 1, (2), 3, 4, (6), (11), 13, 14, 17, 21, (22), 24, (26), 30, (31), 35, (37), (39), 40, (42), 45, (48), 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (78), (80), 82, 83, 85, (88), 89, (93). $Phlebia chrysocreas (Berk. & Curt. apud Berk.) Burdsall. 
25,0,n$. Syn.: Corticium lividum auctt.   : According to Lombard et al. (1975) the species is tetrapolar. If this is correct, the species is unique in this respect (as far as known) in the astatocoenocytic group
25,0$b. Colony locally becoming Light Ochraceous Buff to Light Pinkish Cinnamon, turning red in KOH. Chlamydospores globose, (8-)10-18(-25) µm diam. [p. 53]///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed or downy to thinly cottony, rarely plumose, locally becoming subfelty. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)3-6(-10) µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1-)2-5 µm wide, often covered with crystals or resinous material. Submerged hyphae up to 10 µm wide. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Boidin, 1958a; Sen, 1973; Ginns, 1976.///Code: 1, (2), 3, 4, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (16), (17), 21, (23), (24), 30, (31), (33), (34), (35), 37,09), 40, 42, (44), 45, (48), (50), (51), 52, 53, 54, 55, 57, 58, 82, 83, 85, 89, (93), (95), (96), 97, (99), (100). $Merulius tremellosus Schrad. ex Fr. 
25,0,n$ : Sen (1973) describes a culture as Merulius tremellosus Ptychogaster with drab tinges and conidia (chlamydospores) in chains. This is probably Ptychogaster rubescens Boud. Its perfect state, Punctularia atropurpurascens (Berk. & Br.) Petch, is not related to Merulius†
26,27$a. (24) Australia, India (S.E.-Asia) .
26,29$b. North-temperate.
27,0$a. (26) Colony at first white, becoming Tilleul Buff, Vinaceous Drab, Drab Gray or Light Drab. Reverse brownish. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 5.5-12 x 2.5-7 µm. Thick-walled skeletal hyphae present. ///Growth rate 60->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed or slightly raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat thinly cottony to cottony-floccose, becoming subfelty to felty. Marginal hyphae 1.2-4 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.2-4 µm wide. Branched and unbranched skeletal hyphae 0.5-4 µm wide. On angiosperms. Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1969.///Code: 1, (6), (7), (12), (13), (14), (17), 19, 21, 24, 25, 30, (31), 33, 38, (39), (40), (44), 45, (46), (47), (48), 51, 52, 53, 85, 89. $Polyporus cervinogilvus Jungh

27,28$b. Colony at first white, becoming pink or orange. Reverse bleached or orange. Chlamydospores broadly ellipsoid to globose, in average exceeding 7 µm diam with thin to thickened walls. Skeletal hyphae absent (but some thick-walled hyphae may be taken for skeletals).

28,0$a. (27) Colony finally becoming Cadmium Orange to Orange, turning red in KOH. Tyrosinase absent. Marginal [p. 54] hyphae (3-)4-8 µm wide, with narrow short and much-branched (somewhat dendritically) lateral branches, 1-2 µm wide. ///Growth rate 30-45 mm (on ChA 50-60 mm). Hyphae of advancing zone dense. Mat at first appressed or with scattered dots of woolly mycelium, becoming felty to velvety. Reverse orange. Odour sometimes sweet. Aerial hyphae hyaline to slightly yellowish, thin- to thick-walled, 1-2.5(-3.5) µm wide. Chlamydospores globose-ellipsoid, (7-)10-15 µm diam or 12-15 x 8.5-11 µm.///Code: 1, 3, 4, (7), (8), 11, 13, (17), 19, 22, 25, (26), 30, 32, (35), (36), (38), (39), 40, 42, 45, (47), 48, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 61, (62), (67), 85. $Mycoleptodonoides aitchisonii (Berk.) Maas G.†

28,0$b. Colony after 6 weeks white to Orient Pink or Orange Pink, not reacting with KOH. Tyrosinase present. Marginal hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide, no dendritically branched lateral hyphae. ///Growth rate 30-45 mm (on ChA 55-65 mm). Hyphae of advancing zone dense. Mat at first appressed or low woolly or floccose, rarely becoming subfelty. Reverse bleached. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, rarely thickened, interwoven with narrow thin-walled hyphae, 0.5-1.3 µm, without clamps (and septa?). Swellings present, up to 6 µm diam. Chlamydospores globose, 8-14 µm diam or 8-14 x 7-10 µm. On angiosperms. Australia.///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (8), 11, 13, 19, (21), 22, (24), 30, (33), 37, (39), 40, 42, 44, (45), 48, (50), 51, 52, 53, 54, (78), 80, 83, 85, 89. $Polyporus pulcherrimus Rodway.†

29,0$a. (26) Hyphae not exceeding 4.5(-5) µm in width. Odour sometimes strong, suggesting carbid. ///Growth rate 25-45 mm, on ChA faster. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed to woolly, remaining so or becoming felty, white to Maize Yellow or Warm Buff, becoming Pale Yellow Orange to Pale Ochraceous Buff, turning red in KOH. Reverse unchanged or darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5(-5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-3 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. Chlamydospores globose to broadly ellipsoid, (5-)9-15 µm diam or 10-21 x 9-15 µm. On angiosperms. Temperate. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948. [p. 55]///Code: 1, 3, 4, (7), (8), 11, 13, 14, 22, (25), 30, 31, (32), 35, 36, (38), (39), 40, 42, 45, (47), 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, (82), 83, 85, (88), 89. $Grifola frondosa (Dicks. ex Fr.) S. F. Gray.

29,0$b. Widest hyphae at least 6 µm wide. Odour absent. ///Growth rate 35-60 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed to thinly woolly or floccose, rarely cottony, remaining so or becoming floccose-felty to felty, Light Ochraceous Buff, Orange Buff, Light Orange Yellow, to Ochraceous Orange, turning red in KOH. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal hyphae 2-6(-8) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide, with thin to thickened, often encrusted walls. Chlamydospores globose to broadly ellipsoid, (5-)7-15 µm diam. Submerged hyphae (1.5-)2-7(-10) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. On angiosperms. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Fallahyan, 1964; David, 969a.///Code: 1, (2), 3, 4, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, (17), (19), (21), 22, (25), (30), 31, 32, (35), (37), (39), 40, (42), 44, (45), 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (80), 82, 83, 85, 89, (95), (96). $Hapalopilus croceus (Pers. ex Fr.) Donk. /Polyporus mutans Peck. 

29,0,n$ : Polyporus mutans is only known from N.America and may have some narrow, firmwalled aerial hyphae (0.51.5 µm); add (51) to its Code), while the distribution of Hapalopilus croceus is northtemperate and such narrow hyphae are absent from that species

30,31$a. (22) On gymnosperms. ///Plate covered in 1-2 weeks. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant to rather distant. Mat at first appressed, later sparsely downy or locally farinaceous to low woolly, rarely floccose or cottony-woolly, white. Reverse unchanged or bleached. Aerial hyphae sometimes encrusted. Chlamydospores globose.

30,32$b. On angiosperms (see also Poria lindbladii under 31c, and P. rivulosa under 31d). ///Growth rate 40->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to bayed, hyphae rather distant to distant. Colony white. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5(-5) µm wide (in Poria alachuana up to 8 µm wide), sometimes encrusted. Chlamydospores globose to broadly ellipsoid.

31,0$a. (30) Basidiocarps when formed phlebioid, olivaceous, brownish when old. Marginal hyphae (1.2-)1.5-7 µm wide, sometimes with narrow much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide. Submerged hyphae 2-5(-9) µm wide, thin- to, thick-walled (walls up to 2 m). Chlamydospores 10-28 µm diam, becoming thick-walled. Arthroconidia absent. N.-America.///Code: (1), 3, (6), (11), 13, (14), (16), (19), (22), 30, 37, (39), 40, 42, (44), 45, 48, 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (61), (78), 82, 83, 85, (88), 90. $Peniophora phlebioides H. S. Jacks. & Dearden.

31,0$b.* Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks, but the colony may become locally covered with granules of 0.5-1 mm diam. Marginal hyphae 3-6.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Submerged hyphae 1.5-5(-8) µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Chlamydospores 8-14 µm diam. Arthroconidia absent. North-temperate///Code: 1, (3), 5, (11), 13, 14, (16), (17), (18), 19, (21),. (22), 30, (39), 40, 42, (44), 45, (48), (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (66), 82, 83, 85, 90. $Irpicodon pendulus (Alb. & Schw. ex Fr.) Pouzar.†
 

31,0$c.* Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae (1.2-)2-6(-10) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. Chlamydospores 7-16(-20) µm diam, thick-walled. Arthroconidia often present (but these may become more or less globose and somewhat thick-walled, thus indistinguishable from chlamydospores). Submerged hyphae 2-6 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, walls partly soluble in KOH. North-temperate Ref.: Nobles, 1948.///Code: 1, (2), 3, (5), (6), (7), 11, 13, 14, (16), 17, (18), 21, 22, (24), 30, (39), 40, 42, 45, 48, 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (78), (80), 82, 83, (84), 85, (89), 90, (93). $Poria lindbladii (Berk. & Br. ex Berk.) Cooke
31,0,n$Syn.: Poria cinerascens (Bres.) Sacc

31,0$d.* Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae 2-5.5(-7.5) µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. Chlamydospores (5-)8-15 µm diam. Arthroconidia may be present. Submerged hyphae thin- to rarely thick-walled, 2-5.5 µm wide. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1948.///Code: 1, (2), 3, (5), (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, 18, 21, 22, (24), 30, (37), (39), 40, 42, 45, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, (78), (80), 82, 83, (84), 85, (89), (90), (93). [p. 57] $Poria rivulosa (Berk. & Curt.) Cooke 
31,0,n$Syn.: Poria albipellucida Baxter.

31,0$e.* Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae 2-7 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 2-4 µm wide. Chlamydospores globose to broadly ellipsoid, 8-18 x 7-14 µm. Arthroconidia absent. North-temperate Ref.: Domanski, 1969c.///Code: 1, 2, 3, (5), (6), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, 18, 21, (22), 30, (37), (39), 40, (42), 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, 57, (78), (80), 82, 83, 85, 90, (93). $Fibuloporia subvermispora (Pil·t) Doman.

32,33$a. (30) Mat appressed, becoming locally downy to farinaceous or granulose, never cottony, cottony-woolly or (sub)felty
32,34$b. Mat becoming cottony, cottony-woolly or floccosewoolly, sometimes (sub)felty
33,0$a. (32) Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks, hydnoid. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5(-6) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Chlamydospores (6.5-)7.5-14(-20) µm diam, becoming thick-walled. Arthroconidia sometimes present, often becoming chlamydospore-like. North-temperate.///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (15), (16), 17, (18), 30, (37), (39), 40, 42, 44, (45), 50, 52, 53, (54), 57, (80), 82, 83, (84), 85, 88, 89. $Mycoacia fuscoatra (Fr.) Donk.

33,0$b.* Basidiocarps rarely formed within 6 weeks, effused. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1.2-)1.5-4 µm wide, sometimes with swellings. Chlamydospores 5.5-8.5 µm diam, with thin to thickened walls. Arthroconidia absent. Europe///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), 13, (14), (15), (16), 17, (18), 30, 36, (39), 40, (42), 44, (45), (50), (51), 52, 53, 80, 83, 85, (88), 89, (95), (96), 97. $Phlebia subserialis (Bourd. & Galz.) Donk.†

33,0$c.* Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae 3-7 µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1.5-)2-4.5 µm wide, walls thin to slightly thickened. Submerged hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, often with swellings up to 18 µm diam. Chlamydospores (8-)10-15 x (6.5-)7.5-11 µm, becoming thick-walled. Arthroconidia absent. N.-America. [p. 58]///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), 13, 14, (15), 18, 30, (37), (39), 40, 42, 44, (45), (48), 50, 52, 53, 54, 57, 80, 82, 83, 85, 89, (93). $Radulodon americanus Ryv.
33,0,n$ Syn.: Hydnum casearium auctt.

34,0$a. (32) Marginal hyphae 3.5-7.5(-8.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae (2-)3.5-8 µm wide, some encrusted with needlelike crystals. Chlamydospores rare, 7-14 µm diam. Arthroconidia absent. Submerged hyphae 2-8(-10) µm wide, sometimes ampullate at the septa.///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21, 24, (25), 30, (31), 37, (39), 40, (42), 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, 78, 82, 83, 85, 89. $Poria alachuana Murr.†

34,0$b.* Marginal hyphae 1.5-5(-6) µm wide, sometimes with densely branched narrow lateral branches. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide, rarely with resinous contents, sometimes with oil drops on the wall. Chlamydospores 7-14 µm diam. Arthroconidia present, sometimes rare. Submerged hyphae sometimes with swellings up to 15 µm diam. North-temperate. Ref.: David, 1969b.///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), (14), 21, 22, 30, (39), 40, 42, (44), (45), (50), 52, 53, (54), (58), (60), (61), (78), 80, (82), 83, 84, 85, (88), 89, (94), (95), (96), 97, 100. $Spongipellis delectans (Peck) Murr. 
34,0,n$Syn.: Spongipellis bredecelensis (Pil·t ex Pil·t) Bond†

34,0$c.* Marginal hyphae 2.5-6 µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1.2-)1.5-4(-6) µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, some with resinous contents. Chlamydospores 6-10 µm diam or 6-12 x 5-9 µm, thin- to thick-walled. Arthroconidia absent. Submerged hyphae 2-7 µm wide, some with resinous contents. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1948.///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (15), 17, 18, (19), (21), 22, 30, (37), (39), 40, 42, 45, 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, 57, 60, 82, 83, 85, (88), 89, (93). $Spongipellis galactinus (Berk.) Pat.†

34,0$d.* Marginal hyphae 2-5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide Much-branched, thick-walled hyphae sometimes present after 5 weeks. Chlamydospores 6-8 µm diam or 7-10 x 5-6.5 µm. Arthroconidia absent. Submerged hyphae without swellings. Ref.: David, 1969b. [p. 59]///Code: 1, (2), 3, (6), (7), 13, 14, (15), 21, 24, 30, (39), 40, (47), 50, 52, 53, (54), 85, 89, (93), (95), (96), 97, 100. $Spongipellis pachyodon (Pers.) Kotl. & Pouzar.†



cs2,0$Key B.  Clamps present at all septa. Skeletal and/or binding hyphae and/or cuticular cells and/or interlocking hyphae present. Laccase present.   
1,0$a. Oedocephaloid conidiophores present. Conidia ovoid to.ellipsoid to fusiform-ovoid, 5-9(-10) x 2.5-4 µm, wall becoming roughened. ///Growth rate 15-40 mm. Advancing zone raised, even to bayed, hyphae distant. Mat floccose to woolly, locally somewhat cottony or farinaceous, becoming felty, white to Light Ochraceous Buff, later Cinnamon Buff. Reverse darker. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5(-5) µm wide, some covered with yellowish resinous material. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide, sometimes terminally slightly widened and cystidium-like. Conidiogenous vesicle up to 10 µm wide, bearing up to 15 conidia en denticles which may become up to 7 µm long. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Maxwell, 1954; Lentz and McKay, 1960. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (9),.(11), 12, 14, (15), (18), 19, (21), 22, 25, 30, (31), (35), 38, 39, 42, 45, 46, (51), 52, 53, (60), 67, (72), (80), 83, 86, 87, 90, (93). $Laurilia sulcata (Burt) Pouzar. 
1,2$b. Oedocephaloid conidiophores and roughened blastoconidia absent.

2,3$a. (1) Cystidia or gloeocystidia present.
2,12$b. Cystidia and gloeocystidia absent

3,4$a. (3) Gloeocystidia present. Cystidia absent. Skeletal hyphae much-branched
3,5$b. Gloeocystidia absent. Cystidia present. Skeletal hyphae not or rarely branched.

4,0$a. (3) Terminal, rarely intercalary, vesicles present, sometimes in clusters, empty, thin-walled, up to 12(-15) µm wide. Odour typically suggesting coconut. Tyrosinase present. [p. 60]///Growth rate 3-9 mm. Advancing zone raised, even, rarely bayed, hyphae dense. Mat at first downy to low cottony, becoming somewhat silky, sometimes plumose, white to cream. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1-3.5(-4) µm wide, aerial hyphae sometimes firm-walled. Gloeocystidia somewhat irregularly cylindrical, often curved, 40-90 x 3,5-7 µm, containing yellowish resinous material. Skeletal hyphae 1-2 µm wide, much-branched, sometimes with swellings. On angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1941; Nobles, 1948; Boidin, 1958a, ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (10), (11), 12, (15), (17), (18), 20, 21, (23), (26), 30, 31, 36, 39, 42, 45, 47, 51, 52, 53, 73, 75, 80, 83, (88), 89, 95. $Cystostereum murrayi (Berk. & Curt.) Pouzar  
4,0$b. Vesicles absent. Odour absent or not suggesting coconut. Tyrosinase absent. Vararia, $See key D under 24a 

5,6$a. (3) Cystidia with a more or less swollen upper part, the apex covered with a cap of crystals (or one flat crystal), thin-walled
5,9$b. Cystidia not distinctly capitate, becoming very thick-walled, often originating from skeletal hyphae; usually at least the upper half covered with crystals when encrusted

6,0$a. (5) Cystidia up to 70 µm long, capitate, apically up to 9(-12) µm wide, bearing one large flat crystal. Growth rate 14-20 mm. Cuticular cells or interlocking hyphae present, but rare ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Mat at first downy to cottony, becoming cottony to silky, rarely woolly, white to cream, locally Cinnamon Buff. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, sometimes inequivalently branched. Aerial hyphae 2-4 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, sometimes yellowish, some with swellings (up to 11 µm diam). Skeletal hyphae 2-4 µm wide, very rare or absent. On angiosperms. India. Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), 20, 21, (22), 30, 31, (34), 39, 42, 45, (46), (48), 50, 52, 53, 63, (64), (67), 72, (75), 80, (82), 83, (85), 89. $Trametes incerta (Currey) Cooke  
6,0,n$Chlamydospores as described by Bakshi et al. (1970) were not found; only swellings were noted. [p. 61] 
6,7$b. Cystidia shorter, rarely reaching 5 µm in largest diam, covered with a number of crystals, giving it a spiny appearance. Growth rate typically exceeding 20 mm. Cuticular cells and interlocking hyphae absent. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first downy or immediately cottony-floccose to woolly, rarely locally somewhat farinaceous, white to cream. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-5(-6.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae with thin to thickened walls. (Hirschioporus)

7,0$a. (6) On angiosperms. ///Growth rate 25-40 mm. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948; Macrae, 1967; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (12), (13), (14), (17), 19, 21, 22, (24), 30, 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, 52, 53, 54, 72, (81), (82), 83, (88), 89, (90), (94). $Hirschioporus pargamenus (Fr.) Bond. & Sing. 
 7,0,n$Two (sub)tropical species, Hirschioporus versatilis (Berk.) Imaz. and Polyporus elongatus Berk., also key out here. Bakshi et al. (1969) mention some differences: H. versatilis has distinct hyphal swellings (code 80) and hyphal strands (81), while H. pargamenus and P. elongatus have not, and the optimum temperature for growth of P. elongatus is 27∞C, while that of H. pargamenus and H. versatilis is 36∞C. 
7,8$b. On gymnosperms

8,0$a. (7) Skeletal hyphae abundant, 2-4 µm wide. Mat not translucent, sometimes becoming (sub)felty. Hyphal strands absent. Growth rate 25-40 mm. Ref.: Macrae, 1967. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), (17), (21), 22, (25), 30, (31), 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 48, 52, 53, 54, 72, (82), 83, 90,94). $Hirschioporus laricinus (P. Karst.) Teramoto 
8,0$b. Skeletal hyphae scarce, 2-4.5 µm wide, or absent, but thick-walled generative hyphae up to 6 µm wide, sometimes abundant; these may be encrusted with small crystals. Mat typically translucent, locally downy to farinaceous, never felty. Hyphal strands often present. Growth rate 35-65 mm. Ref.: Robak, 1942; Nobles, 1948; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Macrae, 1967; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, 17, (18), 19, (21), 22, 30, 39, 42, 44, (45), (46), 48, 52, 53, 54, 57, 72, (81), 82, 83, (89), 90, (94). [p. 62] $Hirschioporus abietinus (Pers. ex Fr.) Donk   Hirschioporus fuscoviolaceus (Ehrenb. ex Fr.) Donk     

9,10$a. (5) Chlamydospores present. On gymnosperms
9,11$b. Chlamydospores absent. On angiosperms

10,0$a. (9) Colony white to Light Ochraceous Buff. Mainly on Abies and Tsuga. ///Growth rate 7-15 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae dense. Mat at first downy to cottony, becoming cottony-woolly to felty, locally farinaceous. Reverse darker. Odour indistinct or suggesting almonds. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4(-4.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae with thin to thickened walls, some with swellings up to 8 µm diam, some with brownish contents. Skeletal hyphae hyaline to brownish, 3-4.5 µm wide. Some hyphae with lateral globose protuberances, Chlamydospores ellipsoid, brownish, 9-22 x 7-12 µm. Cystidia with several swellings, the upper part encrusted. Basidia and basidiospores scattered, not organized in a hymenium. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (9), (10), (11), (12), (13), (17), 18, 21, (22), (23), 25, 30, 31, (34), (36), 38, 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 48, 52, 53, (60), 67, (72), 80, (82), 83, 85, (88), 90. $Echinodontium tinctorium Ellis & Everh.  
10,0,n$E. tsugicola (P. Henn. & Shirai) Imaz. is indistinguishable in culture. 

10,0$b. Colony at first white to Light Buff, becoming Cinnamon Drab to Drab. Mainly on Taxodium.///Growth rate 10-25 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first downy to cottony-woolly or woolly, sometimes floccose, becoming felty or farinaceous-felty. Reverse darker, Marginal and aerial hyphae 1,5-4(-6) µm wide. Aerial hyphae with thin to thickened walls, hyaline to brownish. Skeletal hyphae 2.5-6 µm wide. Cystidia with one or several swellings. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 12-25 x 4-10 µm, with firm to thickened walls. N.-America. Ref.: Lentz and McKay, 1960. ///Code: (1), 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, 19, (21), 22, 25, 30, 31, 33, (34), 38, 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, (48), 52, 53, 54, (57), 67, 72, (80), (82), 83, 85, 90. [p. 63] $Echinodontium taxodii (Lentz & McKay) Gross 

11,0$a. (9) Arthroconidia present. Growth rate 70 mm ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first downy to farinacous, becoming subfelty. White spots of cottony or woolly mycelium develop locally, sometimes covering most of the plate. Basidiocarp initials sometimes formed. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-3.5(-4) µm wide, some much-branched. Skeletal hyphae 2-4 µm wide. Cystidia apically inflated. Some submerged hyphae irregular, up to 9 µm wide. Europe, Africa, Asia . Ref.: David, 1967. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, 21, 22, 24, 30, 39, (42), (44), 45, 46, (51), 52, 53, (54), (55), 72, (80), (83), 84, 89, (94). $Lenzites warnieri Dur. & Mont. apud Mont.  / Syn .: †Lenzites reichardtii S. Schulz. apud Th¸m.   
11,0$b. Arthroconidia absent. Growth rate 20-40 mm, on ChA often faster. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat hyaline in most places, locally pellicular to felty or velvety, white to Light Buff. After 3-5 weeks basidiocarps with salmon pores develop. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, sometimes inequivalently branched. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide, sometimes encrusted. Cystidia sometimes present, thick-walled, apically somewhat swollen and with thinner wall, 4.5-8 µm wide. Cosmopolitan. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), (15), (16), (17), (18), 24, 25, (26), 30, 31, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, (50), 52, 53, (54), 57, (72), (80), 82, 83, (88), 89, (90). $Junghuhnia nitida (Pers. ex Fr.) Ryv.  / Syn .: Poria eupora (P. Karst.) Cooke 

12,13$a. (2) Cuticular cells present, more or less globose, thin- to slightly thick-walled, typically hyaline. Short hyphae with many short terminal branches (staghorn hyphae) often present, especially in a zone 1-2 cm behind the margin. Mat remaining very low, at first typically downy to low cottony, becoming felty to farinaceous-felty. Skeletal hyphae much-branched (except Fomes fomentarius) [p. 64]
12,22$b. Globose thin-walled hyaline cuticular cells absent and not with this combination of the other characters

13,14$a. (12) Chlamydospores or arthroconidia present
13,18$b. Chlamydospores and arthroconidia absent

14,0$a. (13) Arthroconidia present, 2.5-4.5 µm diam. Chlamydospores absent. Growth rate 40-65 mm. Colony white and remaining so or becoming Buckthorn Brown to Cinnamon Brown. Reverse unchanged or darker. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae hyaline to yellowish, thin- to firm-walled. Skeletal hyphae hyaline to yellowish, 1-3 µm wide. On angiosperms. N.-America. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21, 24, 25, 30, (31), (34), (38), 39, 42, (44), 45, 47, (48), 51, 52, 53, (54), 63, (67), (80), (82), 83), 84, 89. $Globifomes graveolens (Schw.) Murr. 
14,15$b. Arthroconidia absent. Chlamydospores present

15,16$a. (14) Chlamydospores, at least some of them, roughened to warted or spiny
15,17$b. Chlamydospores smooth and remaining so

16,0$a. (15) Chlamydospores, especially in submerged mycelium, striate, thick-walled, wall appearing double, ellipsoid when young, 11-18 x 7-11 µm, (sub)globose when old, 10-15 µm diam. Chlamydospores in the aerial mycelium more often smooth and relatively thin-walled. ///Growth rate 40-55 mm. Colony at first white, becoming cream to Maize Yellow. Reverse bleached. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1.5-)2-6.5 µm wide, some aerial hyphae with thickened walls. Skeletal hyphae 1-2 µm wide. (Sub)tropical. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), 13, 14, (17), 18, 21, 25, 30, 31, 35, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, (63), (80), 82, 83,-85. $Ganoderma weberianum (Bres. & Henn.) Steyaert  / Syn .: Ganoderma rivulosum Pat.     
16,0$b. Chlamydospores smooth, roughened, blunt-warted or spiny, hyaline to brown, somewhat ellipsoid when young, 17-28 x 12-18 µm, (sub)globose when old, 15-30 µm diam, wall up to 5 µm thick, spines up to 6.5 x 2.5 µm. [p. 65]///Growth rate 5-15 mm. Colony white to Antimony Yellow. Reverse often darker. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, some aerial hyphae with thickened walls. Skeletal hyphae rare, 1.5-2.5 µm. On angiosperms. (Sub)tropical. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, 14, (17), 18, 25, 30, 31, 35, 39, 42, 44, 45, (47), 48, 52, 53, 63, (80), 83, 85, 89. $Ganoderma colossum (Fr.) C.F. Baker  
16,0,n$This fungus differs widely from the species described under that name by van der Westhuizen (1958). According to the description, the latter is probably not Ganoderma, as it has many clampless septa and lacks laccase. 

17,0$a. (15) Growth rate 35-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat remaining white, except for the basidiocarps which may develop vinaceous tinges. Reverse often bleached. Spores ovoid, 6-8.5 x 5-6.5 µm. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5(-6) µm wide. Staghorn hyphae abundant. Branched skeletal hyphae 0.5-1.5(-2) µm wide, unbranched skeletal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Chlamydospores subglobose, ovoid or ellipsoid, 10-19 x 7.5-13.5 µm, becoming very thick-walled. Mainly on Fraxinus and Juglans, but also other angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Baxter, 1924; Campbell, 1938; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), (20), 21, 25, (26), 30, (31), (37), 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 47, 51, 52, 53, (54), 61, (63), (80), 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $Haploporus cytisinus (Berk.) Doman. / Polyporus fraxineus (Bull.) ex Fr.   
17,0,n$According to Nobles (1948), the chlamydospores may become roughened.  

17,0$b.* Growth rate 45-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to slightly bayed, hyphae distant. Mat remaining white. Basidiocarp not formed within 6 weeks. Reverse typically bleached. Marginal hyphae 1-4.5(-7) µm wide. Staghorn hyphae rare or absent. Branched skeletal hyphae 1-2.5 µm wide, unbranched skeletal hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Chlamydospores subglobose to typically ellipsoid, 11-16(-20) x 7.5-11 µm or 8-13 µm diam, sometimes irregular, often very thick-walled. Mainly on Robinia, but also other angiosperms. N.-America ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), 13, 14, (15), (17), 18, (21), 25, 26, 30, (31), 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 47, (48), 51, 52, 53, (54), (61), 63, 82, 83, 85, 89. [p. 66] $Polyporus robiniophilus (Murr.) Lloyd 
17,0$c.* Growth rate 55->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Mat at first white, remaining so or more typically becoming Anilin Yellow, Massicot Yellow, Old Gold or Primuline Yellow, sometimes wrinkled. Reverse locally bleached, locally darker, often veined. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5(-10) µm wide. Staghorn hyphae abundant to absent. Branched skeletal hyphae 1-2.5(-3) µm wide. Some cuticular cells may become brownish and thick-walled (up to 3 µm thick). Chlamydospores ellipsoid, rarely subglobose, 11-18(-21) x 7-13 µm, typically with thickened walls, but in some strains becoming rather thick-walled (1 m). On angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, 18, 21, 25, 30, (31), (35), (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, (50), 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (61), 63, 82, 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $Ganoderma resinaceum Boud. apud Pat. / / Syn .:† Ganoderma sessile Murr.  
17,0,n$Nobles (1965) corrected the earlier identification of her isolates as Ganoderma lucidum (Curt. ex Fr.) P. Karst. (Nobles, 1948). The CBS collection contains five isolates of G. lucidum of widely different origin./// Reidentification is not possible because of the lack of voucher specimens. All isolates, however, possess chlamydospores (except CBS 251.61, which deviates also in other respects) and the above description fits them well. 

18,0$a. (13) Skeletal hyphae brown, not or only occasionally branched. Mat becoming distinctly brown, Cinnamon Brown to Sayal Brown. Tyrosinase present. Staghorn hyphae absent. $See Fomes fomentarius under 56b 
18,19$b. Skeletal hyphae hyaline, much-branched; unbranched skeletals rarely present, hyaline. Mat white or becoming yellowish, more rarely with olivaceous or vinaceous tinges. Tyrosinase absent. Staghorn hyphae often present. ///Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-5 (-6.5) µm wide. Branching sometimes inequivalent. Branched skeletal hyphae 1-2.5(-4) µm wide. (Ganoderma)

19,20$a. (18) On gymnosperms (See also Ganoderma applanatum, which rarely occurs on gymnosperms)
19,21$b. On angiosperms

20,0$a. (19) Colony white to cream, often becoming Maize Yellow to Old Gold. Western N.-America.///Reverse locally bleached or darker, sometimes veined. Some generative hyphae with thickened walls, sometimes irregular or with swellings, rarely yellowish, rarely fragmenting. Mainly on Tsuga and Abies. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, 24, 25, (26), 30, 31, 35, (36), (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, (46), 47, 48, (50), 51, 52, 53, (54), (61), 63, (67), 80, 83, 90. $Ganoderma oregonense Murr. 
20,0$b. Colony at first whitish, becoming Naples Yellow, Mustard Yellow, Primuline Yellow, Honey Yellow to Russet or even Buckthorn Brown. North-temperate. ///Growth rate 25-50 mm. Reverse locally bleached or darker, sometimes veined. Coloured parts sometimes crustose. Mainly on Tsuga and Larix. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), 18, (21), 24, 25, (26), 28, 30, 31, (34), 35, (37), 38), 39, 42, (44), 45, 47, (48), (51), 52, 53, (54), (61), 63, (67), (82), 83, 90. $Ganoderma valesiacum Boud. / Syn .:† Ganoderma tsugae Murr. 

21,0$a (19) Growth rate 40-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Colony pure white, sometimes becoming Pale Orange Yellow. Reverse bleached, sometimes locally darker, often veined. Marginal hyphae 1.5-6.5(-9) µm wide. Aerial hyphae sometimes with short projections, some encrusted. Europe ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, (19), 21, 24, (25), 30, (31), (35), 37, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), (59), (61), 63, 82, 83,, 89. $Ganoderma adspersum (S. Schulz.) Donk  / Syn .: Ganoderma europaeum Steyaert 
21,0$b.* Growth rate 30-60 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Colony pure white to cream, sometimes becoming Cream Buff to Olive Buff, rarely with vinaceous tinges. Reverse bleached, locally darker, sometimes wrinkled, but not veined. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5(-5) µm wide. Some aerial hyphae with thickened walls, sometimes pale yellowish, rarely encrusted. Cosmopolitan. [p. 68] Ref.: Campbell, 1938; Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, 17, 18, 21, 24, 25, 30, (31), (33), (35), 37, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, (48), (50), 51, 52, 53, (57), (61), 63, (80), (82), 83, 89, (90), (94). $Ganoderma applanatum (Pers. ex S. F. Gray) Pat.  
21,0,n$ Refshauge and Proctor (1935) mention chlamydospores. It is, however, questionable (also with respect to the colour) whether their fungus really was G. applanatum. 
21,0$c.* Growth rate 35-55 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Colony pure white to cream. Reverse bleached, locally slightly darker, somewhat wrinkled, not veined. Marginal hyphae 1,5-4(-6.5) µm wide. Mainly on Fagus, sometimes on Quercus. Europe. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, 18, (21), 24, 25, 30, (31), 7, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, (50), 51, 52, 53, (54), 61, 63, 82, 83, 89. $Ganoderma cupreolaccatum Kalchbr.  / Syn .:† Ganoderma pfeifferi Bres. apud Pat. 
21,0$d.* Growth rate 25-60 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Colony at first white, becoming Cream Buff to Strontian Yellow or Avellaneous. Mat becoming felty, sometimes furrowed. Reverse bleached to darker, sometimes veined. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5(-6) µm wide. N.-America. Ref.: Campbell, 1938; Davidson et al, 1942; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), 18, 21, 25, 30, 31, (33), 35, (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, (50), (51), 52, 53, (54), (61), 63, (67), 82, 83, 89. $Ganoderma lobatum (Schw.) Atk. 

22,23$a. (12) Vinaceous or drab tinges present
22,28$b. Colony never vinaceous or drab

23,24$a. Arthroconidia present (Polyporus)
23,25$b. Arhroconidia absent

24,0$a. (23) Branched skeletal hyphae absent. Arthroconidia abundant, up to 50 x 3-5(-6) µm, later often somewhat rounded, some with thickened walls. ///Growth rate 7-25 mm. Margin raised or appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first downy to cottony, often somewhat floccose or farinaceous, becoming pellicular [p. 69] to felty, white to Cinnamon Buff, Snuff Brown, Drab or Sepia. Reverse unchanged to slightly darker. Marginal hyphae 1.6-5(-6) µm wide, some much-branched. Some aerial hyphae becoming thick-walled and pale brownish, often with clampless (besides clamped) septa. Swellings often present. Pellicular area consists of thick-walled irregular hyphae with many protuberances. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), (10), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), (18), (19), 21, (22), 24, 25, 30, (31), 33, (34), (38), 39, 42, 44, 48, 50, 52, 53, (54), 61, 67, 80, 83, 84, (88), 89, (94). $Polyporus squamosus Huds. ex Fr. 
24,0$b. Branched skeletal hyphae abundant. Arthroconidia often rare, not with thickened walls. $See Polyporus tuberaster under 26a

25,0$a. (23) Branched skeletal hyphae absent. ///Growth rate 55->70 mm. Advancing zone ppressed to raised, hyphae distant. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, becoming felty. Colony at first white, becoming Drab Gray, Avellaneous or Chaetuna Drab. Reverse bleached, sometimes locally darker. Marginal hyphae 2-5(-6) µm wide, some with many short laterals. Aerial hyphae 2-6 µmwide, thin- to thick-walled. Skeletal hyphae 2-3 µm wide, not or rarely branched. On angiosperms. (Sub)tropical. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1958. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, 21, 22, (24), 25, 30, 31, 33, 37, (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 48, 52, 53, 54, 64, (67), (83), 89. $Amauroderma rude (Berk.) Torrend 
25,26$b. Branched skeletal hyphae abundant. ///Advancing zone raised, more rarely appressed, hyphae distant or rather distant. Mat at first downy to cottony or cottony-woolly, white, becoming (suddenly) pellicular and avellaneous to drab. Reverse often bleached after 3-4 weeks, becoming darker under coloured areas. Marginal hyphae generally (1.5-)2-4.5 µm wide. Branched skeletal hyphae (1-)1.5-2(-3) µm wide, unbranched skeletal hyphae sometimes present, 2-3 µm wide. Pellicular areas consisting of thick-walled irregular hyphae with many short interlocking projections, often pale yellowish or brownish. On angiosperms. Long clavate basidiocarp [p. 70] initials often formed within 6 weeks. (Polyporus s. str.)

26,0$a. (25) Growth rate 20-40 mm. ///Submerged mycelium sometimes with swellings. Basidiocarps develop in nature from a sclerotium, which is connected with angiosperms. Cosmopolitan Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, 21, 24, 25, 30, 31, 33, 34, (38), 39, 42, 4, 47, 52, 53, (54), 64, (67), (80), 83, (84), (89), (91), (94). $Polyporus tuberaster (Jacq.) ex Fr. 
26,27$b. Growth rate faster

27,0$a. (26) Chlamydospores present, but often very rare, subglobose to ellipsoid, 8-13.5 x (5-)6-9 µm. Colony becoming Vinaceous Cinnamon to Cinnamon Buff or Avellaneous, rarely Tawny Olive.///Growth rate 55->70 mm. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1968; Siepmann, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (17), 21, (22), 24, 25, 30, 31, 33, (34), (37), 39, 42, 44, (45), (46), 47, 52, 53, (64), (67), 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $Polyporus brumalis (Pers. ex Fr.) Fr. 
27,0$b.* Chlamydospores present, 9-13.5 x 6-7.5 µm or absent. Colony becoming Avellaneous to Light Cinnamon Drab or Vinaceous Drab. ///Growth rate 60->70 mm. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Refshauge and Proctor, 1935; Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1958; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (17), (19), 21, (22), 24, 25, 30, 31, 33, (34), (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), 47, (50), 52, 53, (54), 64, (67), 83, (85), (88), 89, (94). $Polyporus alveolarius (Bosc.) ex Fr.  / Syn .: Polyporus arcularius auctt. 
27,0$c.* Chlamydospores absent. Colony becoming Light Drab to Cinnamon Drab or Wood Brown.///Growth rate 50 - >70 mm. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Siepmann, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (17), 21, (22),.24, 25, 30, 31, 33, (34), 37, (38), 39, 42, 44, 47, 52, 53, 64, (67), (82), 83, (88), 89, (94). $Polyporus ciliatus (Fr.) ex Fr. 
27,0$d.* Chlamydospores absent. Colony becoming Avellaneous or Wood Brown to Verona Brown.[p. 71]///Growth rate 60->70 mm. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Siepmann, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (17), (19), 21, (22), 24, 25, 30, 31, 33, (34), 37, 39, 42, 44, (46), 47, (48), (50), 51, 52, 53, 64, 67, 83, (88), 89, (94). $Polyporus melanopus (Pers.) ex Fr.
27,0$e.* Chlamydospores absent. Colony becoming Cinnamon Buff to Cinnamon or Ochraceous Tawny to Clay Color ///Growth rate about 40 mm. Branched skeletal hyphae 0.5-1 µm wide, others branched or unbranched, 1-3 µm wide. Submerged hyphae up to 6 µm wide. Subtropical. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, (7), (8), (12), (13), (14), (17), 22, 24, 25, (28), 30, 31, (33), 34, (35), 37, (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 47, 51, 52, 53, 54, 64, (67), 91. $Polyporus sacer Afz. ex Fr. 
27,0,n$Polyporus grammocephalus Berk. as described by Bakshi et al. (1969) also keys out here. These authors call the skeletals unbranched or sparingly branched, but draw a muchbranched hypha. 

28,0$a. (22) Skeletal hyphae (branched or unbranched) absent, only interlocking hyphae present. Colony white to cream. ///Growth rate 35-55 mm. Mat lacunose, with felty spots and more or less concentric felty ridges, remaining parts thin cottony-woolly. $See Radulomyces, key D under 82a.  
28,0,n$Trametes lactinea (Berk) Pat. as described by Bakshi et al. (1970) also keys out here. It differs in the faster growth rate (plate covered in 2 weeks) and the more regular mat. 
28,29$b. Branched and/or unbranched skeletal hyphae present

29,30$a. (28) Crustose areas present
29,37$b. Crustose areas absent

30,0$a. (29) Agar with dark lines consisting of thick-walled, brown, interlocking hyphae. No crustose layer on the agar. $See Datronia mollis under 71c 
30,31$b. No dark lines in the agar. A hard crustose layer formed directly on the agar

31,0$a. (30) Thick-walled hyphae with minute projections present, others with small knobs bearing very narrow much-branched short hyphae or a crystalline construction. Skeletal hyphae irregular, locally swollen and much-branched, [p. 72] locally unbranched and with the lumen hardly visible.///Growth rate 20-40 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat appressed near the margin, becoming farinaceous, whitish, around inoculum felty and Cinnamon Buff. After 5 weeks low fascicles (± 0.1 mm) present, Antimony Yellow to Yellow Ocher, in between whitish basidiocarps. Locally crustose areas, often overgrown with aerial mycelium. Reverse becoming darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Thick-walled, brown interlocking hyphae and irregular cuticular cells (often with a clamp at the base) present. Submerged hyphae 1.5-7(-10) µm wide, some thick-walled. On angiosperms. Japan. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), 11, 13, 14, 18, (24), 25, 28, 30, 31, (34), (35), (38), 39, 45, 47, 48, (51), 52, 53, 54, (55), 59, 61, (63), 64, 67, 82, 83, (88), 89. $Trachyderma tsunodae (Yasuda) Imaz.
31,32$b. No hyphae with minute projections or very narrow, much-branched short hyphae present. Skeletal hyphae typically not swollen

32,33$a. (31) Much-branched narrow skeletal hyphae (firm- to thick-walled) present in the uppermost layer of the aerial mycelium, 0.8-1.5 µm wide
32,35$b. Much-branched skeletal hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide, not confined to the uppermost layer of the aerial mycelium

33,0$a. (32) Unbranched skeletal hyphae absent. Mat at first downy to farinaceous, becoming pellicular to felty, whitish, locally crustose, cream to Buckthorn Brown. Chlamydospores subglobose to ellipsoid, 8-15 x 6-11 µm, often rare. ///Growth rate 45-60 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3.5 µm wide, some with much-branched rather short lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae up to 4.5 µm wide, with thin to thickened walls. Interlocking hyphae hyaline- to brown, abundant, some structures can be interpreted as cuticular cells. On angiosperms. S.-America.. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, 18, 24, 25, 28, 30, 31, (34), (37), 39, 42, (44), 45, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, (61), (63), 64, 67, 82, 83, 85, 89. $Polyporus platensis Speg. 
33,34$b. Unbranched skeletal hyphae present. Mat at first cottony-woolly [p. 73] to floccose-woolly, becoming felty, white, but locally abruptly crustose and brown. Chlamydospores absent. ///Growth rate 40-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, rarely raised, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached, but often darker after 4-6 weeks. Interlocking hyphae abundant, thick-walled, brown, some structures can be regarded as cuticular cells. On angiosperms.

34,0$a. (33) Crustose areas Cinnamon to Snuff Brown, sometimes Ochraceous Brown to Hazel. Reaction on laccase weak. Marginal hyphae 1.2-2.5 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Skeletal hyphae 2.5-3.5 µm wide, hyaline to yellowish. Some aerial hyphae brown, thick-walled, sometimes in fascicles, some with- yellowish to brown resinous contents. Japan. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (12), (13), (14), 21, 22, 25, (26), 28, 30, 34, 37, (38), 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, (60), (63), 64, 67, (75), 83, 89. $Daedaleopsis nipponica Imaz. 
34,0$b.* Crustose areas Ochraceous Tawny, Cinnamon Brown, Natal Brown, Sayal Brown or Prouts Brown. Reaction on laccase weak. Marginal hyphae 2.5-4.5 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 3-5 µm wide. Thick-walled brown aerial hyphae present. (Sub)tropical Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, (7), 13, (14), (15), 17, 21, 22, 25, 28, 30, 31, 34, (35), 37, 38, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, (60), (63), 64, 67, 89. $Trametes acupunctata Berk. 
34,0$c.* Crustose areas Cinnamon to Cinnamon Brown. Reaction on laccase strong. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-3.5(-4) µm wide. Thick-walled brown aerial hyphae present. (Sub)tropical Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1970; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), 13, (14), (15), (17), (21), 22, 25, 28, (29), 30, 31, 34, 37, (38), 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, (60), (63), 64, 67, (88), 89. $Trametes scabrosa (Pers.) G.H. Cunn.  / Syn .:† Trametes corrugata (Pers.) Bres. 

35,0$a. (32) Branched skeletal hyphae absent. ///Growth rate 5-15 mm. Advancing zone even, appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first downy to farinaceous, becoming subfelty, white to Pinkish Buff or Cinnamon Buff. Reverse unchanged to darker. [p. 74] Marginal hyphae 1-2 µm wide, often somewhat irregular and much-branched. Aerial hyphae hyaline to brownish, 1.5-5 µm wide, firm- to thick-walled, some with swellings, some with irregular projections or cuticular cell-like structures. Skeletal hyphae 2-4 µmwide. On angiosperms. ///Code: 1, (3), (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, (21), 24, 30, 31, (34), (38), 39, 42, 45, 46), 48, 51, 52, 53, 64, 67, 80, 83, 89. $Lopharia cinerascens (Schw.) G.H. Cunn. 
35,36$b. Branched skeletal hyphae present, (1-)1.5-3 µm wide ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, even to bayed, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first downy to cottony-woolly, white to cream, rarely yellowish orange, becoming floccose-woolly or felty and sometimes brownish. Crustose areas becoming dark brown. Reverse unchanged or darker, rarely bleached when young. Brown thick-walled aerial hyphae with irregular projections and sometimes also cuticular cell-like structures present.

36,0$a. (35) (Sub)tropical. Unbranched skeletal hyphae abundant, yellowish to brownish, 2-4 µm wide.///On various angiosperms. Growth rate 30-50 mm. Colony at first white to Pale Yellow Orange or Pale Orange Yellow, becoming Ochraceous Tawny to Sayall Brown, Cinnamon Brown or Russet, rarely Argus Brown. Marginal hyphae 1.2-3(-4) µm wide. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (15), (17), 21, 22, 24, 25, (28), 30, 31, 34, 35, (37), (38), 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, 64, 67, 83, 89. $Fomes sclerodermeus (LÈv.) Cooke  / Syn .:† Fomes marmoratus (Berk. & Curt.) Cooke Cf. also Fomes fomentarius under 56b. 
36,0$b.* Cosmopolitan, but mainly temperate. Unbranched skeletal hyphae absent or very rare, 2-3.5 µm wide. ///On various angiosperms. Growth rate 25-50 mm. Crustose areas Hazel to Sayal Brown or Snuff Brown. Marginal hyphae (1-)1.5-3.5(-5)µm wide. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (7), (8), (9), (11), (13), (14), (15), (17), (18), 21, 22, 24, 25, (26), (27), 28, 30, 31, 34, (37), 38, 39, 42, 45, (46), 47, 48, (49), (51), 52, 53, (63), 64, 67, 83, 89. [p. 75] $Daedaleopsis confragosa (Bolt. ex Fr.) Schroet. 
36,0$c.* Asia, N.-America. Unbranched skeletal hyphae absent. Mainly on Alnus. /// Growth rate 20-50 mm. Crustose areas Sayal Brown to Bister. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-5.5(-6.5) µm wide. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), (15), (17), (18), 19, 21, 22, 25, (26), (27), 28, 30, 31, 34, 38, 39, 42, 45, 47, 48, (51), 52, 53, 54, 64, 67, 83, (88), 89. $Datronia scutellata (Schw.) Doman.    
36,0$d.* (Sub)tropical. Unbranched skeletal hyphae present, 2-3 µm wide. On various angiosperms.///Growth rate about 45-65 mm. Crustose areas Cinnamon Brown to Tawny to Mummy Brown. Marginal hyphae 2-4 µm wide. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, (7), 12, (14), 15, (17), 19, 21, 22, 24, 25, 28, 30, (31), 34, 38, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 48, 52, 53, (63), 64, 67, 89. $Hexagonia tenuis Hook. ex Fr.  
36,0,n$ Three species from (sub)tropical Asia described by Sen (1973) also key out here: Daedalea flavida LÈv. with pinkish crustose areas and swellings in the submerged hyphae, Daedalea brasiliensis Fr. (also known from America) and Hexagonia discopoda Pat. & Har. which is sometimes considered as a form of Hexagonia tenuis. The record of multiple clamps for D. brasiliensis is erroneous, as is clear from the drawing. 

37,38$a. (29) Much-branched and unbranched skeletal hyphae present
37,57$b. Only one type of skeletal hyphae present

38,39$a. (37) Chlamydospores present *erreur 
38,46$b. Chlamydospores absent

39,40$a. (38) Orange to red dots of aerial mycelium which develop into basidiocarps appearing within 4 weeks. ///Growth rate 35->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat at first downy to farinaceous or woolly-floccose becoming cottony-woolly, felty or granulose, aerial mycelium may be locally absent. Reverse unchanged to bleached, locally darker. Tyrosinase present. Unbranched skeletal hyphae 1-2.5(-3) µm wide. Aerial hyphae [p. 76] often covered with red crystals, soluble in KOH. Arthroconidia typically present, 1.5-3 µm wide. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, (5-)7-15(-20) x 4.5-10m. Terminally much-branched hyphae often present. On angiosperms. (Pycnoporus) 
39,41$b. Colony never orange to red

40,0$a. (39)Colony Light Ochraceous Salmon to Flesh Ocher, Flame Scarlet or Salmon Orange, rarely Carnelian Red. North-temperate, rarely subtropical. ///Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Nobles and Frew, 1962; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (4), (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, 24, 25, (29), 30, 32, (33), (37), (38), 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 51, 52, 53 (57), 61, (84), 85, 88, 89, (94). $Pycnoporus cinnabarinus (Jacq. ex Fr.) P. Karst. 
40,0$b.* Colony Pale Orange Yellow to Apricot Buff or Apricot Orange. Reverse often brownish. (Sub) tropical Ref.: Nobles and Frew, 1962. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (4), (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (15), 17, 19, 22, 24, (25), (29), 30, 32, 33, (37), (38), 39, 42, 5, 46, 47, 51, 52, 53, (54), (57), (61), (84), 85, 88, 89, (94). $Pycnoporus coccineus (Fr.) Bond. & Sing.  
40,0$c.* Colony Light Salmon Orange to Apricot Orange, Carnelian Red or Flame Scarlet, rarely Cinnamon Rufous. (Sub)tropical. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1958; Nobles and Frew, 1961; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (4), (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, (18), 19, 22, 24, (25), (29), 30, 32, 33, 37, (38), 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 51, 52, 53, (54), (57), (61), (84), 85, 88, 89, (94). $Pycnoporus sanguineus (L. ex Fr.) Murr. 

41,0$a. (39) Staghorn hyphae present. $See Haploporus cytisinus under 17a 
41,42$b. Staghorn hyphae absent. ///Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to farinaceous and immediately (sub)felty or downy to cottony-woolly or floccose, finally felty. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-4(-5) µm wide. Unbranched skeletal hyphae 2-4 µm wide; branched skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5 (-3) µm wide. Chlamydospores subglobose to ellipsoid.

42,43$a. (41) Mat remaining very low, at first downy to farinaceous, becoming felty or farinaceous-felty. Basidiocarps thin when formed, not on water-soaked elevations. Basidiospores ellipsoid to truncate-ovoid, with thickened walls (except Lenzites palisotii)
42,44$b. Mat becoming cottony-woolly or floccose, later felty. Basidiocarps, when present, on elevations which are often spongy. Basidiospores more or less cylindrical (Trametes)

43,0$a. (42) Growth rate 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to bayed, hyphae rather distant. Colony white to pale cream. Reverse often bleached. Odour distinct, sweet. Chlamydospores 5-12 x 3.5-10 µm. Arthroconidia sometimes present. Basidiospores cylindrical, 3.5-6.5 x 2-2.5 µm. (Sub)tropical Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1958. ///Code: 1, (6), (7), (8), 13, (14), (15), 17, 18, 24, 25, 30, 31, 36, 37, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, (48), (51), 52, 53, (80), (84), 85, (88), 89. $Lenzites palisotii (Fr.) Fr.  / Syn .:† Trametes elegans (Spreng. ex Fr.) Fr. 
43,0$b.* †Growth rate 30-50 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even, hyphae distant. Colony white, Reverse unchanged. Odour absent. Chlamydospores 5-10 x 4-7 µm. Arthroconidia abundant. Basidiospores ellipsoid, 4-5(-7) x 3-4 µm. N.-America. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, (17), 18, (19), (22), 24, (25), 30, 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 47, 52, 53, (57), (82), 83, 84, (85), (88), 89. $Perenniporia elongata (Overh.) Doman. 
43,0$c.* Growth rate 30-55 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Colony white. Reverse unchanged. Odour absent or sweet. Chlamydospores 9-18 x 5-9 µm. Arthroconidia absent. Basidiospores 6-7.5 x 4.5-6 µm. Mainly on Fraxinus. N.-America Ref.: Campbell, 1938; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, (21), 24, 25, (26), 30, (36), 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, (51), 52, 53, 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $Perenniporia fraxinophila (Peck) Ryv. 
43,0$d.* Growth rate 60->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Colony white, becoming greyish to isabelline; basidiocarp darker. Reverse darker. Odour absent. Unbranched skeletal hyphae dextrinoid. Chlamydospores 10.5-13 x 6-7.5 µm. Arthroconidia absent. [p. 78] Basidiospores 4.5-6 x 2.5-4.5 µm. (Sub)tropical Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1970; Wright, 1976. ///Code: 1, (6), (7), 13, (14), (17), 24, 25, 30, 31, 33, (34), 38, 39, (42), (44), 45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, 83, 85, (88), 89. $Loweporus lividus (Kalchbr.) J. E. Wright    

44,0$a. (42) Odour strong, suggesting anisaldehyde. Growth rate 50->70 mm. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Colony white to cream, later often with ochraceous tinges. Reverse bleached. Some hyphae from the advancing zone with many short lateral branches. Chlamydospores 7.5-16 x 4-6 µm, some thick-walled, some arthroconidium-like. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Hirt, 1932; Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1971 ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, 17, 18, (19), 21, 22, 25, (27), 30, 31, (35), 36, 37, 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 47, 51, 52, 53, 61, (80), 83, (84), 85, 89, (94). $Trametes suaveolens (Fr.) Fr. 
44,45$b. Odour absent or indistinct. Plate covered in 2 weeks

45,0$a. (44) Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat white, downy to cottony or farinaceous, becoming subfelty. Basidiocarps becoming greyish. Reverse bleached. Chlamydospores 6-12.5 x 3-8 µm. Arthroconidia often present. (Sub) tropical. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1958; Banerjee and Naha, 1960; Bakshi et al., 1970; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, (19), 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, (31), 37, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 51, 52, 53, (84), 85, (88), 89. $Trametes cingulata Berk. apud Hook. 
45,0$b.* Advancing zone raised, hyphae distant. Mat white, downy to cottony-woolly or farinaceous, becoming felty. Reverse bleached. Chlamydospores sometimes present, 6-14 x 4.5-9 µm. Arthroconidia absent. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (11), 12, 14, (17), (18), 21, 22, 25, 30, 37, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, (48), (51), 52, 53, (64), 83, (85), (88), 90, (94). $Trametes pubescens (Schum. ex Fr.) Pil·t 
45,0$c.* Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat white, downy to cottony-woolly or floccose, becoming [p. 79] (sub)felty and cream. Reverse bleached. Chlamydospores typically present, sometimes arthroconidium-like, 4.5-9 x 2.5-6 µm. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Bayliss, 1908; Jay, 1934; Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1969; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (11), 13, (14), 17, (18), 19, 21, 22, 24, 25, (28), 30, 31, (34), 37, (38), 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, (48), (51), 52, 53, (64), (67), (80), (84), (85), (88), 89, (94). $Trametes versicolor (L. ex Fr.) Pil·t 
 45,0,n$Isolates from (sub)tropical regions may develop a crustose, dark layer with interlocking hyphae. Chlamydospores then seem to be absent (Bakshi et al., 1969; van der Westhuizen, 1971). 
45,0$d.* Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Mat white, downy to farinaceous or cottony-woolly to floccose, becoming (sub )felty. Reverse bleached. Chlamydospores present. sometimes abundant, 6-14 x 4.5-9 µm. Arthroconidia absent. Mainly on Populus. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (11), (12), (13), 14, 17, (18), 19, (21), 22, 24, 25, 30, (31), 37, 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 47, 52, 53, (54), (61), 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $Trametes zonata (Nees ex Fr.) Pil·t 

46,47$a. (38) Colony remaining white to cream
46,55$b. Colony becoming yellowish to brown

47,48$a. (46) Growth rate not exceeding 35 mm
47,52$b. Growth rate faster

48,0$a. (47) Arthroconidia present. $See Perenniporia elongata under 43 b 
48,49$b. Arthroconidia absent

49,0$a. (48) Growth rate on ChA (28-35 mm) faster than on MEA (about 15 mm). Mat at first downy to low woolly, becoming densely woolly to cottony-woolly, white to cream. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae dense to rather distant. Basidiocarps often formed within 4 weeks. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-6 µm wide. Aerial hyphae mainly developed as much-branched skeletal hyphae, 0.8-1.5(-2) µm wide; some unbranched [p. 80] skeletal hyphae also present but rare, about 2 µm wide. Mainly on Quercus. North-temperate. Ref.: David, 1972. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), 11, 13, (14), 17, 21, 22, (23), 30, 31, 37, 39, 42, 45, (46), 47, 51, 2, 53, 54, 83, (88), 89. $Pachykytospora tuberculosa (DC. ex Fr.) Kotl. & Pouzar  / Syn .:† Trametes colliculosa (Pers.) Lund. & Nannf.; Trametes morganii Lloyd 
49,50$b. Growth rate similar on both MEA and ChA. Mat at first downy to farinaceous, becoming (sub)felty to farinaceous-felty, rarely locally low and woolly (Perenniporia)

50,0$a. (49) Growth rate 8-15 mm. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae dense to rather distant. Reverse unchanged. Basidiocarps formed within 5 weeks. Marginal hyphae 2-4 µm wide. Branched skeletal hyphae 1-2 µm wide; unbranched skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide. Submerged hyphae 1-3(-4) µm wide, much-branched, with granular contents. Some with thickened walls. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, 24, 25, 30, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, (48), 51, 52, 53, (82), 83, (88), 89. $Perenniporia ohiensis (Berk.) Ryv. 
50,51$b. Growth rate 20-40 mm

51,0$a. (50) On Shepherdia. N.-America. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae dense. Reverse unchanged, slightly bleached or slightly darker. Basidiocarps formed within 6 weeks, somewhat irpicoid and confluent. Marginal hyphae 1.2-3.5(-4.5) µm wide, branching sometimes inequivalent. Much-branched skeletal hyphae 1-2.5 µm wide; unbranched skeletal hyphae very rare or absent. Ref.: Baxter and Manis, 1939; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), 17, 18, (22), 24, 25, 30, (37), (38), 39, 42, 45, (46), 47, (50), 51, 52, 53, 83, (88), 89. $Fomes ellisianus Anderson 
51,0$b. On various angiosperms, mainly Quercus. North-temperate. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Reverse often bleached. Basidiocarp often formed within 6 weeks. [p. 81] Marginal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Much-branched skeletal hyphae 1-2(-2.5) µm wide, abundant; unbranched skeletal hyphae rare or absent. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (7), (8), (9), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, 24, 25, 30, (37), 39, 42, 45, (46), 47, 51, 52, 53, 83, (88), 89. $Perenniporia medullapanis (Jacq. ex Fr.) Donk 

52,0$a. (47) ëSkeletal hyphaeí with clamps. often at large distances (up to 800 µm). Mat at first cottony to floccose, becoming woolly and lacunose, not tough. $See Cerrena unicolor, key D under 96a.  
52,0,n$Nobles (1965) also observed clamp connections on ëskeletal hyphaeí of Lenzites betulina (
52,53$b. Skeletal hyphae without clamps. Mat becoming tough. (See also Trametes hirsutus, T. pubescens and T. versicolor under 45, Perenniporia elongata under 43b and a fastgrowing form of P. medullapanis under 51b)

53,0$a. (52) Mainly on gymnosperms, rarely angiosperms. Shape and diameter of clamps variable.///Growth rate 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat downy to low woolly or slightly floccose, soon becoming cottony-woolly to felty or pellicular, white. Basidiocarp sometimes formed. Marginal hyphae 2-6.5 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1-2 µm wide, some branched. Submerged hyphae up to 7 µm wide, sometimes ampullate at the septa, often inequivalently branched. North temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Domanski, 1964. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, 19, 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, (47), 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 78, (80), 83, (88), (89), 90. $Perenniporia subacida (Peck) Donk 
53,54$b. On angiosperms, some species very rarely on gymnosperms. Clamps not variable

54,0$a. (53) Arthroconidia present. Mat at first downy to low woolly, becoming felty or farinaceous felty, white to cream to Light Ochraceous Buff. Elevations and mycelial lumps present on which some basidiocarps may be formed within 6 weeks. According to van der Westhuizen (1971) the colony may become Maize Yellow, Naples Yellow or Mustard Yellow. [p. 82]///Growth rate 60->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5(-5) µm wide. Much-branched skeletal hyphae 0.8-1.5(-2) µm wide; unbranched skeletal hyphae hyaline to yellowish, (2-)3-5(6.5) µm wide. (Sub)tropical. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (17), 18, 22, 25, (26), (27), 30, 31, (35), 39, 42, 4, 45, 46, 47, 51, 52, 53, (54), (67), 83, 84, (88), 89, (94). $Coriolopsis polyzona (Pers.) Ryv.  / Syn .:† Polyporus occidentalis Klotzsch 
54,0$b.* Arthroconidia often present. Mat at first downy to low cottony-woolly, becoming felty-farinaceous, white. Balls of mycelium formed within 3 weeks, confluent, becoming farinaceous; after 6 weeks pores may be formed. ///Growth rate 55->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 1-4(-6) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-3.5 µm wide, typically not or rarely branched, but much-branched skeletal hyphae may be present. Thick-walled generative hyphae present. Submerged hyphae in some isolates up to 10 µm wide. Europe, Asia. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, 18, (19), 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, 37, 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, (47) 48, 51, 52, 53, (54), (55), (61), (82), 83, (84), (88), 89, (94). $Trametes gibbosa (Pers. ex Fr.) Fr. 
54,0$c.* Arthroconidia often present. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly or floccose, becoming (sub)felty, rarely farinaceous-felty, white to cream. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. ///Plate covered in 2 weeks. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide, some with narrow, much-branched lateral hyphae. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide, most of them branched. Thick-walled generative hyphae present. Chlamydospores absent, but some arthroconidia may become rounded and slightly thick-walled. Submerged hyphae up to 6.5 µm wide. North-temperate. Ref.: Bose, 1943; Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1958; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (11), (12), (13), (14), (17), (19), 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 47, 48, (50), 52, 53, 54, 61, (82), 83, (84), (88), 89, (94). [p. 83] $Trametes hirsuta (Wulf. ex Fr.) Pil·t 
54,0$d.* †Arthroconidia often present. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, becoming (sub)felty and sometimes farinaceous, white to cream. Later some elevations may be formed on which pores develop.///Plate covered in 1-2 weeks. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4(-7) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-5(-6) µm wide, most of them branched. Thick-walled generative hyphae rare. Submerged hyphae up to 6.5 µm wide. India, Australia. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1958, 1971; Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: 1, 3, (5), (6), (11), (12), (13), (14), 18, (19), 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 47, (48), 51, 52, 53, 54, (82), 83, (84), (88), 89, (94). $Trametes meyenii (Klotzsch) Lloyd 
54,0$e.* Arthroconidia absent. Mat at first downy or cottony-woolly to floccose, becoming felty to floccose-felty to lacunose, often with balls of mycelium, white to cream. Basidiocarps typically not formed.///Growth rate 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5(-6) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1-4 µm wide, some of the narrower ones much-branched. Thick-walled generative hyphae present. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), (17), 19, 21, 22, 25, (27), 30, 31, 37, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, (54), 83, (88), 89, (94). $Lenzites betulina (L. ex Fr.) Fr. 
54,0$f. * Arthroconidia absent. Mat at first cottony, rarely downy, becoming more densely cottony, while cottony mycelial balls are formed. Later the mat becomes appressed to subfelty, locally floccose to felty, somewhat lacunose. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. ///Growth rate 45-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed to, raised, hyphae distant. Reverse unchanged. Marginal hyphae 2-7 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide, some much-branched. Thick-walled generative hyphae present. (Sub)tropical. Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1969. [p. 84]///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, 17, (18), 19, 21, 24, 25, (27), (29), 30, 31, 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 47, 48, 52, 53, 54, (82), 83, 89. $Polyporus caperatus Berk. 
54,0$g.* Arthroconidia absent. Mat at first downy to granular and floccose, becoming felty to woolly-floccose, rarely cottony-woolly, white to pale cream. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. ///Growth rate 35-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae 2-5(-6) µm wide, often much-branched, branching sometimes inequivalent. Much-branched skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide. Unbranched skeletal hyphae 2-3.5(-4) µm wide, some with swellings up to 10 µm diam. Some generative hyphae with thickened walls. (Sub)tropical. ///Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1969. Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, 17, (18), 19, (21), 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, 37, 39, 42, 45, 46, 47, (48), (50), 52, 53, (54), (61), 80, 83, 89. $Polyporus leoninus Klotzsch  
54,0,n$The tropical Polyporus flabelliformis Klotzsch (=Microporus affinis Blume & Nees ex Fr.) O. Kuntze fide Ryvarden) and Polyporus xanthopus Fr. (= Microporus xanthopus (Fr.) O. Kuntze) as described by Bakshi et al. (1969) also key out here. Both have a growth rate of about 50 mm and a low (sub)felty pure white mat. Some (skeletal?) hyphae of P. flabelliformis have swellings up to 5.5 µm diam. /// Typical isolates of Polyporus conchifer (Schw.) Fr. may display unbranched and muchbranched skeletal hyphae. The species occurs in N.America, mainly on Ulmus. 

55,0$a (46) On gymnosperms, mainly Juniperus and Cupressus. Growth rate 6-15 mm on ChA, no or very little growth on MEA. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae dense. Mat at first downy to farinaceous, white, becoming rather abruptly Amber Yellow or Yellow Ocher, low cottony or woolly to velvety. Basidiocarp formed within 6 weeks (coarse pores or plates). Coloured areas turning red with KOH. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled, hyaline to brown, 2.4 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae unbranched or branched, 2-4 µm wide, quite often with secondary septa, sometimes with swellings. Clamps variable in shape. Ref.: Campbell, 1938. [p. 85]///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), (10), 11, (12), (13), 17, (18), (21), (22), 26, 30, (31), 35, 38, 39, 42, 44, 45, (46), (47), 48, 52, 53, 67, 80, 82, 83, 88, 90. $Pyrofomes demidoffii (LÈv.) Kotl. & Pouzar   / Syn .:† Polyporus juniperinus Schrenk
55,56$b. On angiosperms. Growth rate faster, similar on MEA and ChA

56,0$a. (55) Much-branched skeletal hyphae hyaline, 0.8-1.5 µm wide, abundant. ///Growth rate 35-60 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae distant or rather distant. Mat cottony to low woolly, sometimes floccose, becoming subfelty to felty, white to cream to locally Ochraceous Tawny or Buckthorn Brown. Reverse darker. Marginal hyphae (1-)1.5-5 µm wide, some hyphae much-branched. Clamps variable in diameter. Unbranched skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide. Generative aerial hyphae hyaline to brownish, thin- to thick-walled, sometimes walls irregularly thickened. Europe. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 12, (14), (19), 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, 34, (35), 38, 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 47, 48, (49), 51, 52, 53, (61), 67, 82, 83, 89. $Apoxona nitida (Dur. & Mont.) Donk  
56,0,n$Hexagonia hexagonioides (Speg.) Wright (CBS 464.63) is indistinguishable from Apoxona nitida in culture.  /// * When the colony is distinctly yellow and arthroconidia are present, cf. Coriolopsis polyzona under 54a 
56,0$b. Much-branched skeletal hyphae hyaline to brownish, 1.5-3 µm wide, often rare. ///Growth rate 40->70 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat downy to low cottony or low woolly, soon (sub)felty and often somewhat farinaceous, white to cream to Chamois or Sayal Brown. Reverse typically darker. Marginal hyphae (1-)1.5-3.5(-4.5) µm wide, some much-branched, some inequivalently branched. Unbranched skeletal hyphae 1.5-3(-4) µm wide. Cuticular cells may be present. Generative aerial hyphae hyaline to brownish, with thin to thickened walls. Temperate. Ref.: Hilborn, 1942; Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al.., 1970. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (4), (6), (7), (11), 12, (14), 17, 18, 21, 22, 24, 25, (26), 30, 31, 34, (35), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, (47), 48, 50, (51), 52, 53, (61), (63), 67, 83, 89, (94). $Fomes fomentarius (L. ex Fr.) Fr. 

57,58$a. (37) Chlamydospores present
57,65$b. Chlamydospores absent

58,59$a. (57) Chlamydospores abundant in the aerial mycelium, some parts consisting completely of chlamydospores. ///Growth rate 50->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant or distant. Mat at first downy or cottony-woolly to floccose, becoming felty or woolly-felty, somewhat tufted. Basidiocarps typically formed within 4 weeks. Marginal hyphae (1-)1.5-6 µm wide. Chlamydospores terminal, lateral or intercalary, with thickened walls, often stalked.
58,60$b. Chlamydospores in the aerial mycelium not abundant or absent

59,0$a. (58) Chlamydospores globose to subglobose, 6-14 µm diam. ///Colony white to cream, but basidiocarps pink, often with exudate drops (Japan Rose). Aerial hyphae 1-3 µm wide, with thin to thick walls often resembling skeletal hyphae. Some with distinct inflations intercalary or more often terminal, up to 12 µm diam; they are however not constant and sometimes only present after more than 6 weeks. Submerged hyphae up to 7.5 µmwide. On angiosperms, more rarely gymnosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1969 ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, 19, 21, 22, 25, 30, 31, (33), 39, 42, 44, 45, (46), 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, (80), 85, 88, (89), (90). $Abortiporus biennis (Bull. ex Fr.) Sing. 
59,0$b. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 8-14 x 6-9 µm. Aerial mycelium mainly consisting of thick-walled much-branched skeletal hyphae, 1-2(-3) µm wide. ///Colony white to cream, some felty parts yellowish; yellowish exudate often present. Inflations not seen. Submerged hyphae up to 6 µm wide. Mainly on Quercus but also on other angiosperms. N.-America. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 19, 21, (22), 24, 25, 30, 31, (35), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, 83, 85, 88, 89. $Polyporus compactus Overh.  
59,0,n$CBS 326.49, received as Polyporus colensoi Berk. from Australia, is indistinguishable from P. compactus.     

60,0$a. (58) Skeletal hyphae unbranched, 1.5-3 µm wide ///Growth rate 20-40 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, remaining so or locally becoming felty, white. Basidiocarps sometimes formed. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide, sometimes much-branched. Chlamydospores ellipsoid to subglobose, 6-15 x 6-10 µm. Submerged hyphae up to 6 µm wide. Mainly on Quercus, also on other angiosperms. N. America. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948; David, 1969b. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (11), 12, (14), 21, 22, (25), 30, (37), 39, 42, 45, 46, (48), 52, 53, (54), (61), 83, 85, (88), 89, (94), (95), (96), 97, 100. $Spongipellis unicolor (Schw.) Murr.  / Syn .:† Polyporus obtusus Berk. 
60,61$b. Skeletal hyphae much-branched

61,0$a †(60) Chlamydospores, especially in submerged mycelium, striate, thick-walled, wall apparently double, ellipsoid when young, 11-18 x 7-11 µm, (sub)globose when old, 10-15 µm diam. Chlamydospores in the aerial mycelium more often smooth and relatively thin-walled. $See Ganoderma weberianum under 16a 
61,62$b. Chlamydospores smooth

62,0$a. (61) Chlamydospores globose to broadly ellipsoid, (5-)9-15 µm diam or 10-21 x 9-15 µm. Tyrosinase absent. On angiosperms. $See Grifola frondosa, key A under 30a 
62,63$b. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, up to 8 µm wide. Tyrosinase present. On angiosperms or gymnosperms. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant, sometimes rather distant. Mat at first appressed, locally becoming farinaceous-downy, and later low cottony, cottony-woolly or felty to farinaceous-felty, often transparent in many places. Basidiocarps often formed. Marginal hyphae 2-5 µm wide. Much-branched skeletal hyphae 1-3 µm wide. Arthroconidia often present.

63,0$a. (62, 69) On angiosperms. Arthroconidia abundant. Chlamydospores 5-10 x 4-7 µm, sometimes rare. $See Perenniporia elongata under 43b [p. 88] 
63,64$b. On gymnosperms, exceptionally angiosperms (Dichomitus)

64,0$a. (63) Plate covered in 2 weeks. North-temperate. ///Colony white, basidiocarps somewhat yellowish. Chlamydospores, when present, 9-12 x 5-8 µm. Arthroconidia absent or rare. Submerged hyphae up to 7 µm wide. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (11), (13), 14, (17), 18, 19, 21, 22, 24, (25), 30, (31), 37, 39, 42, (44), 45, 47, 51, 52, 53, 54, 83, (84), (85), (88), (89), 90, (94). $Dichomitus squalens (P. Karst.) D. Reid  / Syn .:† Polyporus anceps Peck
64,0$b. Growth rate 30-55 mm. Europe. ///Colony white to cream, remaining so or becoming locally brownish; basidiocarp yellowish. Chlamydospores (5-)7-10 x (3-)4-5 µm. Arthroconidia sometimes present. Submerged hyphae locally irregular, up to 12 µm wide. Ref.: Domanski, 1966b. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), 18,(19), 21, 24, (25), 30, (31), (34), (35), (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (78), 80, 81, (84), (85), (88), (89), 90, (94). $Dichomitus albidofuscus (Doman.) Doman. 

65,66$a. (57) Skeletal hyphae much-branched
65,77$b. Skeletal hyphae not or rarely branched

66,67$a. (65) Colony remaining white or pale cream
66,72$b. Colony becoming yellowish, ochraceous or brownish

67,0$a. (66) Growth rate 7-15 mm. ///Advancing zone appressed, even to bayed, hyphae distant. Mat at first downy to somewhat farinaceous, soon low woolly or cottony-woolly to (sub)felty. Reverse unchanged or rarely bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3(-4.5) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1-2 µm wide. On angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, 14, (15), 17, (18), (21), 22, 24, (25), 30, (36), (37), 39, 42, (44), 45, 47, 51, 52, 53, 83, 89, (94). $Polyporus mori (Pollini) ex Fr.  / Syn .:† Polyporus alveolaris (DC. ex Fr.) Bond. & Sing. [p. 89]     
67,68$b. Growth rate faster

68,69$a. (67) Thick-walled generative hyphae absent
68,71$b. Thick-walled generative hyphae present

69,70$a. (68) Swellings at least present on submerged hyphae. On gymnosperms
69,63$b. Swellings absent. On angiosperms or gymnosperms. 
69,63,n$Polyporus consors (Berk.) Stevenson as described by Bakshi et al. (1969) also keys out here. It can be distinguished by a rapid growth rate (plate covered in 2 weeks), hosts (angiosperms), distribution (E.-Asia, Australia) and a thick cottony-woolly to felty mat.

70,0$a. (69) N.-America. Mat submerged to appressed, remaining so or becoming downy to farinaceous, white. Locally opaque granules are present in the agar. ///Growth rate 20-50 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Odour sometimes distinct, but undefinable. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae, when present, 1.5-2.5 µm wide. Swellings sometimes moniliform (but not separated by septa), up to 18 µm diam. Ref.: Zeller, 1915; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), (16), (17), (18), 30, (36), 39, 42, 44, 45, (47), 52, 53, (54), (79), 80, 90, (94). $Cryptoporus volvatus (Peck) Shear 
70,0$b. Europe. Mat at first submerged to appressed, becoming downy to cottony or floccose, later (sub)felty. $See Dichomitus albidofuscus under 64b 

71,0$a. (68) Arthroconidia typically present. Mat at first downy to low cottony-woolly, becoming felty-farinaceous. Balls of mycelium typically present. Basidiospores subcylindrical, 4-5 x 2-2.5 um. $See Trametes gibbosa under 54b 
71,0$b.* Arthroconidia absent. Mat appressed, locally downy to farinaceous or rarely low cottony and becoming subfelty, but never over the whole surface. Basidiocarps sometimes formed; spores ellipsoid, 2.5-3.5 x 1.5-2.5 µm. ///Growth rate 30-55 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant or rather distant. Marginal hyphae 1.5-6 µm wide, some terminally with many short side-branches. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled, 1.5-3 µm wide, septa sometimes very distant. Skeletal [p. 90] or skeletoid hyphae similar, without clamps, branched, but sometimes only slightly branched. Submerged hyphae up to 6.5 µm wide. Mainly on angiosperms, but also on gymnosperms. North-temperate. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), 18, (21) 24, 30, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, (46), (47), 48, 52, 53, 54, 61, (82), 83, (88), (89), (90). $Trametes semisupinus (Berk. & Curt.) Ryv. 
71,0$c.* Arthroconidia absent. Mat at first downy or low woolly, becoming woolly to floccose, rarely locally velvety or felty. Basidiocarps typically formed; spores cylindrical, 8-10 x 2.5-3.5 µm. ///Growth rate 45-60 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Reverse bleached to darker, sometimes with dark lines. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, some much-branched. Aerial mycelium consists mainly of much-branched skeletal hyphae, 1.5-2 µm wide. Brown lines (when present) consist of thick-walled, brown, interlocking hyphae. Submerged hyphae up to 6.5 µm wide. On angiosperms, very rarely gymnosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: David, 1967. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (11), 13, 14, 17, 19, 22, (25), (27), 30, (31), (37), (38), 39, 42, 45, 47, 48, 52, 53, 54, (61), (64), (67), 83, (88), 89, (90), (94). $Datronia mollis (Sommerf.) Donk 

72,0$a. (66) Growth rate up to 15 mm. On gymnosperms, mainly Juniperus and Cupressus. $See Pyrofomes demidoffii under 55a 
72,73$b. Growth rate faster

73,74$a. (72) Hyphal swellings present
73,76$b. Hyphal swellings absent

74,0$a. (73) Tyrosinase present. On gymnosperms. $See Dichomitus albidofuscus under 64b 
74,75$b. Tyrosinase absent. On angiosperms. ///Growth rate 45-->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached but locally darker, especially under the inoculum. Marginal hyphae 1.2-2.5 µm wide. Generative aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled, sometimes irregular with swellings up to 5(-12) µm wide. Some hyphae covered with yellowish crystals. Submerged hyphae often irregular with short [p. 91] lateral branches, knob-like protuberances and swellings, sometimes irregularly branched. (Funalia)

75,0$a. (74) Mat appressed at the margin, becoming downy farinaceous or short cottony and finally subfelty to felty or velvety, white to Pinkish Buff or Tawny Olive, sometimes more brownish. North-temperate Ref.: Domanski, 1968; David, 1967. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, 21, 24, (25), (26), 30, 31, (34), (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, 48, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), 80, 82, 83, (88), 89, (94), 95, 98, 100. $Funalia gallica (Fr.) Bond. & Sing.  / Syn .:† Trametes hispida Bagl. 
75,0$b. Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to low cottony-woolly, later felty, rarely plumose, white to cream to Pale Orange Yellow. North-temperate Ref.: David, 1967. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21, 22, (23), 24, 25, 30, 31, (35), (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, (48), 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), 80, 82, 83, (88), 89, (90), (94), 95, 98, 100. $Funalia trogii (Berk. apud Trog) Bond. & Sing. 

76,0$a. (73) Much-branched skeletal hyphae 1-1.5 µm wide. Colony white to Straw Yellow or Naples Yellow. ///Growth rate 45-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat thin cottony to cottony-woolly, rarely becoming subfelty. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-5 µm wide, some aerial hyphae with thickened walls. Submerged hyphae up to 6(-8) µm wide. North-temperate. Ref.: Cooley and Davidson, 1940; White, 1951; Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (7), (11), 13, 14, 21, (22), (24), 30, 31, 35, (36), 39, 42, 44, 45, 47, 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (82), 83, (89), (90), (94), 95. $Scytinostroma galactinum (Fr.) Donk  
76,0$b. Branched skeletal hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide. Colony becoming Chamois to Sayall Brown. $See Fomes fomentarius under 56b.  
76,0,n$Fomes tricolor (Murr.) Sacc. & Trotter as described by Bakshi et al. (1970) also keys out here. It occurs in S.E. Asia and seems to be restricted to Shorea robusta. 

77,78$a. (65) Mat becoming distinctly coloured [p. 92]
77,85$b. Mat remaining white to cream

78,79$a. (77) Growth rate not exceeding 20 mm
78,82$b. Growth rate at least 30 mm

79,80$a. (78) On gymnosperms. Growth rate on MEA distinctlyless than on ChA
79,81$b. On angiosperms. Growth rate on MEA equal or faster than on ChA

80,0$a. (79) Mat becoming Amber Yellow to Yellow Ocher. On Juniperus and Cupressus. $See Pyrofomes demidoffii under 55a 
80,0$b. Mat white, but sometimes with pinkish tinges. Mainly on Pinus. $See Skeletocutis amorpha under 92h 

81,0$a. (79) Colony becoming Pinkish Buff to Cinnamon Buff. Basidiocarps not formed. $See Lopharia cinerascens under 35a 
81,0$b. Colony becoming Cream Color to Napels Yellow. Within 6 weeks pubescent stalks are formed, 5-8 mm wide and up to 25 mm long. ///Growth rate 8-15 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat appressed to farinaceous, around the inoculum velvety. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide. Skeletal and aerial hyphae 1.5-2.5(-3) µm wide. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled. Tropical Africa and Asia. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), (10), 13, (14), 18, (25), 26, 30, 31, 35, 39, (44), 45, 46., 48, 52, 53, 83, (88), 89. $Cymatoderma elegans Jungh. 
81,82$/ Syn .:† Cladoderris spongiosa Fr.

82,83$a. (78) Colony becoming pinkish to vinaceous or drab. Hyphal strands may be present
82,84$b. Colony becoming brownish, never pinkish to vinaceous. Hyphal strands absent (Cf. also Fomes fomentarius under 56b)

83,0$a. (82) Advancing zone bayed, appressed to submerged. Mat mainly appressed, but with white plumose outgrowths from radiating hyphal strands. Later farinaceous to felty pustules are formed, at first white, becoming Light Grayish Vinaceous to Avellaneous. Growth rate: 30-50 mm. Marginal hyphae 2-3.5(-5) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae hyaline to [p. 93] brownish, 2.5-5 µm wide. Submerged hyphae up to 5.5 µm wide. On angiosperms, rarely gymnosperms. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, (7), (8), 13, (14), 15, (16), (18), (19), 23, (25), 30, 33, (37), 39, 42, 45, 46, (48), 52, 53, 54, 67, 81, (88), 89, (90), (94). $Polyporus vinosus Berk.    
83,0$b. Advancing zone appressed, even. Mat downy or low cottony to floccose-woolly, becoming felty, white to Bittersweet Pink, Flesh Color, Orient Pink or Light Vinaceous Cinnamon. ///Growth rate 60->70 mm. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae yellowish, 2.5-4.5 µm wide. On angiosperms. India, Sri Lanka. Ref.: Bakshi et al, 1969. ///Code: 1, (6), (7), 13, (14), (17), 19, (21), 22, 25, (27), 30, (31), 33, (37), 39, (44), (45), 46, 48, 52, 53, 67, 89. $Polyporus zeylanicus Berk. 

84,0$a. (82) Aerial mycelium with many terminal or lateral swellings (allocysts), apically up to 7-12 µm diam, typically not immediately separated by a septum, but stalked, from the closest septum to the top 18-35 µm long ///Growth rate 15-25 mm (on ChA often less). Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae dense to rather distant. Mat downy to low cottony, becoming cottony or locally farinaceous, white, sometimes with concentrical zones. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, with small clamps. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled, 2-3.5 µm wide, merging into skeletoid hyphae. Submerged hyphae 2-4(-7) µm wide, rarely with swellings. On angiosperms. Ref.: Domanski, 1969b. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (8), (9), (12), (13), (14), 17, (18), (19), 21, (22), 30, (31), (37), 39, 42, 44, (46), 48, (50), 52, 53, (54), 75, 80, 83, (88), 89, (94). $Schizopora paradoxa (Schrad. ex Fr.) Donk 
84,85$b. Allocysts absent

85,86$a. (77, 84) Growth rate at least 40 mm. Hyphal swellings absent
85,90$b. Growth rate not exceeding 40 mm. Hyphal swellings typically present

86,0$a. (85) ëSkeletal hyphaeí with clamps, often at great [p. 94] distances (up to 800 µm). Mat at first cottony to floccose, becoming woolly and lacunose, not tough. $See Cerrena unicolor, key D under 97a 
86,87$b. Skeletal hyphae without clamps. Mat becoming tough

87,0$a. (86) Basidiocarps formed within 4 weeks, bearing ochraceous to orange spines, which may be branched. $See Steccherinum ochraceum under 92a 
87,88$b. Basidiocarps when present not bearing spines

88,0$a. (87) Thick-walled generative hyphae present $See Trametes semisupinus under 71b 
88,89$b. Thick-walled generative hyphae absent

89,0$a. (88) Mainly on gymnosperms, rarely on angiosperms. $See Perenniporia subacida under 53a 
89,0$b. Mainly on Ulmus, rarely on other angiosperms. ///Growth rate 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant or rather distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming cottony-woolly to floccose or subfelty. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae (1-)1.5-6 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae sometimes branched, 1.5-2.5 µm wide. Submerged hyphae sometimes inequivalently branched, some hyphae with many short lateral branches. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 19, (21), 22, 24, 30, 37, 39, 42, 45, 46, 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, 83, 89, (94). $Polyporus conchifer (Schw.) Fr. 

90,0$a. (85) Mat farinaceous to velvety, erect aerial hyphae encrusted in the apical part (often the terminal cell); this part may be contorted, but more often is not. Growth rate 2-12 mm. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae dense. Mat rarely becoming felty, white. Reverse unchanged or bleached at first, sometimes becoming darker, yellowish to brownish. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3.5(-4.5) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae when present 1.5-2.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled, 1.5-4.5 µm wide, rarely wall thickening somewhat irregular. On angiosperms, rarely gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (10), (11), (12), (13), (17), 18, (24), (25), 26, 30, (37), (38), 39, 42, 45, (46), (48), 52, 53, 56, (57), 82, 83, 89, (90). [p. 95] $Incrustoporia nivea (Jungh.) Ryv.  / Syn .: I. semipileata (Peck) Donk     
90,91$b. Hyphae not encrusted in the apical part, if occasionally encrusted, then growth rate at least 25 mm. ///Mat at first appressed, later downy or farinaceous, remaining so or becoming felty to velvety, rarely somewhat cottony or floccose. Colony white to cream. Reverse unchanged or slightly bleached, sometimes slightly darker under inoculum. Marginal hyphae (1-)1.5-4(-5.5) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae generally 1.5-2.5(-3) µm wide. Thick-walled hyphae often present. Submerged hyphae typically with swellings .

91,0$a. (90) Some marginal hyphae with many short lateral much-branched hyphae. $See Trametes semisupinus under 71b 
91,92$b. Short lateral much-branched hyphae absent

92,0$a. (91) Growth rate 30->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Basidiocarps often present within 6 weeks, consisting of ochraceous to orange spines. Some aerial hyphae with brownish resinous contents. Swellings sometimes indistinct. On angiosperms and gymnosperms. Cosmopolitan Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 3, (6), (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), 18, (21), 22, 24, 30, 31, (32), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, (48), (51), 52, 53, 57, (80), 82, 83, (88), (89), (90), (94), 95, 97. $Steccherinum ochraceum (Pers. ex Fr.) S. F. Gray 
92,0$b* Growth rate 25-40 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Basidiocarps typically, not formed within 6 weeks. Some hyphae ampullate at the septa. Submerged hyphae up to 7.5(-8.5) µm wide. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (11), 13, (14), 18, 24, 30, (37), 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), 57, 78, 80, 82., 83, 90. $Junghuhnia collabens (Fr.) Ryv.  / Syn .:† Poria rixosa (P. Karst.). P. Karst. 
92,0$c.* Growth rate 20-40 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to slightly bayed, hyphae rather distant. Basidiocarps often formed within 6 weeks, consisting of ochraceous [p. 96] to salmon pores. Swellings sometimes absent. On angiosperms, rarely gymnosperms. Cosmopolitan ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), (15), (16), (17), (18), 24, 25, (26), 30, 31, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, (50), 52, 53, (54), 57, (72), (80), 82, 83, (88), 89, (90). $Junghuhnia nitida (Pers. ex Fr.) Ryv.  / Syn .:† Poria eupora (P. Karst.) Cooke 
92,0$d.* Growth rate 14-25 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Swellings abundant, up to 15 µm wide. On angiosperms. North-temperate. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, (19), (25), (26), 30, (37), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, 52, 53, 54, (78), 80, 83, 89. $Steccherinum laeticolor (Berk. & Curt.) Banker
92,0$e.* Growth rate 12-20 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to bayed, hyphae rather distant to distant. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Swellings often indistinct. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (11), 13, (14), (15), 17, 18, (25), 26, 30, (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, 52, 53, (54), 80, 83, 90. $Junghuhnia luteoalba (P. Karst.) Ryv. 
92,0$f.* Growth rate 8-15 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Swellings distinct, up to 8 µm wide. On angiosperms and gymnosperms. North-temperate ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, 14, 17, (18), (19), 21, 25, 30, (31), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, (48), 52, 53, 54, (60), (78), 80, (89), (90), 95, 97. $Steccherinum fimbriatum (Pers. ex Fr.) J. Erikss
92,0$g.* Growth rate 7-15 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae dense. Mat thinly cottony to somewhat silky, becoming densely cottony. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Submerged hyphae often constricted at the septa, with distinct swellings. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (40), 12, (20), 21, 30, (31), 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 48, (51), 52, 53, 78, 80, 82, 83, 90. $Fibricium rude (P. Karst.) J¸lich  / Syn .:† Fibricium greschikii (Bres.) J. Erikss. 
92,0$h.* Growth rate 2-8 mm, on MEA less than on ChA. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Basidiocarps sometimes formed within 6 weeks, salmon. Swellings [p. 97] distinct, up to 10 µm diam. On gymnosperms, mainly Pinus. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: 1, 3, (10), 11, 13, (14), 17, 18, (21), (22), 25, (26), 30, (31), (33), (39), 42, 45, (46), 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, 80, 82, 83, (88), 90, (94). $Skeletocutis amorpha (Fr.) Kotl. & Pouzar 

cs3,0$ Key C. Species with clamps at all septa. Skeletal and/or binding hyphae and/or cuticular cells, laccase absent

1,2$ a. Colony at first white to Pale Salmon Pink, becoming Seashell Pink to Vineceous Pink or Onionskin Pink, rarely Vinaceous Fawn. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Mat at first downy to low cottony, becoming low cottony-woolly to (sub)felty, sometimes even velvety. Reverse unchanged to pinkish. Odour absent or sweet. Basidiocarps often formed. Skeletal hyphae (1.5-)2-3 µm wide; binding hyphae 1-2 µm wide. Some submerged (and sometimes also some aerial) hyphae with irregularly thickened walls and meandering lumen. Mainly on gymnosperms. (Fomitopsis)
1,3$ b. Colony never pinkish

2,0$ a. (1) Chlamydospores ellipsoid, (8-)11-20(-24) x 6-8 µm///Growth rate 15-45 mm. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4(-4.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1.5-) 2-6.5(-8) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1970; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: (7), (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), 14, (15), 17, 21, 22, 24, 25, (26), 30, 33, (36), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 47, 48, 49, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (80), (82), 83, 85, (88), (89), 90, (93). $ Fomitopsis cajanderi (P. Karst.) Kotl. & Pouzar  / Syn.: Fomes subroseus (Weir) Overh.
2,0$ b. Chlamydospores absent. ///Growth rate 25-45 mm. Marginal hyphae 1.5-6 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide, thin- to rarely thick-walled. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1970. [p. 98]///Code: (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, 17, (18), 21, (22), 24, 25, (26), 30, (31), 33, (38), 39, 42, 4, 45, 46, 47, (48), 49, (51), 52, 53, 54, 83, (88), (89), 90, (93). $ Fomitopsis rosea (Alb. & Schw. ex Fr.) P. Karst

3,4$ a. (1) Generative hyphae with irregularly thickened walls and meandering lumen present
3,12$ b. Generative hyphae with irregularly thickened walls and meandering lumen absent

4,5$ a. (3) Some aerial hyphae (thin- or thick-walled) with short blunt refractive projections (sometimes difficult to find; samples of different parts of the mat should be examined). Chlamydospores rarely present. On angiosperms and gymnosperms. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, even, rarely bayed, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat at first downy to thinly woolly, becoming densely woolly or cottony-woolly, later often (sub)-felty, white to cream. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-4.5 µm wide, rarely reaching 6 µm in some species. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled. Skeletal hyphae completely or nearly solid, rarely to occasionally branched. (Coriolellus)
4,8$ b. No blunt refractive projections on the hyphae. Chlamydospores present, thick-walled (absent in Daedalea andamani). On angiosperms

5,6$ a. (4) Thick-walled generative hyphae often irregular with numerous blunt projections
5,7$ b. Thick-walled generative hyphae regular, rarely with blunt projections, which, however, may be present on the thin-walled hyphae

6,0$ a. (5) On angiosperms, rarely gymnosperms. Chlamydospores absent. ///Growth rate 28-45 mm. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae up to 6 µm wide, some with short, much-branched hyphae. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Sarkar, 1959; Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, 17, (18), 21, 22, 24, (25), 30, (36), 39, 42, 45, 46, 48, 49, 52, 53, (54), 59, 61, 83, (88), 89, (90), (93). $ Coriolellus albidus (Fr. ex Fr.) Donk  / Syn.: Coriolellus sepium (Berk.) Murr.
6,0$ b.* On angiosperms, mainly Rosaceae. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 8-15 x 5-9 µm. [p. 99]///Growth rate 20-35 mm. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-3(-6) µm wide. Aerial hyphae up to 6 µm wide. North-temperate. Ref.: Sarkar, 1959; Domanski, 1966a. ///Code: (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), (14), (17), 21, 22, 24, (25), 30, (36), 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 48, 49, 52, 53, (54), 59, 83, 85, (88), 89, (92). $ Coriolellus malicola (Berk. & Curt.) Murr.  
6,0,n$ The species described by Davidson and Campbell (1943) from Prunus under the name Trametes serialis Fr. is probably this species.
6,0$ c.* On gymnosperms. Chlamydospores absent. ///Growth rate ± 25 mm. North-temperate. Ref.: Domanski, 1969a. ///Code: (8), (9), (17), 21, 22, 24, (25), 30, 39, 42, (44), (45), 46, 48, 49,52,53,04), 59, 90, (92). $ Coriolellus ramentaceus (Berk. & Br.) Doman

7,0$ a. (5) Growth rate 25-35 mm. Mat after 6 weeks cottony to woolly, never felty.///Marginal hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide, some with much-branched, short, lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae up to 6 µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 2-3.5 µm wide. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Sarkar, 1959. ///Code: (8), (11), 13, (14), 17, (21), 22, 30, 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 48, 49, 52, 53, 54, 59, (61), (80), 83, (88), (89), 90, (93). $ Coriolellus heteromorphus (Fr.) Bond. & Sing.
7,0$ b.* Growth rate 25-40 mm. Mat becoming felty within 6 weeks. ///Marginal hyphae 1.5-4.5(-6) µm wide, some with short, much-branched, lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae up to 6.5(-8) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide. Swellings or chlamydospores sometimes present, 14-22 x 5-7.5 um. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1943, 1948; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Sarkar, 1959; Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: (2), (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, 17, (18), (19), 24, 25, 30, (31), (36), 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 48, 49, 52, 53, 54, (55), 59, (61), (80), 83, (85), (88), (89), 90,03). $ Coriolellus serialis (Fr.) Murr.
7,0$ c.* Growth rate 25-45 mm. Mat becoming felty within 6 weeks. ///Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-4.5(-6) µm wide. [p. 100] Skeletal hyphae 2-2.5 µm wide. Swellings or chlamydospores sometimes present, 14-22 x 5-7.5 µm. On gymnosperms. North-temperate, Australia. Ref.: Nobles, 1943; Sarkar, 1959. ///Code: (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, (15), 17, (21), 22, (24), 25, 30, 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 48, 49, 52, 53, (54), 59, (61), (80), 83, (85), (88), 90, (93). $ Coriolellus sinuosus (Fr.) Sarkar  / Syn.: Poria vaporaria Pers. sensu Fr.
7,0$ d.* Growth rate 15-30 mm. Mat becoming felty within 6 weeks. ///Marginal, aerial and skeletal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Mainly on gymnosperms. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Sarkar, 1959. ///Code: (8), (9), 13, 14, 17, 23, 24, 25, 30, 31, (36), 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, (47), 48, 49, 52, 53, 59, 83, (88), (93). $ Coriolellus variiformis (Peck) Murr.

8,0$ a. (4) Chlamydospores absent. ///Growth rate 30-40 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, locally farinaceous to subfelty, later felty, white. Marginal hyphae 2-3 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 2-4 µm wide, with thin to thickened, sometimes encrusted walls. Skeletal hyphae 2-3 µm wide. On angiosperms. India. Ref.: Sen, 1973. ///Code: (8), 13, 18, 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 39, (44), 46, 48, (49), 52, 53, 57, 89. $ Daedalea andamani Berk.
8,9$ b. Chlamydospores present, ellipsoid, thick-walled. ///Advancing zone typically raised, even to bayed, hyphae dense. Mat at first downy or cottony-woolly to floccose, becoming woolly, floccose-felty or felty, often locally farinaceous, white to cream. Sometimes lumps of more strongly pigmented mycelium present, which may become basidiocarps. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Hyphae with irregularly thickened walls (1.5-)2.5-5(-6) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae thick-walled to solid, 1.5-3.5 µm wide (but cf. Trametes moesta). (Daedalea)

9,0$ a. (8) Chlamydospores 6-8 x 4.5 µm. Skeletal hyphae often somewhat irregular, 0.7-5.2 µm wide. ///Growth rate 20-30 mm. Mat locally farinaceous. Basidiocarps may be formed within 6 weeks. Marginal and [p. 101] aerial hyphae (1.5-)2-5.5 µm wide. Africa. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: (8), (9), 12, 15, 18, 21, 22, 25, 30, (31), 37, 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, (48), 49, 51, 52, 53, (54), 80, , (88), 89. $ Trametes moesta Kalchbr.
9,10$ b. Chlamydospores larger. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-3.5 µm wide

10,0$ a. (9) Chlamydospores 18-30 x 9-12,um. ///Growth rate 20-35 mm. Advancing zone even, appressed. Lumps of mycelium present, becoming brown. Basidiocarps may be formed within 6 weeks. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Africa. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971./// Code: (8), (9), 13, 18, (19), 21, 22, 25, 30, (31), (34), (35), (37), 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, (48), 49, 52, 53, 85, (88), 89. $ Trametes roseola Pat. & Hariot
10,11$ b. Chlamydospores 10-20 x 6-13 µm

11,0$ a. (10) Growth rate 30-50 mm. Mat sometimes at first downy, later locally plumose or fan-shaped, not felty. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide. India, Japan. Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: (8), (9), 12, (15), 19, 21, 22, 25, 30, (31), 39, 42, 45, 46, 48, 49, 52, 53, 85, 89. $ Daedalea dickinsii (Berk.) Bond.
