17,0$ b. Chlamydospores ellipsoid to pip-shaped, 13-20 x 8-16 µm, wall up to 1.5(-2) µm thick. Skeletal hyphae hyaline to brown, 24(-5) µm wide, some with swellings ///Growth rate 25-50 mm. Advancing zone appressed or raised, even, hyphae distant. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, locally becoming felty, white to Cinnamon Buff or Cinnamon Brown. Basidiocarps often formed. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, some aerial hyphae up to 12 µm wide. Branching sometimes inequivalent. On angiosperms. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942. ///Code: (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, (19), 21, 22, (25), 30, (31), 34, (35), (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, 50, 52, 53, (54), (55), 67, (80), 85, (88), 89. $ Poria nigra (Berk.) Cooke

18,0$ a. (16) Colony at first white, becoming locally (especially against the margin of the Petri dish) Pinard Yellow to Orange Chrome or Flame Scarlet. Chlamydospores generally ellipsoid, rarely subglobose, 12-20 x 9-14 µm, but some in the aerial mycelium (sub)globose, 20-26 µm diam, walls up to 2(-2.5) µm thick. ///Growth rate 40-55 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae distant. Mat at the margin plumose, becoming thinly cottony. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-6.5(-7) µm wide. [p. 103] Branching sometimes inequivalent; some narrow hyphae much-branched. Aerial hyphae sometimes thick-walled. Skeletal hyphae 2-5 µm wide, rarely with swellings, walls up to 1.5(-2) µm thick. Oil drops between hyphae sometimes present. On angiosperms. N. America, Africa. ///Code: (7), (11), 12, 14, 21, 23, 30, (32), 35, 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, (58), (61), (80), 83, 85, 89. $ Tyromyces trichrous (Berk. & Curt.) Lowe  / Syn.: Polyporus pseudosulphureus W. H. Long
18,0$ b Colony at first white, becoming locally cream to Light Ochraceous Buff. Chlamydospores always subglobose to ellipsoid, 12-16(-20) x 9-13 µm, walls up to 0.8 µm. thick. ///Growth rate 25-40 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first thinly cottony to subfelty, becoming felty. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-6(-8) µm wide, some with swellings, some narrow hyphae much-branched. Skeletal hyphae 2-4 µm wide. On angiosperms. Australia, New Zealand. Ref.: Refshauge and Proctor, 1935. ///Code: (8), (11), 12, (14), (17), 21, (22), 24, 25, 30, 3-1, (35), 39, 42, 44, 46, 52, 53, 54,05),(61), 80, 83, 85, 89. $ Fomes hemitephrus (Berk.) Cooke 
18,0,n$ There are some contradictions with other facts: the species has been reported to cause a white rot (Cunningham, 1965) and also the description of Refshauge and Proctor deviates in some respects from the present one. 

19,0$ a. (14) On gymnosperms. N.-America, S.E.-Asia. Skeletal hyphae 1-2(-2.5) µm wide. ///Growth rate 20-35 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae dense. Mat cottony, becoming woolly to (sub)felty, locally lacunose, rarely farinaceous, at first whitish to Pale Maize Yellow, becoming Pinkish Buff to Cinnamon Buff, rarely Cinnamon Rufous, Reverse darker after 6 weeks. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1-5 µm wide, some with swellings up to 7 µm wide. Hyphae from older parts often with clampless septa. Some hyphae with resinous yellowish brown contents, some encrusted with minute crystals. Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: (8), (9), (11), 13, (18), 21, 22, 24, 25, (27), (29), 30, 31, (34), 35, 38, (39), (42), 45, 46, (48), 51, 52, 53, (57), 60, 67, (80), 82, 83, 90. [p. 104] $ Trametes carbonaria (Berk. & Curt.) Overh.
19,0$ b. On angiosperms, mainly Eucalyptus, rarely Pinus. Africa. Skeletal hyphae 2-4 µm wide.///Growth rate 30->70 mm. Advancing zone raised, even or slightly bayed. Mat silky to cottony, becoming woolly to felty, sometimes lacunose, at first whitish to Naphthalene Yellow or Buff Yellow, becoming Ochraceous Tawny to Cinnamon Brown. Reverse becoming bleached. Odour sweet. Marginal and aerial hyphae 3-5 µm wide, some aerial hyphae narrower, 1.5-3 µm wide and with clampless septa, some with brown resinous contents, some with up to 10 µm wide swellings. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1973. ///Code: (6), (7), (8), 12, (15), 20, 21, 22, 25, (27), 30, (31), 34, 37, (39), (40), (42), 45, 46, (48), 52, 53, 60, 67, (80), 89, (90). $ Polyporus baudonii Pat.

20,21$ a. (13) Growth rate about 15 mm
20,22$ b. Growth rate at least 25 mm. ///Mat at first downy or immediately cottony or cottony-woolly, sometimes floccose or with lumps of mycelium, sometimes becoming felty. Basidiocarps often formed within 6 weeks, (allowing a certain identification). Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-6(-7) µm wide. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5(-3) µm wide (in Fomitopsis pinicola up to 4 µm wide), thick-walled to solid. Chlamydospores when present subglobose to ellipsoid.

21,0$ a. (20) Chlamydospores ellipsoid, in the aerial mycelium 3.5-7.5 x 1.5-5.5 µm, in the submerged mycelium 12.5-20 x 5.5-7.5 µm. Mat farinaceous to floccose, becoming subfelty and finally floccose-felty to farinaceous. ///Colony white to cream. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3.5(-4) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide thin- to thick-walled, sometimes with swellings. Skeletal hyphae 1.8-3.6 µm wide, branched. On angiosperms. India, Sri Lanka. Ref.: Sen, 1973. ///Code: (9), 13, 18, 19, 24, (25), 30, 31, (37), 39, 45, 46, (47), 48, 52, 53, 80, 85, 89. $ Hexagonia sulcata Berk. [p. 105]
21,0$ b. (20) Chlamydospores ellipsoid, in the aerial mycelium 3.5-7.5 x 1.5-5.5 µm, in the submerged mycelium 12.5-20 x 5.5-7.5 µm. Mat farinaceous to floccose, becoming subfelty and finally floccose-felty to farinaceous. ///Colony white to cream. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3.5(-4) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide thin- to thick-walled, sometimes with swellings. Skeletal hyphae 1.8-3.6 µm wide, branched. On angiosperms. India, Sri Lanka. Ref.: Sen, 1973. ///Code: (9), 13, 18, 19, 24, (25), 30, 31, (37), 39, 45, 46, (47), 48, 52, 53, 80, 85, 89. $ Hexagonia sulcata Berk.

22,0$ a. (20) Mat with distinct or indistinct hyphal strands, becoming plumose and reaching the lid of the petri dish. Chlamydospores typically absent. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant or rather distant. Mat downy at the margin, becoming cottony to cottony-woolly, highest near the margin. Basidiocarps formed within 2-4 weeks. Submerged hyphae rarely up to 10 µm wide, sometimes ampullate or constricted at the septa, rarely with swellings. Mainly on gymnosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1958; Lombard and Gilbertson, 1965. ///Code: (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, 21, 22, 23, 30, (31), 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 52, 53, 54, (55), (78), (80), 81, 82, 83, (85), 88, (89), 90. $ Poria vaillantii (DC. ex Fr.) Cooke  / Syn.: Poria vaporaria auctt. 
22,0,n$ Lombard and Gilbertson (1965) found in slightly less than half of the isolates ëa few oval to spindleshaped immature chlamydospores, relatively thinwalled and measuring 824(31) x 79 µmí. These were not found in our isolates nor described by Nobles (1948) or van der Westhuizen (1958).
22,23$ b. Mat without hyphal strands, never plumose. Chlamydospores present (rarely absent in Polyporus palustris, sometimes in Piptoporus betulinus, but then chlamydospore-like swellings present).
22,23,n$ Polyporus secernibilis Berk. (? = Gloeoporus crispus (Pers. ex. Fr.) G. ///. Cunn.) as described by Bakshi et al. (1969) also keys out here; in this species chlamydospores are lacking and some hyphae are apically swollen.

23,0$ a. (22) Chlamydospores 7-13.5 x 3.5-8.5 µm. On angiosperms. $ See Polyporus rubidus, key E, note under 24a
23,24$ b. Chlamydospores on average wider. On angiosperms or gymnosperms

24,0$ a. (23) Chlamydospore-like swellings and sometimes also chlamydospores present, 6-12 x 5-8 µm, with thin or only slightly thickened walls. Restricted to Betula. Basidiospores allantoid, 4.5-6 x 1.2-1.5 µm. ///Growth rate 45->70 mm, on ChA much less. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Branching of hyphae sometimes inequivalent, some hyphae with short, much-branched lateral hyphae. North-temperate. Ref.: MacDonald, 1937; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: (6), (7), (12), (13), 14, 17, 19, 21, (22), 30, (31), 39, 42, 44, 46, (48), (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), (61), 80, 83, (85), (88), 89, (93). $ Piptoporus betulinus (Bull. ex Fr.) P. Karst.  
24,0,n$ Some aerial hyphae have, as also mentioned by Nobles (1948), a somewhat irregularly thickened wall, but they definitely cannot be considered as hyphae with a meandering lumen as in e.g. Coriolellus.
24,25$ b. Chlamydospores present, becoming thick-walled (except sometimes Polyporus palustris and Polyporus meliae). Not known from Betula (except sometimes Polyporus meliae). Basidiospores ovoid, ellipsoid or cylindrical, not allantoid, at least 2 µm diam

25,0$ a. (24) Chlamydospores globose, 7-10 µm diam. Basidiocarps not formed. On gymnosperms.///Growth rate about 40 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Some submerged hyphae with oily contents, 1.5-3 µm wide. India, Sri Lanka. Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), 18, (19), 21, 30, 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, 48, 52, 53, 54, (60), 83, 85, 90. $ Fomes rufolaccatus Bose
25,0$ b Chlamydospores 10-20 x 7.5-12 µm. Basidiocarps typically formed within 2-3 weeks. Spores cylindrical, 6-8(-9) x 2-3.5 µm. Mainly on gymnosperms, rarely angiosperms. ///Growth rate 35-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant or rather distant. Submerged hyphae up to 11(-15) µm wide, some with thickened walls. N.- and S.-America, Asia. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; McKay, 1967; Bakshi et al., 1969. ///Code: (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, 19, 21, 24, 25, 30, (31), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, 48, 52, 53, 54, 55, (80), 83, (85), 88, (89), 90, (93). $ Polyporus palustris Berk. & Curt. [p. 107]
25,0$ c. Chlamydospores typically subglobose, 6-12(-14) x 6-9(-14) µm, some ellipsoid, up to 20(-25) x 11(-14) µm. Basidiocarps sometimes formed within 6 weeks. Spores ovoid to ellipsoid, 6-8 x 3.5-4 µm. On gymnosperms and angiosperms. ///Growth rate 35-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Skeletal hyphae typically unbranched, 1.5-4 µm wide, but some isolates rarely with narrow, much-branched skeletal hyphae, (1-)1.5-2 µm wide. Some aerial and submerged hyphae up to 9(-18) µm wide, but these are not present in all isolates. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Mounce, 1929; Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Bakshi et al., 1970; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), (18), 19, 21, , 22, (24), (25), 30, (31), 39, 42, (44), 45, 46, (47), (48), (51), 52, 53, 54, (55), 83, 85, (88), (89), (90), (93). $ Fomitopsis pinicola (Schw. ex Fr.) P. Karst.
25,0$ d. Chlamydospores 12-20 x 7-16 µm. Basidiocarps sometimes formed within 6 weeks. Spores cylindrical, 6-8 x 2-3 µm. On angiosperms. /// Growth rate 40->70 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae distant or rather distant. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide, some branched, but typically not branched. Some submerged hyphae up to 9 µm wide. N.-America. Ref.: McKay, 1967. ///Code: (6), (7), (8), (11), 12, (14), 21, 22, (24), (25), 30, (31), (37), 39, 42, 44, 45, 46, (47), (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), 82, 83, 85, (88), 89, (93). $ Polyporus meliae Underw
25,0$ e. Chlamydospores (8-)13-18(-20) x (6-)11-15 µm, becoming very thick-walled. Basidiocarps sometimes formed within 6 weeks. Spores ovoid, 4.5-7 x 4-5 µm. On angiosperms, rarely gymnosperms. ///Growth rate 40-65 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae distant. Skeletal hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide, some branched. Submerged hyphae up to 7 µm wide, N.-America. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942. ///Code: 2, (7), (8), (11), 12, 14, (17), (19), 21, 22, (25), (26), 30, 39, 42, 44, (45), 46, (47), (48), (51), 52, 53, 54, 83, 85, (88), 89, (90), (92). $ Polyporus spraguei Berk. & Curt.

cs4,0$ Key D. Clamps present at all septa.Skeletal binding hyphae cuticular cells absent. Laccase present


1,2$ a. Oedocephaloid (erect colourless conidiophores with a terminal swelling or vesicle) conidiophores present. Imperf. state: Spiniger
1,10$ b. Oedocephaloid (erect colourless conidiophores with a terminal swelling or vesicle) conidiophores absenta
1,7$ autres Vararia

2,3$ a. (1) Conidia cylindrical or slightly curved, smooth, 9-19 x 2.5-4.5 µm.///Growth rate 35-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat at first downy to cottony, becoming subfelty. Reverse unchanged or bleached, rarely somewhat darker under the inoculum. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, some with many short lateral branches. Conidiogenous denticles up to 2 µm long. Submerged hyphae sometimes up to 9 µm wide. (Hyphoderma, keyed out according to McKeen, 1952).
2,4$ b. Conidia globose, ovoid, pyriform or ellipsoid

3,0$ a. (2) Conidia 11.5-17 x 3.5-4 µm (average 14.3 x 3.7 µm). Conidiogenous vesicles up to 10 µm diam. Mat white at first, later with cream to pinkish buff or pale brownish tinges. Basidiocarps not formed. Restricted to Populus. N.-America. Ref.: McKeen, 1952. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21, 24, 30, 31, (34), (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 52, 53, 54, 55, (60), 61, (67), 83, 86, 87, 89, (93). $ Hyphoderma populneum (Peck) Donk / Imperf. st.: Spiniger curiosus (Parm. & Zukov) Stalpers
3,0$ b.* Conidia 10-19 x 2.5-4.5 µm (average 13.8 x 3.3 µm). Conidiogenous vesicles 8-15.3 µm diam. Mat white, but sometimes brownish against the side wall. Basidiocarps not formed. On angiosperms (including Populus). North-temperate ///Ref.: Nobles, 193-5; McKeen, 1952; Boidin, 1958a. Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, (17), 21, (22), 24, 30, (37), 39, 42, 45, 52, 53, (54), (55), (61), 83, 86, 87, 89, (93). $ Hyphoderma mutatum (Peck) Donk  / Syn.: Peniophora allescheri Bres.
3,0$ c.* Conidia 9-15 x 2.7-3.6 µm (average 12.0 x 3.3 µm). Conidiogenous vesicles up to 10 µm diam. Mat white [p. 109] with pinkish buff tinges. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. On angiosperms. N.-America ///Ref.: McKeen, 1952. ///Code: 1, (7), 13, (17), (19), 21, 24, 30, 31, (37), 39, 42, 45, 52, 53, (67), (81), 86, 87, (88), 89, (93). $ Hyphoderma heterocystidium (Burt) Donk

4,5$ a. (2) Chlamydospores present, sometimes rare. Gloeocystidia sulpho-positive, with papillate protuberances. Dichohyphidia typically present, with thickened or thick dextrinoid walls. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant or rather distant. Reverse unchanged to typically brownish. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-4(-6) µm wide. Some aerial hyphae with oil drops or resinous contents. Conidiogenous denticles after conidium dehiscence sometimes with strongly staining apical portion. (Vararia, keyed out according to Lanquetin, 1973).
4,9$ b. Chlamydospores, gloeocystidia and dichohyphidia absent.

5,0$ a. (4) Conidiogenous vesicles 5.5-12 µm wide. ///Growth rate 20-35 mm. Mat at first downy to cottony-woolly, becoming locally floccose or woolly-farinaceous, white to cream. Conidia subglobose to ovoid, (2-)2.5-5.5 x 2-4 µm. Gloeocystidia sometimes rare, dichohyphidia typically absent (but present on Hagem agar). Chlamydospores subglobose to ellipsoid, 7.5-15.5 x 4.5-8 µm. North-temperate Ref.: Maxwell, 1954; Lanquetin, 1973. ///Code: 1, (8), (9), 13, (17), (18), 19, 21, 22, 30, 31, (35), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 52, 53, 60, (73), (80), 85, 86, 87, 90, (93), 95, 98, (99), (100). $ Vararia granulosa (Fr.) Laurila
5,6$ b. Conidiogenous vesicles 4-6(-8) µm wide

6,0$ a. (5) Conidia up to 4 x 3.5 m, on average smaller ///Growth rate 25-50 mm. Mat downy or low cottony-woolly to woolly, becoming (sub)felty to farinaceousfelty or somewhat floccose, white to cream or Avellaneous. Basidiocarps often formed. Some aerial hyphae with thickened walls and/or yellowish contents. Conidia subglobose to ovoid, 2.5-4 x 2.5-3.5 µm. Chlamydospores hyaline to yellowish, (sub)globose, 12-15(-17) µm diam. Dichohyphidia absent [p. 110] (but present on Hagem agar). Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Lyman, 1907; Nobles, 1942, Lanquetin, 1973. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), 18, (19), 21, 22, 24, (25), 30, 31, (33), (35), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), 51, 52, 53, (54), (58), (60), (67), 73, (83), 85, 86, 87, (88), 89, (94), 95, 100. $ Vararia effuscata (Cooke & Ellis) D. P. Rogers & H. S. Jacks.  
6,0,n$ Lanquetin (1973) describes Vararia aff. dura differing mainly in the constant presence of dichohyphidia on MEA.   
6,8$ b. Conidia at least 4 x 3.5 µm, on average larger than 4 µm


7,0$ a. (6) Chlamydospores abundant. Colony becoming cream to Avellaneous. Dichohyphidia absent or very rare (but present on Hagem agar). $ See Vararia effuscata under 6a
7,0,n$	Dichostereum effuscatum (Cooke & Ellis) Boidin & Lanq., Mycotaxon 6(2): 284, 1977.///Bas.: Corticium effuscatum Cooke & Ellis, Grevillea 9(51): 103, 1881.///Basidiome resupinate, effused, adnate, membranaceous to crustaceous, hymenophore smooth, cream to ochraceous, margin indeterminate. Hyphal system dimitic, generative hyphae with clamps, thin-walled, 2-3 µm wide, dichohyphae present in the subiculum, thick-walled, branches about 1-3 µm wide, dextrinoid. Gloeocystidia sinuous cylindrical, somewhat irregular, usually obtuse, tubular, variable in size, up to 100 µm long and 8-12 µm wide, with distinct walls, and with granular contents, SA+. Basidia subclavate, sinuous, 30-40 x 5-7 µm, with 4 sterigamata, and a basal clamp. Basidiospores subglobose, 6-8 x 5-7 µm, warted with strongly amyloid warts, sligthly thick-walled, hyaline to yellowish brown.///Distribution in Europe: Germany, France, Macedonia, Greece, Netherlands, Sweden, Ukraine, and Russia. Uncommon species in all Europe. It is the first record in Italy, known from Basilicata Region.///Substratum: on Alnus.

7,0$ b. Chlamydospores very rare or absent. Colony at first white to cream, becoming cinnamon. Dichohyphidia present. ///Growth rate about 40 mm. Mat at first cottony-woolly, becoming subfelty. Conidia 3-4(-5) x 2.5-3(-3.8) µm. Chlamydospores 7-12 x 6-10 µm. N.-America. Ref.: Lanquetin, 1973. ///Code: 1, (7), (8), 13, 21, 22, 24, 30, 31, 34, (35), 38, 39, 42, 45, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (60), 67, 73, (85), 86, 87, 89, (94), 95, 97, (99), 100). $ Vararia pallescens (Schw.) D. P. Rogers & H. S. Jacks./ Syn. Dichostereum pallescens (Schwein.) Boidin & Lanq.
7,0,n$Mats white to Light Buff, around inocula thin, appressed and subfelty or moderately thin, slightly raised, and subtomentose, then becoming raised, slightly thicker, woolly to cottony toward margins at 2 wk, by 6 wk white to Light Ochraceous-Buff or Tawny-Olive, moderately thick to thick, around inocula appressed to slightly raised, subfelty to felty or downy, then toward margins becoming raised, tomentose to felty; margins even, slightly raised to raised; no odor at 2 and 6 wk; agar unchanged or Ochraceous-Buff to Ochraceous-Tawny at 6 wk; not fruiting by 6 wk. TAA mats at 2 wk white to Light Buff, thick, slightly raised to raised, tomentose to cottony- woolly, often zonate; margins slightly uneven, raised.///MEA 40-42; 90+; TAA 26-33; 44-46; GAA 0-11; 0-15; TyA yellow, 29-41; 75-88///Microscopic characters: Marginal hyphae 3-5.5 µm diam, thin walled, nodose septate, sparsely branched, often branches arising opposite clamps. Submerged and aerial hyphae 1.5-5.5 µm diam, thin walled, nodose septate, moderately branched. Gloeoplerous hyphae 1.5-2 µm diam, aseptate but clamped at base, with branches rigid and arising at right angles and the tips often slightly swollen, filled with solid refractive materials, numerous in aerial and submerged mats at 2 and 6 wk. Gloeocystidia more or less cylindrical, 2.5-3.5 µm diam, often with an apical papilla, terminal or lateral, containing oil-like refractive globules, scattered to numerous in aerial mats at 2 and 6 wk. Chlamydospores limoniform, 10-20 x 7-10 µm, slightly thick walled, intercalary, rare in submerged mats at 6 wk (in FP 90120). Dichohyphidia 1-2 µm diam, hyaline at first then yellowish brown, slightly thick walled, aseptate, dichotomously branched, with branches long and flexuous, acuminate, scattered to numerous in aerial mats at 2 and 6 wk.///Conidia spherical, 3-4 µm diam, thin walled, blastic, borne on oedocephaloid conidiophores,  these 3.5-6 µm diam, often swollen at base, absent or rare in aerial mats at 2 wk, absent at 6 wk.


Western North America, northeastern U. S. A., and Ontario
Throughout U. S. A., British Columbia, Ontario, and West Indies Southeastern U. S. A. sexuality: Tetrapolar (Lanquetin 1973b, p. 179)
Cultural descriptions: Lanquetin (1973b, p. 179); Lentz & McKay (1966, p. 15); Stalpers (1978, p. 110); Thind & Rattan (1977, p. 18).
Species codes: 2. 3c. 8v. 15p. (32). (33). (34). 37. 38. (39). 44.54. 60. 2a. 3c. 8v. 15. 33. (34). 37. 39. 44.54. 60. 63. (Lanquetin 1973b, p. 180, except 25d replaced with 8v).
Remarks. --Although Rogers a Jackson (1943) considered Corticium sordulentum Cooke & Massee a synonym of Vararia pallescens (Schwein.) D. P. Rogers & H. Jacks., Lanquetin (1973b) has shown that the two species are interincompatible and therefore distinct taxa. Since most mycologists have accepted the synonymy proposed by Rogers & Jackson, papers dealing with V. pallescens should be used cautiously. Dichostereum pallescens is associated with a white rot (Gilbertson & al. 1974; Ginns 1986a) of angiospermous and gymnospermous woods and is found widely throughout the U. S. A. (Gilbertson 1965b; Shaw 1973; Gilbertson & al. 1974), British Columbia and Ontario in Canada (Ginns 1986a), and the West Indies (Welden 1965). Dichostereum pallescens is the only Dichostereum species treated herein that develops dichohyphidia regularly on MEA (Table 4). In addition, it only occasionally develops conidia and chlamydospores. See also discussions under D. effuscatum and D. sordulentum. 

8,0$ a. (6) Conidia globose, 4-4.5(-5) µm diam, rarely subglobose, 4-6 x 3.5-5 µm. Australia. ///Growth rate about 30 mm. Mat downy to low cottony, becoming cottony-woolly, white to cream, locally pinkish. Reverse unchanged. Chlamydospores subglobose, 7-14 x 8-11 µm. Ref.: Lanquetin, 1973. ///Code: 1, (8), (9), 13, (17), 21, 22, (24), 30, 31, 39, 42, (44), (45), (51), 52, 53, (60), 73, 85, 86, 87, (89), (90), (94), 95, 98, 100. $ Vararia rhodospora (Wakef.) G. H. Cunn. 
8,0$ b. Conidia subglobose to pyriform, 4-4.5(-5) x (3-)3.5-4 (-4.5) µm. Tropical Africa.///Growth rate about 40-65 mm. Mat downy to cottony-woolly, at first white to cream, later locally with yellowish or brownish tinges. Reverse unchanged to darker. Chlamydospores subglobose, 10-13 µm diam, or ellipsoid, 10-16 x 6-9 µm. Ref.: Lanquetin, 1973. [p. 111]///Code: 1, (7), (8), 13, (17), 21, 22, 30, 31, (34), 35, (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), (51), 52, 53, (54), (60), (61), 73, 85, 86, 87, 89, 95, 98. $ Vararia ramulosa Boidin & Lanquetin

9,0$ a. (4) Conidia (sub)globose, 34(-5) x 2.5-3(-3.8) m, somewhat thick-walled. $ See Vararia pallescens, under 7b
9,0$ b. Conidia narrowly ovoid to narrowly ellipsoid, 4.5-7 x 2.5-3.5 m, thin-walled. ///Growth rate about 10 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even to somewhat bayed. Mat cottony, at first white, later locally ochraceous to brownish. Hyphal strands present. Reverse darker. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-3.5 µm wide. Conidiogenous vesicles up to 4.5 µm wide. Conidiophores sometimes swollen at the base. On gymnosperms. N.-America. Ref.: Maxwell, 1954. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), 13, (15), 21, 30, (31), (34), (35), 38, 39, (42), (44), (45), 52, 53, 81, 86, 87, 90. $ Resinicium furfuraceum (Bres.) Parm.

10,11$ a. (10) Stephanocysts present. ///Growth rate 20-45 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2.5(-6.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae with thin to somewhat thickened walls, often short-celled (most cells smaller than 60 µm), with small clamp connections. Cells often somewhat swollen. Submerged hyphae often irregular, with swellings up to 8(-15) µm diam, often with thickened walls (Hyphoderma)
10,12$ b. Stephanocysts absent

11,0$ a. (10) Stephanocysts each consisting of a sessile hemispherical cell surrounded by a row of spines directly on the hyphae. There is always a minute clamp at the septum of the stephanocyst. Cystidia or cystidioid structures absent. ///Mat appressed after 2 weeks, becoming locally downy to farinaceous, later silky, radiating, white. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, (18), 20, 30, 39, 44, (48), 52, 53, 54, (55), 77, 80, 83, (89), (90), (93), 95, 97, 99. $ Hyphoderma puberum (Fr.) Wallr. [p. 112]
11,0$ b. Stephanocysts each consisting of an ovoid structure bearing a circular row of spines which surrounds a hemispheric cell. Two clamps are present, one at the base of the ovoid structure and one at the row of spines. Cystidioid structures typically present, clavate to capitate, often scarcely to densely encrusted. ///Mat appressed to downy or farinaceous, becoming cottony or cottony-woolly, sometimes silky and radiating, rarely pellicular, white. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1950, 1958a; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, 14, (15), 17, 18, (20), 21, (22), (24), 30, (37), 39, 44, (48), 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), 72, (75), 77, 80, 82, 83, (89), (90), (92), (93), 95, 97, 99. $ Hyphoderma tenue (Pat.) Donk  / Syn.: Hyphoderma praetermissum P. Karst.  
11,0,n$This is probably a species aggregate; isolates varying greatly in growth rate and texture of the mat.

12,13$ a. (10) Cystidia and/or gloeocystidia present
12,28$ b. Cystidia and gloeocystidia absent

13,14$ a. (12) Cystidia present
13,18$ b. Only gloeocystidia present

14,15$ a. (13) Cystidia not capitate, smooth or encrusted over much of the upper part, with thickened walls, often brownish. /// Growth rate 55-65(->70) mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first cottony to cottony-woolly, white, becoming pellicular to felty, cream or Light Buff or with brown tinges. Reverse darker. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-5(-6) µm wide. Aerial hyphae hyaline to brownish, thin- to thick-walled, (2-)2.5-5(-8) µm wide, some with resinous content, others sparsely encrusted or with oil drops on the wall. North-temperate. (Amylostereum)
14,16$ b. Cystidia encrusted at the very apex which is often somewhat clavate or capitate, or cystidia capitate with at least basally thickened walls (the thickening may disappear in KOH) (if cystidia are thin-walled, smooth and capitate, $ See under 28; if torulose, See under 65a) 16

15,0$ a. (14) Arthroconidia present. Odour sweet or indistinct ///Ref.: Siepmann and Zycha, 1968. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (4), (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, (34), 35, (36), 38, 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, †52, 53, (54), (57), 58, 60, 72, (80), (82), 83, 84, (88), 90. [p. 113] $ Amylostereum areolatum (Fr.) Boidin  
15,0,n$ Siepmann and Zycha (1968) mention a strain which forms septa with multiple clamps in addition to septa without or with only a simple clamp connection. This is probably based on a misidentification.
15,0$ b. Arthroconidia absent. Odour indistinct. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Basham, 1959. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (4), (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), 21, (22), 24, 30, 31, 35, 38, 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (51), 52, 53, (54), (55), (57), 58, 60, 67, 72, (80), (82), 83, 90, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Amylostereum chailletii (Pers. ex Fr.) Boidin

16,0$ a. (14) Cystidia capitate, not encrusted, at least basally with thickened walls, apical part 6.5-9.5 µm wide. Reaction on laccase weak. $ See  Xenasma pruinosum, key E under 44a
16,17$ b. Cystidia encrusted at the very apex. Reaction on laccase distinct

17,0$ a. (16) Cystidia capitate, apically covered with large angular crystals giving it a stellate appearance. Diameter of apex with crystals up to 25 µm. Apex without crystals distinctly capitate, up to 6.5(-10) µm diam. Arthroconidia sometimes present. ///Growth rate 25-45 mm. Advancing zone appressed, rarely raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat downy to woolly or cottony-woolly, low, often with radiating hyphae or strands; colony translucent at many places. Reverse bleached. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-5(-6.5) µm wide, with small clamps. Mainly on gymnosperms, but also on angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Nobles, 1953; Boidin, 1958a; Siepmann, 1971; van der Westhuizen 1971. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (8), (12), (13), (14), 17, (19), 21, 22, 30, 37, 39, (42), 44, 52, 53, (54), 72, (75), 81, 82, 83, (84), (89), (90), 95, 97, 99. $ Resinicium bicolor (Alb. & Schw. ex Fr.) Parm.
17,0$ b. Cystidia typically clavate, covered with rather small crystals not appearing stellate, apex with crystals up to 9 µm wide, but often much less. Apex without crystals, clavate to slightly capitate, rarely reaching 5 µm diam. Arthroconidia absent. $ See Hirschioporus, key B under 8b   

18,19$ a. (13) Growth rate up to 40 mm. Chlamydospores often present
18,23$ b. Growth rate at least 40 mm. Chlamydospores absent (except Vararia sphaericospora)

19,20$ a. (18) Basidiocarps always present within 6 weeks, hydnoid to coralloid.///Growth rate 10-30 mm. Advancing zone appressed to submerged, hyphae distant. Reverse unchanged. Hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide. Aerial ///nd submerged hyphae often with swellings or constricted at the septa. Gloeocystidia somewhat irregular, acute, with yellowish contents, sulpho-negative, 4-10 µm wide. Chlamydospores subglobose to ellipsoid, with firm to thickened walls. North-temperate. (Hericium)
19,21$ b. Basidiocarps when present not hydnoid or coralloid

20,0$ a. (19) On angiosperms. Chlamydospores 7-11 x 5.5-7.5 µm. Mat appressed to downy or downy-farinaceous, remaining so or becoming thinly cottony. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942; Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), (10), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, 18, (21), 30, (31), 39, 42, 45, 52, 53, 54, (60), 73, (80), (82), 83, 85, 88, 89, (94). $ Hericium erinaceus (Bull. ex Fr.) Pers.
20,0$ b. Mainly on gymnosperms, but also angiosperms. Chlamydospores (7.5-)9-15 x (5.5-)6.5-9 µm. Mat appressed to downy, typically becoming thinly cottony to cottony-farinaceous. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, (18), 21, 30, (31), 39, 42, 45, (51), 52, 53, 54,00), 73, (80), (82), 83, 85, 88, (89), (90), (94), 95, 99. $ Hericium coralloides (Scop. ex Fr.) S. F. Gray 
20,0,n$ The size of the chlamydospores probably cannot be used for identification purposes. Boidin ascribed 6.517.5 x 512 µm to H. erinaceus and 68 x 58 µm to H. coralloides. The basidiospores of H. coralloides produced in culture are 4.86.1 x 4.55.5 µm, as on the natural substrate. Those of H. erinaceus are 4.55.5 x 3.75 µm (in all three isolates) which is considerably smaller than on the natural substrate.   

21,0$ a. (19) Chlamydospores absent. In cones of Pinus. Basidiocarp initials sometimes present within 6 weeks, longstipitate. ///Growth rate 12-20 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat appressed, sometimes locally downy, white to cream. Reverse unchanged or slightly darker under inoculum. Marginal hyphae [p. 115] (1-)1.5-3.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, some thinly encrusted. Gloeocystidia 6-9 µm wide. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Petersen, 1976. ///Code: (1), 3, (9), (11), 13, (14), (16), (17), 30, (31), (38), 39, 42, 45, 51, 52, 53, (57), (60), 73, 82, 83, (88), 90, (94). $ Auriscalpium vulgare S. F. Gray  
21,0,n$ Boidin et al. (1976) described some species of Vararia from tropical Africa which key out here; chlamydospores absent; substrate angiospermous or unknown; often with yellowish tinges and growth rate about 3540 mm.  †  
21,22$ b. Chlamydospores present. On angiosperms. Basidiocarps, when present, effused. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae dense. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, with thin to thickened walls.

22,0$ a. (21) Submerged hyphae irregular, locally up to 6(-8) µm wide. Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to low woolly and finally cottony to farinaceous, white to cream, sometimes locally Pinkish Buff to Cinnamon Buff. ///Growth rate 12-25 mm on both MEA and ChA. Chlamydospores 6-12 x 5-10 µm. Gloeocystidia irregularly cylindrical to clavate. Some hyphae covered with brownish resinous material. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, (16), 17, 18, (19), (21), 22, 30, 31, (34), 39, 42, (44), 45, 52, 53, 54, (57), 73, 80, (82), 83, 85, (88), 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Gloeocystidiellum porosum (Berk. & Curt.) Donk
22,0$ b. Submerged hyphae regular, up to 4(-5) µm wide. Mat at first downy to low cottony, soon becoming farinaceous or sometimes velvety or minutely floccose, white to cream or locally with brownish tinges. ///Growth rate 7-35 mm on MEA, on ChA 20-35 mm. Some aerial hyphae narrow, about 1 µm wide. Chlamydospores present, sometimes rare, 8-13 x 5.5-8 µm. Gloeocystidia cylindrical, sometimes locally inflated, with irregular contents. Some hyphae covered with brownish material. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (10), 13, (14), (15), (17), 18, (19), (21), 26, 30, 31, (34), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, †51, 52, 53, (57), (58), 73, (82), 83, (85), (88), 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. [p. 116] $ Laxitextum bicolor (Fr.) Lentz     

23,0$ a. (18) Chlamydospores globose, 10-17 µm diam, becoming thick-walled. Dichohyphidia with short terminal branches (2-7 x 1 µm). ///Plate covered within 2 weeks. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat woolly, white, becoming pellicular and cream. Hyphae 1-6(-8) µm wide, often containing oil-drops. Gloeocystidia rare, sulpho-negative. Submerged hyphae irregular, 1-8(-10) µm wide. N.-America. Ref.: Boidin et al., 1976. ///Code: 1, 2, (6), 13, (21), 22, 24, 30, 31, 39, 44, 45, (47), (50), 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), 60, (61), (73), 80, 85, (93), 95, 97, 99. $ Vararia sphaericospora Gilbertson
23,24$ b. Chlamydospores absent. Dichohyphidia, when present, with long terminal branches

24,0$ a. (23) Mat after 6 weeks distinctly yellowish. Dichohyphidia present, with long narrow (up to 1 µm wide) terminal branches. ///Growth rate 40-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat low cottony-woolly to floccose, becoming somewhat farinaceous. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1-5(-7) µm wide. Gloeocystidia sulpho-positive, often with apical protuberances (schizopapillae). Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Boidin et al., 1976. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, (17), 18, (19), 21, 22, 30, 31, 35, 39, 44, 45, (47), (50), 51, 52, 53, 54, (60), 73, (81), 83, (89), (90), 95, 97, 99. $ Vararia investiens (Schw.) P. Karst.  
24,0,n$ Other species of Vararia may also key out here, e.g. V. pectinata (Burt) D. P. Rogers & H. S. Jacks. with sulphonegative gloeocystidia and bulbils, described by Boidin et al. (1976).
24,25$ b. Mat never yellowish, but sometimes locally brownish. Dichohyphidia absent. ///Plate covered in 2 weeks, rarely growth rate 60-70 mm. Advancing zone typically raised, hyphae distant. Mat typically cottony-woolly to woolly-floccose, sometimes becoming subfelty, white at first, later (especially agains the side of the plate) with brownish tinges. Basidiocarps sometimes formed. Reverse [p. 117] unchanged or darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5(-7) µm wide, sometimes with short much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae hyaline or with brownish contents, thin- to thick-walled; walls somewhat gelatinized when thick. Spirally coiled hyphae sometimes present. Gloeocystidia typically lateral, clavate or narrowly triangular to somewhat pyriform. (Peniophora)

25,26$ a. (24) On angiosperms, mainly Populus
25,27$ b. On gymnosperms

26,0$ a. (25) Mat remaining rather low. Reverse typically dark. Thick-walled aerial hyphae absent or very rare after 2 weeks. Basidiocarps (when formed) lacking cystidia. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; McKay & Lentz, 1960. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 12, 14, 19, 21, 22, 24, 30, (31), (34), 38, 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, (54), (57), (60), 61, (67), (73), 82, 83, (88), 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora polygonia (Pers. ex Fr.) Bourd. & Galz.
26,0$ b. Mat after 2 weeks loosely cottony to cottony-woolly. Reverse unchanged or slightly darker. Thick-walled aerial hyphae abundant after 2 weeks. Basidiocarps (when present) with cystidia. North-temperate Ref.: McKay and Lentz, 1960. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), 19, 21, 22, (24), 30, 31, (34), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, (54), (57), 61, 73, 82, 83, (88), 89, (94). $ Peniophora rufa (Fr.) Boidin

27,0$ a. (25, 85) Mainly on Pinus, very rarely other gymnosperms. Odour absent or suggesting spearmint. Europe, Asia. Ref.: K‰‰rik & Rennerfelt, 1957; Weresub and Gibson, 1960. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 12, 14, (19), 21, (24), (25), 30, 31, (34), (36), (38), 39, 42, 45, 48, (50), 52, 53, 54, 61, 73, (81), 82, 83, 90, (94). $ Peniophora pini (Schleich. ex Fr.) Boidin
27,0$ b.* Mainly on Pinus, very rarely on other gymnosperms. Odour absent. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1956 (as Fungus `Tí); Weresub and Gibson, 1960. [p. 118]///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, (19), 21, †22, (25), 30, 31, (34), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, 54, 61, 73, (81), 82, 83, 90, (94). $ Peniophora duplex Burt
27,0$ c.* Mainly on Pinus, very rarely other gymnosperms. Odour suggesting coconut. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1956; Boidin, 1958a (as P. pini); Nobles, 1965; Weresub and Gibson, 1960; McLain and Weresub, 1962. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 12, 14, 18, 19, 21, 22, 24, (25), 30, 31, (34), 36, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), 52, 53, (54), (60), 61, (67), 73, (80), 82, 83, (88), 90, (94). $ Peniophora pseudopini Weresub & Gibson
27,0$ d.* Mainly on Picea, very rarely other gymnosperms. Odour absent or faint. Typical swellings with narrow branches often present. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1956. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 12, (14), 19, 21, 22, 24, 30, 31, 34, (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), (50), (51), 52, 53, (54), (57), (60), 61, (65), (67), 73, 80, 82, 83, (88), 90, (94). $Peniophora septentrionalis Laurila
27,0$ e.* Mainly on Picea and Pseudotsuga, rarely other gymnosperms or on angiosperms. Odour absent or suggesting iodoform. Characteristic swellings with narrow branches (bulbils) often present. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1956; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, (17), 19, 21, 22, 24, 30, 31, 34, (36), (37), 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, (54), (57), 60, 61, (65), 67, 73, 80, 82, 83, (88), (89), 90, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora pitya (Pers.) J. Erikss     

28,29$ a. (12) Terminal swellings (allocysts, capitate structures) present
28,38$ b. No terminally swollen structures present

29,0$ a. (28) Chlamydospores present. Clamps not at all septa. $ See Climacodon septentrionalis and Columnocystis abietina, key G under 16
29,30$ b. Chlamydospores absent. Clamps present at all septa

30,0$ a. (29) At least some marginal hyphae with many narrow, much-branched lateral hyphae. [p. 119] ///Growth rate 50->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to densely cottony or cottony-woolly, later sometimes subfelty or somewhat farinaceous, white to cream, later locally brownish. Reverse unchanged to darker. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-5(-6.5) µm wide. Some aerial hyphae with thickened or thick walls, some with swellings or irregular; swellings often apical, thin- to thick-walled, up to 9 µm diam. Some hyphae covered with brownish material. North-temperate. ///Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, (18), 21, 22, (24), 30, 31, (34), (38), 39, 42, 45, 48, 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, 58, (60), 61, (75), 80, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. /// $ Peniophora lycii (Pers.) Hˆhn. & Litsch.
30,31$ b. Marginal hyphae without narrow, much-branched, later al hyphae

31,0$ a. (30) Plate covered in 1-2 weeks. Mat at first downy, becoming floccose to plumose or cottony, finally felty, white to cream. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. ///Advancing zone appressed, even to bayed, hyphae distant. Reverse unchanged or bleached. Marginal hyphae (1-)2-4.5(-7) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1-6 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, all septa with clamps or clamps lacking at some septa of widest and narrowest hyphae. Terminal or lateral vesicles ovoid to pyriform, up to 10 µm diam, often stalked. Sometimes hyphae with brownish contents. On angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; van der Westhuizen, 1958. ///Code: 1, 3, (5), (6), (11), 13, 14, (15), 17, 19, 21, 23, 25, 30, 31, (37), 39 or (39), (42), 45, 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, (60), 75, (82), 83, (88), 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Chondrostereum purpureum (Pers. ex Fr.) Pouzar
31,32$ b.* Growth rate 20-60 mm. Mat thin, downy to cottony, sometimes locally farinaceous, white to cream. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks.///Advancing zone typically raised, hyphae rather distant. Reverse unchanged or bleached. Vesicles up to 11 µm diam.
31,33$ c.* Growth rate not exceeding 20 mm. Mat typically downy [p. 120] to farinaceous or cottony, white to cream, sometimes yellow or with brownish tinge. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks

32,0$ a. (31) Some aerial hyphae distinctly thick-walled (up to 1.5 µm thick), 2-3.5 µm wide, skeletoid. Growth rate 20-35 mm on MEA, on ChA less.///Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide. Submerged hyphae 2-4(-7) µm wide, sometimes with swellings. On angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Domanski, 1969b. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (8), (9), (12), (13), (14), 17, (18), (19), 21, (22), 30, (31), (37), 39, 42, 44, (46), 48, (50), 52, 53, (54), 75, 80, 83, (88), 89, (94). $ Schizopora paradoxa (Schrad. ex Fr.) Donk 
32,0$ b. Aerial hyphae always thin-walled. Growth rate about 50 mm on both MEA and ChA. ///Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Africa. Ref.: van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: 1, (7), 12, (14), (15), 17, 21, 30, (37), 39, 42, (44), 52, 53, 75, 83, (89), (90). $ Polystictus subiculoides Lloyd

33,0$ a. (31) Colony becoming distinctly yellow. Hyphal strands typically present. Hyphae often ampullate at the septa. $ See Trechispora vaga under 60a
33,34$ b. Colony white to cream, rarely locally with brownish tinge. Hyphal strands typically absent. Hyphae in most species not ampullate at the septa

34,35$ a. (3 3) Hyphae with thickened to thick walls
34,36$ b. Hyphae thin- to firm-walled

35,0$ a. (34) Many aerial hyphae often irregular, with intercalary or terminal swellings, up to 18 µm wide. $ See Laeticorticium polygonioides under 69d
35,0$ b. Aerial hyphae regular (submerged hyphae may be irregular). Swellings never intercalary. $ See Schizopora paradoxa under 32a, and Hyphodontia cf pruni under 37a

36,0$ a. (34) Vesicles lateral, with a septum immediately below, thin- to somewhat thick-walled, often collapsed, up to 24 µm diam. Aerial hyphae irregular, with swellings and often with ampullate septa, thin-walled. Clamps irregular in shape. ///Growth rate 3-9 mm. Advancing zone raised, rarely [p. 121] appressed, hyphae dense to rather distant. Mat downy to cottony, sometimes farinaceous, white. Marginal hyphae (1.2-)1.5-3 µm wide, soon forming swellings up to 14 µm diam. Some hyphae with short irregular branches. Aerial hyphae sometimes covered with yellowish resinous material. North-temperate. /// Code: 1, (2), 3, (10), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, (18), 21, 30, (31), 39, 42, 44, (50), (51), 52, (57), 58), (61), 75, 78, 80, (82), 83. $ Trechispora mutabilis (Pers.) Liberta
36,37$ b. Vesicles lateral and terminal, with a septum at some distance, typically thin-walled, (sometimes slightly thick-walled). Aerial hyphae regular or rarely with swellings, sometimes constricted at the septa, thin- to firm-walled. Clamps hemispherical, but variable in size. ///Growth rate 3-12 (rarely up to 20) mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae dense to distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to farinaceous, rarely low cottony and later pellicular, white to cream. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide (in Hyphodontia cf pruni up to 6 µm wide). Diameter of clamps always less than that of the hyphae. (Hyphodontia)

37,0$ a. (36) On angiosperms, very rarely gymnosperms. Hyphae up to 6 µm wide, some with swellings (up to 8 µm diam). Vesicles up to 17 µm diam. Europe, Asia . ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (10), (11), 13, 17, 18, (26), 30, 39, 44, (48), 52, 53, 54, (57), (61), 75, (78), 80, 82, 83, 89. $ Hyphodontia cf. pruni (Lasch) J. Erikss. & Hjortstam
37,0$ b.* On angiosperms, very rarely gymnosperms. Hyphae up to 4.5(-5) µm wide. Vesicles up to 18(-26) µm diam. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), (10), 13, 14, (17), 18, (24), 30, 39, 44, 52, 53, 75, (80), (82), 83, (88), 89, 95, 97, 99. $ Hyphodontia sambuci (Pers. ex Pers.) J. Erikss.  †
37,0$ c.* Mainly on agiosperms, sometimes also gymnosperms. Hyphae up to 4(-5) µm wide, some with much-branched lateral hyphae and somewhat irregular. Vesicles up to 12 µm diam. Europe, Asia. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, (24), 30, †39, 44, (51), 52, 53, 61, 75, 80, (82), 83, (88), (89), (90), 95, 97, 99. [p. 122] $ Hyphodontia crustosa (Pers. ex Fr.) J. Erikss.  
37,0,n$ Boidin (1958a) mentions chlamydospores for tube cultures, but none of the isolates examined produced them on plates.
37,0$ d.* On angiosperms and gymnosperms. Aerial and submerged hyphae with swellings up to 10(-14) µm diam. Vesicles up to 12 µm diam. Europe, Asia Ref.: Boidin, 1958a (both species). ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), 13, (14), 17, (18), 30, 39, 44, 52, 53, 75, 80, 82, 83, (89), (90), 95, 97. $ Hyphodontia papillosa (Fr.) J. Erikss. / Hyphodontia subalutacea (P. Karst.) J. Erikss.
37,0$ e.* On gymnosperms. Aerial and submerged hyphae regular, only sometimes constricted at the septa. Vesicles up to 10(-12) µm wide. Europe, Asia. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a (both species). ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (16), 17, (18), (24), (26), 30, 39, 44, 52, 53, 75, (82), 83, 90, 95, 97, 99. $Hyphodontia alutacea (Fr.) J. Erikss. 
37,0$ e.* On gymnosperms. Aerial and submerged hyphae regular, only sometimes constricted at the septa. Vesicles up to 10(-12) µm wide. Europe, Asia. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a (both species). ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (16), 17, (18), 30, 39, 44, 52, 53, 75, (78), (82), 83, 90, 97, 99.  $Hyphodontia floccosa (Bourd. & Galz.) J. Erikss.

38,39$ a. (28) Blastoconidia and/or chlamydospores present
38,57$ b. Blastoconidia and chlamydospores absent

39,40$ a. (38) Typical blastoconidia present, not in chains. Chlamydospores present or absent
39,42$ b. Typical blastoconidia absent or in chains. Chlamydospores present

40,0$ a. (39) Chlamydospores present in submerged mycelium, ellipsoid, 9-15(-25 ) x 5-8(-10) µm. ///Growth rate 4-20 mm on MEA, on ChA faster. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first appressed or radiating cottony from the start, white to Pale Pinkish Buff or Light Buff, becoming low cottony to farinaceous, often somewhat velvety around the inoculum, Ochraceous Buff to Ochraceous Tawny. Reverse finally darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5.5 µm wide. Diameter of the clamp equal to or smaller than that of the hypha. Conidia hyaline to pale yellow, narrowly ellipsoid to ovoid, sometimes becoming subglobose after detachment. (3-)5-10(-12) x (3-)4-7(10) µm. Conidia single [p. 123] or more typically in dense botryose clusters, attached with a broad base, with thin to thickened walls. Aerial hyphae often covered with brownish crystals or sometimes with resinous material. Submerged hyphae up to 7 µm wide. On angiosperms, mainly Quercus. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942. ///Code: (1), 2, (3), (9), (10), 11, (12), (13), (14), (16), 18, 21, (22), (26), 30, 31, 34, (35), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 52, 53, 54, 57, 58, 82, 83, 85, 86, 89. $ Fistulina hepatica (Schaeff.) ex Fr.
40,41$ b. Chlamydospores absent
41,0$ a. (40) Colony becoming distinctly blue (Parula Blue, Delft Blue, Orient Blue, Tyrian Blue) when cultured in the light (when cultured in the dark yellowish brown, but turning blue within an hour in the light). ///Growth rate 15-30 mm on ChA (on MEA no growth). Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed to minutely downy, becoming farinaceous to velvety. Marginal hyphae 1-2(-2.5) µm wide, sometimes with short, much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide. Conidia terminal, attached with a broad base, hyaline to blue, ovoid, (7-)8.5-16(-21) x 4.5-7(-8) m, wall often roughened by encrustations. Europe, S.-America. Ref.: von Arx, 1973. ///Code: 1, 3, (4), (8), (9), 11, 13, 14, 17, 18, (26), 32, 39, (42), 45, (50), 51, 52, (57), 61, (82), 83, 86. $ Sporotrichum azureum Wright & von Arx
rem$ il n'y a pas de 41b ...

42,0$ a. (39) Cylindrical arthroconidia present, $ See  Bjerkandera adusta, key E under 9a (with notes)
42,43$ b. Arthroconidia absent or chlamydospore-like and separated by clamped septa

43,0$ a. (42) Some hyphae densely covered with spinulose projections. Lamellate basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. ///Growth rate 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, even to bayed or fan-shaped, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat cottony, locally,woolly, floccose-woolly or plumose, becoming felty, white. Odour absent to distinct. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5(-5.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae (1.5-)2-7(-8) µm wide, thin- to thick- walled. [p. 124] Chlamydospores often present, ellipsoid, (4-)5.5-15(-20) x 4-8 µm. On angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Davidson and Campbell, 1942; Nobles, 1948; van der Westhuizen, 1958. ///Code: (1), 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), (15), (19), 21, 22, (23), 25, 30, (36), (37), 39, (44), 45, 48, 52, 53, 54, (55), 59, (85), 88, 89. $ Schizophyllum commune Fr.
43,44$ b. Hyphae without spinulose projections

44,0$ a. (43) At least some chlamydospores brown and warted to blunt spiny., when young ellipsoid, 17-28 x 12-18 µm, becoming (sub)globose, 15-30 µm diam, walls up to 5 µm thick, spines up to 6.5 x 2.5 µm. $ See Ganoderma colossum, key B under 16b
44,45$ b. Chlamydospores smooth

45,0$ a. (44) Colony at first blueish grey (Light Gull Gray to Gull Gray), becoming vinaceous (Pale Vinaceous Drab and finally Dark Vinaceous Brown to Hays Brown). Chlamydospores (arthroconidia) in chains. ///Growth rate 40-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first cottony to cottony-farinaceous, becoming farinaceous to farinaceous-woolly. Reverse darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide, often encrusted. Chlamydospores (arthroconidia) hyaline to reddish brown, subglobose, ellipsoid or ovoid, 4-8(10) x 3-5(-6) µm, sometimes covered with purplish material and then appearing roughened. (Sub)tropical. Ref.: Fidalgo, 1958; von Arx, 1973; Sen, 1973. ///Code: 1, 3, (4), (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, 18, 21, 22, (30), 33, 38, 39, 42, 45, 52, 53, (54), 57, (67), 82, 83, (85), 86, (93). $ Punctularia atropurpurascens (Berk. & Br.) Petch /  Imperf. . st.: Ptychogaster rubescens Boud.
 45,0,n$ Sen (1973), describes Pubescens as the imperfect state of Merulius tremellosus, but this is certainly erroneous
45,46$ b. Colony never dark vinaceous. Chlamydospores typically not in chains

46,47$ a. (45) Hyphae with encrusted, often contorted tips present. Advancing zone appressed, rarely raised, hyphae distant. [p. 125] Mat at first downy to farinaceous, white to cream. Reverse unchanged or rarely bleached. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. (Tyromyces)
46,48$ b. Hyphae with encrusted contorted tips absent.

47,0$ a. (46) Chlamydospores becoming thick-walled, (8-)11-21 x 6-14(-17) µm. Mat becoming (sub)felty with typically foliose basidiocarps (also poroid basidiocarps may be present) with cystidia in the hymenium. ///Growth rate 20-45 mm. Marginal hyphae (2-)2.5-4.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae up to 6 µm wide. Mainly on gymnosperms, but also angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; K‰‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Siepmann and Zycha, 1968. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), 14, 17, 18, (21), (22), 24, (25), 30, 31, (37), 39, 42, (44), 45, 52, 53, (54), 56, 82, 83, 85, 88, (89), 90, (94). $ Tyromyces borealis (Fr.) Imaz.  
47,0,n$ Hyphae with irregularly thickened walls as described by Nobles (1965) were not observed.
47,0$ b. Chlamydospores thin- to firm-walled, (5.5-)6.5-11(-15) x (3.5-)4.5-7(-9) µm. Basidiocarps appear as cottony-felty mycelial balls which become poroid; hymenium without cystidia. ///Growth rate 25-65 mm. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide. Submerged hyphae in some isolates up to 10 µm wide. On angiosperms and gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, 19, (21), (25), 30, (31), (37), 39, 42, (44), 45, 52, 53, (54), (55), 56, 82, 83, 85, (88), (89), (90). $ Tyromyces tephroleucus (Fr.) Donk / Tyromyces chioneus (Fr. ex Fr.) P. Karst.  / Syn.: Tyromyces albellus (Peck) Bond. & Sing.  
47,0,n$ The only difference observed between isolates of T. chioneus and T. tephroleucus is the growth rate, which is faster in T. chioneus (about 50 mm) than in T. tephroleucus (about 30 mm). Since the differences between these and related species have only recently been elucidated (Jahn, 1973), erroneous identification of the available isolates cannot be excluded.

48,49$ a. (46) Thin-walled hyphae present
48,53$ b. Thick-walled hyphae absent.

49,50$ a. (48) Growth rate not exceeding 20 mm (on both ChA and MEA)
49,51$ b. Growth rate at least 25 mm (on ChA at least 40 mm)

50,57$ a. (49) True chlamydospores absent, but swellings which may resemble chlamydospores present (Some species of Aleurodiscus or Laeticorticium)
50,0$ b. True chlamydospores present, ellipsoid, becoming thick-walled, (8-)10-15 x 6-10 µm or subglobose, 10-14 µm diam. ///Growth rate 10-16 mm. Advancing zone appressed or submerged, slightly bayed, hyphae dense. Mat thinly cottony to locally plumose, becoming cottony-woolly or locally forming ceraceous, thickened spots which may develop into poroid basidiocarps. Colony white to cream. Odour somewhat sweet. Marginal hyphae 2-4.5 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 2.5-5.5 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, the widest hyphae with relatively small clamps. On angiosperms, mainly Quercus. Europe, Asia. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (15), 21, 22, 23, 30, 31, (36), 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, 52, 53, 54, (80), 83, 85, 88, 89. $ Spongipellis litschaueri Lohwag

51,0$ a. (49) Chlamydospores 4.5-8 x 4-6 µm. ///Growth rate about 50 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, even to slightly bayed. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, becoming farinaceous to felty or floccose-felty, white. Hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, some aerial ones with thickened walls. Tropical Asia. Ref.: Bakshi et al., 1970. ///Code: 1, (7), (8), (12), (13), (14), (15), 18, 19, 21, 22, 24, (25), 30, 39, (42), (44), (45), 48, 52, 53, 85, 89. $ Spongipellis stramineus Pat.
51,52$ b. Chlamydospores larger

52,0$ a. (51) Chlamydospores globose to broadly ellipsoid, (5-)9-15 µm diam or 10-21 x 9-15 m, not abundant. $ See Grifola frondosa, key A under 29a
52,0$ b. Chlamydospores globose to subglobose, 6-14 µm diam, very abundant in the aerial mycelium. $ See Abortiporus biennis, key B under 59a

53,57$ a. (48) True chlamydospores absent, but many hyphal [p. 127] swellings present, some of which may resemble chlamydospores. Reaction on laccase strong (some species of Aleurodiscus or Laeticorticium)
53,54$ b. True chlamydospores present. Reaction on laccase weak

54,55$ a. Chlamydospores becoming very thick-walled (up to 1.5-2 µm), globose to subglobose. ///Advancing zone appressed to raised, bayed, rarely even, hyphae distant. Mat at first cottony to cottony-woolly or floccose, sometimes plumose, white, becoming farinaceous to felty, cream .
54,56$ b. Chlamydospores with thin to thickened walls (up to 1 µm), ellipsoid to ovoid (sometimes (sub)globose in Tyromyces fumidiceps)

55,0$ a. (54) Chlamydospores 6-11 x (5-)6-9 µm, hyaline ///Growth rate 20-55 mm. Basidiocarps sometimes formed within 6 weeks. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled; some lateral hyphae densely branched. On angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles and Nordin, 1955; Boidin, 1958. ///Code: (1), (2), (3), (7), (8), (9), (11), 12, 14, (15), 18, (19), 21, 22, (23), (25), 30, 31, (38), 39, (42), 44, 45, 50, 52, 53, 54, (61), (80), 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $ Hypochnicium vellereum (Ell. & Cragin) Parm.
55,0$ b. Chlamydospores 11-13(-20) µm diam, yellowish. ///Growth rate about 20 mm. Hyphal strands often present. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae 2-4(-9.5) µm wide, often with swellings, sometimes forming hyphal knots. Aerial hyphae 2.5-5.2 µm wide. On angiosperms and gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Ginns, 1976. ///Code: (1), (9), 13, 14, (15), 18, (19), 21, (22), (23), 24, (25), 30, 31, 39, 42, 45, 52, 53, 54, (55), (78), 80, (81), 85, (88), (89), (90). $ Ceraceomyces borealis (Romell) J. Erikss. & Ryv.

56,0$ a. (54) Chlamydospores globose, 11-15 µm diam to ellipsoid, 12-20 x 9-14 µm. ///Growth rate 12-25 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat downy to thinly woolly or cottony-woolly, becoming densely woolly to floccose, white. Basidiocarps present. Odour often distinct. [p. 128] Marginal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, some hyphae with short, much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide, sometimes covered with small crystals. On angiosperms. N.-America. ///Code: 1, (3), (8), (9), (11), 13, 14, (17), (19), (21), 22, (24), 30, (36), 39, 42, 44, 45, 52, 53, 61, (80), 82, 83, 85, (88), 89. $ Tyromyces fumidiceps Atk.
56,0$ b. Chlamydospores ellipsoid to rarely ovoid, 7.5-16 x 4.5-10 µm. ///Growth rate 40-60 mm. Advancing zone typically raised, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat at first downy to thinly cottony, rarely somewhat farinaceous, becoming (sub)felty. Basidiocarps often formed. Odour absent to distinct. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-7 µm wide, sometimes with swellings up to 12(-15) µm diam. On angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: (1), (2), 3, (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), (14), (17), (18), 21, (24), (25), 30, (31), (36), 39, 42, (44), 45, 52, 53, 54, 80, 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $ Bjerkandera fumosa (Pers. ex Fr.) P. Karst.  
56,0,n$ Polyporus anthracophilus Cooke, CBS 324.49, is indistinguishable from Bjerkandera fumosa.

57,58$ a. (38, 50, 53) Growth rate not exceeding 25 mm
57,77$ b. Growth rate at least 25 mm

58,59$ a. (57) Colony at least locally distinctly yellowish or ochraceous
58,63$ b. Colony remaining white to cream, sometimes with local pinkish tinges

59,60$ a. (58) Hyphal strands present. Growth on ChA faster than on MEA
59,61$ b. Hyphal strands absent. Growth similar on ChA and MEA

60,0$ a. (59) Ampullate septa present. At least in the submerged mycelium hyphae with distinct swellings (up to 15 µm diam) present. Tyrosinase present. ///Growth rate 9-25 mm on ChA (on MEA no or very little growth). Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae dense to rather distant. Mat appressed to downy, becoming farinaceous, white at first, but soon [p. 129] Citron Yellow to Sulphurin Yellow becoming subfelty and Deep Colonial Buff. Reverse unchanged to darker. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, with thin to firm walls. Aerial hyphae often encrusted with yellowish crystals. Hyphal strands composed of firm-walled hyphae, up to 12 µm wide, some septa lacking clamps. On angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (9), (10), 11, (12), (13), (14), (15), 17, 18, (21), (24), 30, 31, 35, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, (54), (55), 57, (75), 78, 80, 81, 82, 83, (89), (90), 95, 97. $ Trechispora vaga (Fr.) Liberta / Syn.: Trechispora sulphurea (Pers. ex Fr.) Liberta
60,0$ b. Ampullate septa and swellings absent (but hyphae may be surrounded by material which swells in lactic acid). Tyrosinase absent. ///Growth rate 15-35 mm (on ChA up to 42 mm). Advancing zone raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first cottony-woolly to woolly, often with erect fascicles, becoming felty, cream to ochraceous. Reverse unchanged. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, some irregular in outline with many short or knob-like branches. Some hyphae with firm walls, some encrusted with yellowish crystals. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. /// Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), 11, 12, (14), (21), 22, (23), (25), 31, 35, 39, 42, 44, 45, 52, 53, 57, 81, 82, 83, 90, 95, 97. $ Amphinema byssoides (Pers. ex Fr.) J. Erikss.

61,0$ a. (59) Aerial hyphae often irregular in outline and ampullate at the septa.///Growth rate 3-6 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat at first farinaceous, white, then low fasciculate to warted or granular, becoming ochraceous. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide, swellings up to 6 µm diam. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (10), (11), 13, 18, (21), (23), (24), 30, 31, 35, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 52, 53, (54), (57), 78, 80, 82, 83, (88), (89), (90), 95, 97. $ Trechispora farinacea (Pers. ex Fr.) Liberta
61,62$ b. Aerial hyphae not ampullate at the septa

62,0$ a (61) Growth rate 15-25 mm on MEA (on ChA up to 35 mm). Aerial hyphae up to 5 µm wide. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat downy to short woolly, at first whitish, becoming ochraceous. Reverse slightly darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide. Aerial hyphae sometimes encrusted. Europe, North and tropical America. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), 11, 13, 14, 17, 22, (30), 31, 35, (38), 39, (42), 45, 52, 53, (57), 82, 83, (89), (90), 95, 97. $ Trechispora amianthina (Bourd. & Galz.) Liberta 
62,0$ b Growth rate 5-9 mm on both MEA and ChA. Aerial hyphae up to 3 µm wide. Advancing zone appressed to submerged, hyphae dense. Mat at first appressed to downy, becoming low cottony to cottony-woolly or pellicular, cream to pale ochraceous. Reverse typically darker. Marginal hyphae 1.2-2(-2.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae sometimes forming small strands. On angiosperms. Europe. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (10), (11), 13, 17, 21, 22, (24), 30, 31, 35, 38, 39, 42, (44), 45, 51, 52, (53), 57, 58, 82, 83, 89, 95, 97. $ Athelopsis baculifera (Bourd. & Galz.) J¸lich

63,0$ a. (57) Arthroconidia present. ///Growth rate 15-25 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant or rather distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming cottony-woolly to plumose. Reverse unchanged to slightly darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-2.5 µm wide, sometimes undulating. Aerial hyphae up to 3.5 µm wide, sometimes irregular in outline. Large plate-like crystals present between the aerial hyphae. (Sub)tropical. ///Code: (1), 2, 3, (9), (11), 13, 14, (16), 21, 22, (23), (24), 30, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 52, 53, 82, 83, 84, (93). $ Punctularia strigosozonata (Schw.) Talbot
63,64$ b. Arthroconidia absent

64,0$ a. (63) Mat farinaceous to velvety, mainly consisting of erect aerial hyphae with encrusted apical part, which may be contorted. $ See Incrustoporia nivea, key B under 90a
64,65$ b. Hyphae with encrusted apical parts absent 

65,0$ a. Toruloid terminal structures with up to 18 elements. (fig. 3g) present, up to 60 x 6 µm. ///Growth rate 15-20 mm (on ChA often less). Advancing zone raised, hyphae dense. Mat cottony to appressed silky, sometimes becoming pellicular, white. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1-4(-5)† µm wide. Toruloid structures often with thickened walls, particularly at the base. Submerged hyphae with swellings up to 10 µm diam. Some hyphae with narrow, much-branched lateral hyphae. Mainly on angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Nobles, 1967. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), 12, 20, 21, (24), 30, (31), 39, 42, 44, (45), (50), 51, 52, 53, (54), (57), 61, (72), (79), 80, 82, 83, 89, (93), 95, 97, 99. $ Basidioradulum radulum (Fr. ex Fr.) Nobles
65,66$ b. Toruloid structures absent

66,67$ a. (65) Hyphal swellings present, at least in the submerged hyphae
66,70$ b. Hyphal swellings absent

67,0$ a. (66) Ampullate septa present. Large crystals absent between the aerial hyphae. $ See Trechispora farinacea under 61a
67,68$ b. Ampullate septa absent. Large cubic crystals typically present between the aerial hyphae

68,0$ a. (67) On gymnosperms. ///Growth rate 2-5 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae dense. Mat at first low cottony or cottony-woolly, becoming subfelty to farinaceous, white to cream, sometimes with pinkish tinge. Reverse unchanged to darker. Marginal hyphae 2.5-4 µm wide, some with short knob-like projections or narrow, much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae 2-6 µm wide, with thin to thickened walls, often irregular, sometimes with yellowish contents. N.-Africa. Ref.†: Boidin et al., 1968. ///Code†: 1, 3, (10), 12, (15), 18, 21, (22), (24), 30, 31, (33), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), (50), 52, 53, 54, (60), 61, (80), 82, 83, 90, (94), 95, 98, (99), (100). $ Aleurodiscus atlanticus Maire  
68,0,n$ Boidin et al. (1968) also described A. penicillatus Burt which has similar cultural characters, known only from N.America and growing mainly on Pseudotsuga, whilst A. atlanticus is only known from Cedrus. [p. 132]
68,69$ b. On angiosperms (Cf. also Radulomyces molaris under 98a, which has local swellings)

69,0$ a. (68) Growth rate 8-30 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant to rather distant. Mat at first appressed to farinaceous, becoming densely farinaceous to pellicular, white to cream. Reverse at first bleached, later sometimes becoming darker. Hyphae 1-3.5(-4) µm wide with short, small clamps (Hyphodontia-type). Some hyphae very irregular with swellings and knob-like projections. N.-America, Australia. Ref.: Refshauge and Proctor, 1935. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, (24), 30, 31, 37, (38), 39, (42), 44, 51, 52, (53), 80, 82, 83, 89. $ Dictyopanus rhipidium (Berk.) Pat.
69,0$ b.* Growth rate 5-12 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first appressed to downy or farinaceous, becoming subfelty, whitish to Pinkish Buff or Cinnamon Buff. Reverse finally darker. Marginal hyphae 1-2 µm wide. Aerial hyphae hyaline to brownish, 1.5-5 µm wide, with thin to thick walls, often with swellings. Submerged hyphae irregular, some much-branched. Mainly (sub)tropical. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, 24, 30, 31, (35), (38), 39, 42, 45, (46), 48, 51, 52, 53, (61), (64), 67, 80, 82, 83, 89, (94). $ Lopharia cinerascens (Schw.) G. H. Cunn.
69,0$ c.* Growth rate 5-12 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat appressed to downy or farinaceous, around the inoculum velvety, white. Reverse unchanged. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-4 µm wide with thin to rarely thickened walls. Submerged hyphae with swellings. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, 18, (26), 30, 39, 42, 45, (48), 52, 53, 80, 82, 83, 89, (92), 95, 98. $ Gloeocystidiellum leucoxanthum (Bres.) Boidin 
69,0$ d.* Growth rate 8-20 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather dense. Mat at first downy to farinaceous, remaining so or becoming low cottony or cottony-woolly, white to cream. Reverse unchanged or slightly darker under the inoculum. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-4(-5.5) µm wide. Some aerial hyphae distinctly thick-walled, often with terminal or intercalary swellings. Submerged [p. 133] hyphae often irregular in outline, some with narrow much-branched lateral hyphae. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Boidin et al., 1968. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, (21), (22), (24), 30, 31, (38), 39, 42, (44), 45, 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, (54), (58), (61), (75), 80, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 98, 100. $ Laeticorticium polygonioides (P. Karst.) Donk

70,71$ a. (66) All or nearly all aerial hyphae with short, small clamps at the septa. Hyphae thin- to firm-walled
70,73$ b. Short, small clamps absent or only occasionally formed and then only on the widest hyphae

71,0$ a. (70) Marginal hyphae 2.5-6 µm wide. Submerged hyphae irregular, often constricted at the septa, up to 8(-9.5) µm wide. Growth rate at least 20 mm. $ See Hypochnicium lundellii, under 100b
