77,78$ a. (57) Mat becoming pink or blue
77,79$ b. Mat not pink or blue, rarely locally pinkish

78,0$ a. (77)) Mat becoming distinctly pink (Shrimp Pink to Saffrano Pink, rarely La France Pink). ///Plate covered in 1-2 weeks (growth on ChA slower). Advancing zone appressed, even to bayed, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming woolly or cottony-woolly to plumose, locally lacunose and finally floccose to felty. Hyphal strands often present. Reverse pink. Odour indistinct or suggesting ëpermanent waveí. Marginal hyphae thin- to firm-walled, 1-4.5(-6) µm wide, septa with short, small clamps (Hyphodontia-type). Some hyphae tortuous. Some wide hyphae with hyaline resinous content. Aerial hyphae 1-6(-7) µm wide, with thin to thickened walls, some with short spiny projections. Arthroconidia may be present, 3.5-5 µm wide. Submerged hyphae often with swellings. Wall sometimes apparently [p. 136] double. On grasses and organic material. Cosmopolitan. ///Code: 1, 3, (5), (6), 13, 14, (15), (19), 21, 22, 23, (25), (31), 33, (36), (38), 39, 44, (45), 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, 59, 60, 80, 81, 83, (84), (89), (91). $ Corticium fuciforme (Berk.) Wakef.
78,0$ b. Mat becoming distinctly blue (Light Windsor Blue, Clear Windsor Blue, Dark Aniline Blue, Urania Blue or Dusky Slate Blue). ///Growth rate 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming thinly cottony, finally densely woolly to velvety. Colony turning greenish in KOH. Reverse bleached. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-5.5(-7) µm wide, some with narrow, much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled, often encrusted with blueish crystals, 1.5-4 µm wide, rarely with swellings up to 6 µm diam. Submerged hyphae 1.5-4(-6.5) µm wide, sometimes with swellings, often with small clamps. On angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, 4, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (18), 21, 22, (24), 26, 30, 33, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), 52, 53, 54, 57, 61, 80, 82, 83, 89, (93), 95, 98. $ Pulcherricium caeruleum (Schrad. ex Fr.) Parm.     

79,0$ a. (77) Arthroconidia present. $ See Punctularia strigosozonata under 63a and Bjerkandera adusta, key E under 9a. If the arthroconidia become ellipsoid and the colony is vinaceous, cf. Punctularia atropurpurascens under 45a
79,80$ b. Arthroconidia absent

80,0$ a. (79) Hyphae covered with numerous spinulose projections. Lamellate basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. $ See Schizophyllum commune under 43a
80,81$ b. Hyphae without spinulose projections

81,0$ a. (80) Sclerotia present, white, globose, loosely attached; consisting of thin-walled, more or less globose cells, 8-15 µm diam, with clamps at the septa. ///Growth rate 40-55 mm (on ChA 20-30 mm): Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant or rather distant. Mat at first appressed or directly downy to low woolly, remaining so, or becoming cottony-woolly, white. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-6(-7.5) µm wide, some [p. 137] with narrow, much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae sometimes firm-walled, some with small clamps. Submerged hyphae often with granular contents, sometimes constricted at the septa, with clamps of variable size. On angiosperms. North-temperate. ///Code: 1, 3, (7), (12), (13), (14), 17, (21), 22, 30, (37), 39, 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, 61, (66), 78, (80), 89, (94). $ Bulbillomyces farinosus (Bres.) J¸lich
81,82$ b. Sclerotia absent

82,0$ a. (81) Mat lacunose with felty spots and distinct ridges, the remaining parts thinly cottony-woolly, white. ///Growth rate 30-50 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Reverse often veined, bleached. Mat at first somewhat downy, somtimes locally becoming crustose, breaking under pressure. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1.3-)1.5-4(-5.5) µm wide, some with short, much-branched lateral hyphae. Aerial hyphae with thin to sometimes thickened walls. Crustose areas consisting of irregularly thick-walled hyphae with swellings and short projections, sometimes plectenchymatous. On angiosperms, rarely gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: (1), (2), 3, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21, 22, (24), 25, 27, (28), 30, (31), 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), (50), (51), 52, 53, (54), 61, (64), 80, (82), 83, 89, (90), 95, 97, 99. $ Radulomyces confluens (Fr.) M. P. Christ. / Radulomyces rickii (Bres.) M. P. Christ.
82,83$ b. Mat not lacunose, ridges absent

83,84$ a. (82) Much-branched short lateral hyphae (often somewhat dendritical) present, especially in the marginal region and/or mat locally becoming brownish, especially near the edge of the Petri dish. Tyrosinase typically present. (Peniophora)
83,92$ b. Short much-branched hyphae and brownish spots absent. Tyrosinase present or absent

84,85$ a. (83) On gymnosperms
84,86$ b. On angiosperms

85,0$ a. (84) On Juniperus. Mat after 2 weeks nearly completely appressed, subsequently developing with radiating zones of downy to farinaceous mycelium. [p. 138]///Growth rate 45-60 mm. Advancing zone appressed to submerged, hyphae rather distant. Reverse darker. Hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Europe. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a.///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, 18, 30, 31, 34, 38, 39, 42, 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, (61), 83, 90, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora junipericola J. Erikss.
85,27$ b. On other gymnosperms (Pinus, Picea, Pseudotsuga). Mat becoming cottony, cottony-woolly or floccose-woolly

86,87$ a. (84) Mat appressed to downy or farinaceous. ///Growth rate 50-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant to rather distant. Reverse unchanged to darker. Hyphae 1.5-5(-6.5) µm wide. ( $ See also Peniophora lycii under 89h).
86,88$ b. Mat becoming cottony, cottony-woolly or floccose-woolly

87,0$ a. (86) On Carpinus. Submerged hyphae often with swellings. Aerial hyphae typically smooth-walled. Europe Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), 13, (14), 17, (18), 30, (31), (38), 39, 42, (44), 45, (50), 52, 53, (54), 60, 80, 83, (88), 89, 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora laeta (Fr.) Donk  / Syn.: Peniophora hydnoidea (Pers. ex Fr.) Donk
87,0†$ b. On Buxus. Submerged hyphae more or less regular, without distinct swellings. Aerial hyphae sometimes encrusted. Europe, Asia. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 30, 31, (34), 39, 42, (44), 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, 57, 60, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $Peniophora proxima Bres.

88,89$ a. (86) Mat becoming locally brownish within 4(-6) weeks. Thick-walled hyphae typically present. ///Growth rate 50->70 mm. Advancing zone typically narrowly appressed, but sometimes raised, hyphae distant. Mat at first downy or from the beginning cottony to cottony-woolly or floccose to floccosewoolly, sometimes plumose. Brown drops of exudate often present after 3-4 weeks. Reverse at first unchanged [p. 139] to bleached, often becoming darker after 3-4 weeks. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5(-7) µm wide. Spirally coiled hyphae sometimes present.
88,90$ b. Mat remaining white to cream, thick-walled hyphae absent

89,0$ a. (88) On Populus. Thick-walled hyphae abundant after 4 weeks. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5(-6) µm wide. $ See Peniophora polygonia, under 26a
89,0$ b.* On Tilia. Aerial hyphae up to 4.5 µm wide, with thin to thickened walls. Some hyphae yellowish brown. Submerged hyphae with swellings. Europe, Asia ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 19, 21, 22, (24), 30, 31, 34, 38, 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), 52, 53, (57), 61, (67), 80, 83, 89, (94). $ Peniophora rufomarginata (Pers.) Litsch.
89,0$ c.* Mainly on Populus and Salix. Mat after 2 weeks downy to cottony, sometimes locally cottony-woolly to plumose, later often subfelty. Aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-4(-5) µm wide, some containing oil drops. Submerged hyphae often with swellings. North-temperate Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21, (22), (23), 24, 30, 31, 34, 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), (51), 52, 53, (57), 60, 61, (67), (78), 80, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora violaceolivida (Sommerf.) Massee
89,0$ d.* †On various angiosperms. Mat after 2 weeks cottony to cottony-woolly, later somewhat floccose or locally subfelty and farinaceous. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. Submerged hyphae up to 6.5(-8) µm wide. North-temperate Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; van der Westhuizen, 1972. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), (17), (18), (19), 21, 22, (24), 30, (31), 34, (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), (50), (51), 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), 61, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora cinerea (Fr.) Cooke
89,0$ e.* On various angiosperms. Mat after 2 weeks cottony, becoming floccose to plumose, often forming cottony-woolly mycelial balls. Aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-5(-6) µm wide, some suggesting skeletals, branched. Submerged hyphae up to 6 µm wide. North-temperate Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 19, 21, 22, 23, 30, 31, 34, (37), (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), (51), 52, †53, 54, (58), 61, (67), (82), 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. [p. 140] $ Peniophora quercina (Pers. ex Fr.) Cooke
89,0$ f.* On various angiosperms. Mat after 2 weeks downy to cottony, becoming plumose. After 6 weeks dark brown hyphal strands are present, overgrowing the white farinaceous mycelium. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5.5 µm wide, some suggesting skeletals, branched, others encrusted with small crystals. (Sub)tropical. Ref.: Boidin and Ahmad, 1963. ///Code: 1, (2), (6), (7), 12, (14), 17, 18, 21, (22), 23, (24), 30, 31, 34, 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, 52, 53, 54, 57, (61), 67, 81, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora tristicula (Pat.) Boidin
89,0$ g.* On various angiosperms. Mat after 2 weeks downy to thinly cottony, locally floccose or plumose. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4(-5) µm wide, sometimes irregular. Submerged hyphae sometimes with vesicles. North-temperate. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 19, 21, (22), (23), (24), 30, 31, 35, 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), (50), 52,, 53, (57), 61, (67), 80, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora nuda (Fr.) Bres.
89,0$ h.* On various angiosperms. Mat after 2 weeks appressed to downy or cottony, sometimes becoming subfelty and farinaceous. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5(-6.5) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, often with vesicles. Submerged hyphae often with vesicles. Europe, Asia. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, (18), 21, (22), 24, (25), 30, 31, 34, (38), 39, 42, 44, 45, 48, (50), (51), 52, 53, 54, (57), (58), (60), 61, 80, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 97, 99. $ Peniophora lycii (Pers.) Hˆhn. & Litsch.

90,0$ a. (88) Tyrosinase absent. $ See Bulbillomyces farinosus, under 81a
90,91$ b. Tyrosinase present. ///Growth rate 55->70 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae distant or rather distant. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5.5(-7) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4(-7) µm wide, some encrusted. Submerged hyphae up to 7 µm wide.

91,0$ a †(90) On Alnus. Mat downy to cottony, sometimes cottony-woolly, becoming subfelty and farinaceous, white. North-temperate. ///Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. [p. 141] ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), 12, (14), 17, (18), 21, (22), 24, 30, (37), 39, 42, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, 57, 61, 82, 83, 89, 5, 98, 99. $ Peniophora aurantiaca (Bres.) Hˆhn. & Litsch.
91,0$ b.* On various angiosperms. Mat at first downy or immediately cottony to cottony-woolly, sometimes plumose, becoming subfelty. North-temperate Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 12, (14), (17), 21, (22), (23), 24, 30, 37, 39, 42, 44, 45, (50), 52, 53, 54, 57, 61, 82, 83, 89, (94), 95, 98, 99. $ Peniophora incarnata (Pers. ex Fr.) P. Karst.
91,0$ c.* On various angiosperms. Mat cottony to plumose, becoming subfelty. Europe, Asia ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 12, (14), 21, (23), 24, 30, (31), 37, 39, 42, (44), 45, 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, 57, 61, 82, 83, 89. $ Peniophora pseudoversicolor Boidin

92,0$ a. (83) Colony distinctly yellowish. Hyphal strands present. $ See Amphinema byssoides, under 60b
92,93$ b. Colony white to cream, rarely with pinkish shade. Hyphal strands absent

93,94$ a. (92) Many aerial hyphae less than 1.5 µm wide. Growth rate 35-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant to sometimes distant. Marginal hyphae (1-)2-4(-6) µm wide. Reverse unchanged to bleached. Aerial hyphae 0.5-4(-6.5) µm wide, some may be encrusted. 
