4,0$ a. (3) Conidia originating singly, laterally on small denticles, cylindrical to allantoid, 7-13.5 x 2.5-4 µm ///Growth rate 25-40 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to farinaceous, whitish. Marginal hyphae 1.5-3.5(-4) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1-2.5(-3.5) µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. North-temperate. Ref.: Lyman, 1907; Nobles, 1937; Bolding, 1958a. ///Code: (8), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, 18, (30), 39, 42, 44, 45, 51, 52, 53, 83, 86, (93), 95. $ Galzinia incrustans (Hˆhn. & Litsch.) Parm. 
4,0$ b. Conidia originating in terminal or lateral sympodial clusters, ovoid, 4-8(-9) x 2-4(-4.5) µm. ///Growth rate 22-35 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even, hyphae rather distant. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, becoming farinaceous. Sclerotia often present, brown. Reverse unchanged. Marginal hyphae 1.5-4 µm wide. Diameter of the clamps typically smaller than that of the hyphae. Aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5 µm wide. Conidiophores hyaline, thin-walled, 2-4 µm wide. Sclerotia, when present, consisting of thick-walled brown pseudo-parenchymatous cells. Submerged hyphae often with swellings and ampullate septa. Arthroconidia often present. North-temperate. Ref.: Maxwell, 1954. ///Code: 3, (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), (14), (17), 18, (20), 21, (22), (24), 30, 39, 42, 44, (45), 52, 53, (66), 78, (80), (82), 83, (84), 86, 87. $ Sistotrema raduloides (P. Karst.) Donk 

5,0$ a. (3) Conidia subcylindrical or truncate-ovoid, with thin- to thickened walls, terminal, often in clusters from sprouting clamps, (4.5-)5.5-7.5 x (2.5-)3-5 µm. ///Growth rate 15-3 5 mm. Advancing zone appressed to submerged, hyphae rather distant. Mat downy to farinaceous, becoming cottony to subfelty, sometimes plumose, at first white, becoming Light Cinnamon Drab to Avellaneous, sometimes Wood Brown. Reverse unchanged or darker. Marginal hyphae and aerial hyphae 2-6 µm wide. Submerged hyphae up to 8 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. On gymnosperms, rarely angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: 2, (8), (9), (11), (13), (14), 17, 18, (19), 21, 23, (24), 30, (31), 33, (34), (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), 83, 86, (89), 90. [p. 146] $ Tyromyces guttulatus (Peck) Murr. 
5,0$ b. Conidia subglobose to usually ellipsoid, becoming thick-walled, lateral, rarely in small clusters, often with a clamp at the base, 7.5-15(-25) x 5-10(-15) µm. ///Growth rate 25-40 mm. Advancing zone raised, hyphae rather distant. Mat thinly cottony, becoming woolly to floccose, at some places farinaceous, white to Light Ochraceous Buff or Light Pinkish Cinnamon. Reverse unchanged. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5(-6) µm wide, sometimes forming hyphal knots. Aerial hyphae 2-6(-7.5) µm wide, thin- to sometimes thick-walled, hyaline to brownish. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Domanski, 1972. ///Code: (1), 2, (8), (11), 12, (14), 18, (19), 21, 22, 30, 31, (34), (35), 39, 42, (44), 45, (48), 52, 53, 54, 65, (67), 83, (85), (86), 90, (94). $ Amylocystis lapponicus (Romell) Bond. & Sing. 

6,0$ a. (2) Thick-walled much-branched aseptate hyphae present, 2.5-6 µm wide. ///Growth rate 12-35 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant. Mat downy to cottony-woolly, becoming floccose to felty or velvety, white, sometimes with pale yellowish tinges. Reverse unchanged. Odour absent or suggestive of apples on MEA, on ChA suggestive of cinnamon. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-4.5(-6) µm wide. Conidia borne singly on narrow hyphae (1-1.5 µm wide), ovoid to ellipsoid, 6.5-9 x 4.5-7 µm. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 8-18 x 7-12 µm, thick-walled. On gymnosperms. N.-America. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Lombard and Gilbertson, 1965. ///Code: (2), (3), (8), (9), (11), 13, 14, 17, 19, (21), 22, (24), 25, (26), 30, (31), (35), (36), 39, 42, 45, 48, (51), 52, 53, 54, 83, 85, 86, 90. $ Poria carbonica Overh. 
6,0$ b. Thick-walled much-branched hyphae absent. ///Growth rate 2-8 mm. Advancing zone appressed, rarely raised, even to seldom bayed, hyphae distant. Mat downy to cottony or cottony-woolly, rarely velvety, sometimes becoming farinaceous, white. Reverse unchanged to yellowish after 6 weeks. Odour absent or fruity. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2.5-6.5 µm wide. Conidia borne apically or laterally on narrow (1-1.5 µm wide) [p. 147] hyphae, subglobose to ovoid or ellipsoid, 4.5-9(-12) x 3-5(-7.5) µm, with thin or slightly thickened walls. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 8-19 x 4.5-12(-16) µm, thick-walled. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Faull, 1916; Nobles, 1948; Cartwright and Findlay, 1958. ///Code: (10), (11), (12), 13, 14, (15), 17, (18), 21, 22, (24), (26), 30, (36), (38), 39, 42, 45, (51), 52, 53, 54, (75), (80), 83, 85, 86, 90. $ Laricifomes officinale (Vill. ex Fr.) Kotlaba & Pouzar 

7,8$ a. (1) Chlamydospores present
7,36$ b. Chlamydospores absent

8,9$ a. (7) Arthroconidia present, cylindrical
8,10$ b. Arthroconidia absent

9,0$ a. (8) Growth rate 65->70 mm. On angiosperms, rarely Picea. ///Advancing zone raised, hyphae distant. Mat cottony-woolly, sometimes floccose, white, becoming felty and cream, rarely Pale Ochraceous Buff. Reverse bleached. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-5(-6) µm wide, sometimes with narrow, much-branched lateral branches. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 5-10(-13) x 4-7(-9) µm, thick-walled, sometimes lacking. Arthroconidia 2-3.5 µm wide, arising from hyphae with or without clamps at the septa. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Bose, 1930; Cartwright, 1931; Nobles, 1948; Bakshi et al., 1969; van der Westhuizen, 1971. ///Code: (1), 3, (6), (7), (11), 12, 14, (19), 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, 37, 39, 42, 45, (50), 52, 53, (54), (61), (83), 84, (85), (88), 89, (90), (93). $ Bjerkandera adusta (Willd. ex Fr.) P. Karst.  
9,0,n$ Van der Westhuizen (1971) reports the occurrence of thickwalled coloured hyphae. These were not found in our cultures and probably occur only in basidiocarps or basidiocarpinitials, which are also reported by van der Westhuizen. Consequently, this character has not been included in the code. 
9,0$ b. Growth rate not exceeding 50 mm. On gymnosperms. $ See Gloeophyllum, key A under 3 

10,11$ a. (8) Thick-walled hyphae present
10,19$ b. Thick-walled hyphae absent

11,0$ a. (10) Some hyphae covered with numerous spinulose [p. 148] projections. Basidiocarps typically formed within 6 weeks. On angiosperms. $ See Schizophyllum commune, key D under 43a 
11,12$ b. Hyphae without spiny projections. Typically on gymnosperms (except P. australiensis)

12,0$ a. (11) Mat at first silky, whitish to Martius Yellow or Barium Yellow, becoming deeply cottony and reaching the lid of the Petri dish, sometimes Lemon Chrome ///Growth rate 12-23 mm on MEA, on ChA less. Advancing zone raised, even, hyphae rather distant. Reverse bleached after 6 weeks. Marginal hyphae (2-)2.5-7(-8) µm wide, often inequivalently branched. Submerged hyphae of 2 types: (a) 1-6 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, some with narrow much-branched side-hyphae, (b) 5-13 µm wide, thick-walled (up to 1.5 µm), without clamps, often constricted or ampullate at the septa. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, rarely subglobose, 13-22 x 11-16 µm. Aerial hyphae (1.5-)2-3.5(-5) µm wide, thin- or rarely thick-walled, often covered with yellow oil drops. On angiosperms. Australia. ///Code: (9), 12, (14), 20, 21, (30), (32), 35, 37, 39, 42, 44, (45), 48, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, (57), 58, (61), 78, (82), 83, 85, 89. $ Polyporus australiensis Wakef. 
12,13$ b. Mat at first appressed or thinly cottony, never silky, never becoming deeply cottony and never orange-yellow

13,0$ a. (12) Thick-walled hyphae or intercalary hyphal parts with many short lateral branches, present in the aerial mycelium, 1.5-3.5 µm wide. ///Growth rate 20-45 mm on ChA, on MEA sometimes less. Advancing zone appressed or raised, hyphae distant. Mat appressed or sparsely cottony, later appressed or locally downy to cottony, white to cream. Basidiocarps often present after 6 weeks. Reverse unchanged. Marginal hyphae 2-5(-6) µm wide. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, (8-)10-18(-20) x 6-8 µm. On gymnosperms, rarely angiosperms. Temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Lombard and Gilbertson, 1965. ///Code: (8), (9), 11, (12), (13), 14, 17, 21, 30, 35, 39, 42, 44, 48, (49), 52, 53, (54), 80, 85, (88), (89), 90. $ Poria xantha (Fr. ex Fr.) Cooke 
13,14$ b. Short thick-walled hyphae with many short lateral branches absent

14,15$ a. (13) Colony becoming ochraceous to pale brownish within 6 weeks
14,16$ b. Colony remaining white to pale cream

15,0$ a. (14) Chlamydospores typically lateral or terminal, 7.5-15(-25) x 5-10(-15) µm. Growth rate 25-40 mm. $ See Amylocystis lapponica under 5b 
15,0$ b. Chlamydospores typically intercalary, 8-14 x 6-13 µm. Growth rate 8-20 mm. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae dense. Mat downy, rarely woolly, white, soon becoming velvety to felty, Ochraceous Buff, Antimony Yellow to Ochraceous Tawny or Hazel, rarely Snuff Brown. Reverse darker. Colour becoming greenish-yellow in KOH. Marginal hyphae 2-3.5 µm wide, branching often from clamps. Clamps often small. Aerial hyphae hyaline to brownish, 1.5-4.5(-5) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, some with yellow resinous contents, others encrusted with brownish crystals. Sometimes oil-drops present between the hyphae. Some submerged hyphae irregularly thick-walled with meandering lumen. Crystals becoming up to 30 µm in length, octahedral or flat. On gymnosperms, mainly Pinus. N.-America, S.E. Asia. Ref.: Gilbertson et al., 1968. ///Code: 4, (9), (10), 11, 13, 17, (22), (24), 25, 26, 30, 34, 35, 38, 39, 42, 44, (45), 48, 49, (50), 52, 53, (57), (58), (59), (60), 67, (80), 82, 83, (85), (88), 90. $ Veluticeps berkeleyi (Berk. & Curt.) Cooke 

16,17$ a. (14) Growth rate 40->70 mm. Some hyphae up to 8(-9) µm wide
16,18$ b. Growth rate up to 30 mm. Hyphae not exceeding 5.5(-6.5) µm wide

17,0$ a. (16) Hyphal strands present. Mat cottony to silky, becoming locally farinaceous. Badidiocarps not formed. India. ///Growth rate 60->70 mm. Marginal hyphae 5-9 µm wide. Aerial hyphae (4-)5-9 µm wide, thin- to slightly thick-walled. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 7.5-16.5 x 5.5-12.5 µm. On angiosperms. Ref.: Sen, 1973. ///Code: (6), (7), 13, 18, 20, 21, 30, 39, (45), 48, 53, 54, 55, 81, 85, 89. $ Poria rhizomorpha Bagchee [p. 150] 
17,0$ b. Hyphal strands absent. Mat cottony or cottony-woolly, rarely plumose, white. Basidiocarps often formed, poroid to foliose. North-temperate. ///Growth rate 40->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed to raised, hyphae distant. Marginal hyphae 2-6.5(-8) µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Aerial hyphae up to 8(-16.5) µm wide, some with thickened to thick walls and swellings up to 18(-25) µm diam, often monilioid, but the swellings not separated by septa. Some aerial and submerged hyphae with irregularly thickened walls and meandering lumen. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 8-21 x 6-14 µm. On gymnosperms and angiosperms. Ref.: Cartwright and Findlay, 1958; Nobles, 1943, 1948. ///Code: (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, 21, 22, (23), 30, 31, 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), 49, (50), (51), 52, 53, 54, 55, 79, 80, 83, (85), 88, (89), (90), 93). $ Poria placenta (Fr.) Cooke  /Syn.: Poria monticola Murr.; Poria microspora Overh. apud Nobles 

18,0$ a. (16) Growth rate 15-30 mm. Hyphae regular, often with ellipsoid chlamydospore-like swellings. ///Advancing zone appressed, even, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to cottony, white. Marginal hyphae 1.2-3 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Aerial hyphae 1.5-5.5 µm wide, with thin to thickened walls, some encrusted. Chlamydospores globose, 9-15 µm diam, thin- to slightly thick-walled. On gymnosperms, rarely angiosperms. North-temperate. ///Code: 2, (8), (9), 13, 14, 17, 21, 30, 39, 42, 45, 48, 51, 52, 53, 54, 57, 80, 82, 83, 85, (89), 90. $ Osteina obducta (Berk.) Donk  /Syn.: Polyporus osseus Kalchbr. 
18,0$ b. Growth rate 2-12 mm. Hyphae often inflated or contorted, chlamydospore-like swellings absent. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae distant or rather distant. Mat at first appressed to downy or farinaceous-granulose, sometimes becoming woolly, white to cream. On and around the inoculum a rimose lobate to warty structure may develop, but no basidia are Seen within 6 weeks. Odour absent (but recorded by Martin and Gilbertson (1976) as ëpepperycinnamoní). [p. 151] Marginal hyphae 1.54(5.5) µm wide. Aerial hyphae irregular, with swellings up to 25 µm diam, thin to rarely thickwalled, often with distorted clamps; branching may be inequivalent. The lobate structures contain swellings which are often arranged in chains, often separated by septa with small clamps. Submerged hyphae up to 6.5 µm wide. Chlamydospores ellipsoid (6)7.510(12) x (5)6.59(10) µm. On gymnosperms. Temperate.  Ref.: Siepmann 1971; Martin and Gilbertson, 1976. ///Code: (9), (10), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, (18), (19), (22), 24, 30, 31, 39, 42, 44, (45), (48), 50, 52, 53, 54, (79), 80, 83, 85, (88), 90, (93). $Sparassis crispa (Wulf. ex Fr.) Fr. / Syn.: Sparassis laminosa Fr., Sparassis radicata Weir

19,20$ a. (10) Chlamydospores not exceeding 11 µm in largest diam
19,28$ b. Chlamydospores in average more than 11 µm in largest diam

20,21$ a. (19) Mat after 6 weeks yellowish or brownish and aerial chlamydospores ellipsoid, not broader than 5 µm. Tyrosinase present. Growth on ChA better than on MEA or no growth on MEA. ///Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant to distant. Chlamydospores coloured in mass, in chains, separated by septa with clamps. Most of the aerial mycelium transformed into chlamydospores. Marginal hyphae often with inequivalent branches which may be densely and somewhat dendritically branched. On gymnosperms, rarely angiosperms. /Imperf. st.: Ptychogaster.
20,22$ b. Mat remaining white or pale cream or chlamydospores. at least on average broader than 5 µm. Tyrosinase absent (as far as known). Growth on MEA and ChA similar

21,0$ a (20) Colony becoming Sayal Brown, Ochraceous Tawny or Buckthorn Brown. ///Growth rate 12-20 mm on ChA. Mat at first thinly downy, becoming farinaceous to floccose-farinaceous. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-6 µm wide. Aerial chlamydospores (5.5-)6.5-8.5(-10) x 3.5-5(-5.5) µm, submerged ones up to 16 x 9 µm. Europe. ///Code: 2, (9), 11, 13, (14), (17), 18, 19, (30), 34, 39, 42, 44, 50, 52, 53, 54, 61, 83,.85, (86), 90. [p. 152] $ Tyromyces ptychogaster (F. Ludw.) Donk  /Imperf. st.: Ptychogaster fuliginosus (Pers. ex Steud.) Donk 
21,0$ b. Colony becoming Light Buff or Pinkish Buff. ///Growth rate 15-30 mm on ChA. Mat at first downy to thinly woolly, becoming farinaceous or floccose-farinaceous. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide. Chlamydospores (4.5-)5-7.5 x 3.5-4.5 µm, wall smooth to minutely roughened. North-temperate. ///Code: 2, (8), (9), 11, 13, 14, (17), 18, 19, (22), 30, 31, 35, 39, 42, 44, 45, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), 61, 83, 85, (86), 90. $ Oligoporus rennyi (Berk. & Br.) Donk  /Imperf. st.: Ptychogaster citrinus Boud. 

22,0$ a. (20) Mat becoming distinctly yellow. $ See Merulius aureus under 43b 
22,23$ b. Mat remaining white or becoming Pinkish Buff to Cinnamon

23,0$ a. (22) Aerial hyphae often irregular, inflated or contorted, with swellings up to 25 µm diam. $ See Sparassis crispa under 18b 
23,24$ b. Aerial hyphae regular

24,25$ a. (23) Odour after 2 weeks often suggesting iodoform. Chlamydospores after 6 weeks with brownish contents or at least some with roughened walls. Mainly on gymnosperms
24,26$ b. Odour absent. Chlamydospores remaining hyaline and smooth-walled. On angiosperms (when on gymnosperms, See Parmastomyces kravtzevianus under 58b) 

25,0$ a. (24, 33) Chlamydospores ellipsoid to subglobose, 7.5-14(-16.5) x 4.5-10 µm, with brownish contents, wall remaining smooth. Colony at first white, often becoming Pinkish Buff or Cinnamon Buff to Cinnamon. ///Growth rate 20-35 mm. Advancing zone appressed, hyphae rather distant. Mat downy to cottony, sometimes plumose. Reverse unchanged or darker. Basidiocarps sometimes formed. Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-7.5 µm wide. North-temperate. Ref.: Davidson and Campbell, 1943; Nobles, 1948. ///Code: (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21, (23), 30, (31), (34), 36, (38), 39, 42, (44), 45, 52, 53, 54, 83, 85, (88), 90. $ Tyromyces balsameus (Peck) Murrill [p. 153] 
25,0$ b. Chlamydospores ellipsoid, 7.5-21 x 4.5-12 µm, hyaline, often rough-walled after 6 weeks. Colony white. ///Growth rate 8-30 µm . Advancing zone appressed or raised, hyphae distant. Mat downy to cottony-floccose, becoming cottony-woolly to sometimes plumose, sometimes felty. Reverse unchanged. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1.5-)2-5.5(-6.5) µm wide. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Lombard and Gilbertson, 1965. ///Code: (8), (9), (10), 11, (12), (13), 14, 17, (19), 21, 22, (23), (25), 30, (31), 36, 39, 42, 44, 45, (48), 52, 53, 54, 83, 85, 90. $ Tyromyces sericeomollis (Rom.) Bond. & Sing.  /Syn.: Leptoporus asiaticus Pil·t. 

26,0$ a. (24) Chlamydospores ellipsoid, rarely ovoid, 7.5-16 x 4.5-10 µm. Basidiocarps often formed within 6 weeks. Growth rate 40-60 mm. ///Advancing zone typically raised, even, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat sparsely cottony, becoming locally (sub)felty. Odour absent or distinct. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-7 µm wide, sometimes with swellings up to 12(-15) µm. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948. ///Code: (1), (2), 3, (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), (14), (17), (18), 21, (24), (25), 30, (31), (36), 39, 42, (44), 45, 52, 53, 54, 80, 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $ Bjerkandera fumosa (Pers. ex Fr.) P. Karst.  
26,0,n$ Bakshi et al. (1969) described Polyporus atypus LÈv. and P. rubidus Berk. which also key out here. According to the description, P. rubidus sometimes forms skeletal and/or binding hyphae within 6 weeks. 
26,27$ b. Chlamydospores globose to subglobose, sometimes broadly ellipsoid, 6-11 x 5-9 µm. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. ///Advancing zone appressed to typically raised, bayed, rarely even, hyphae distant. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly or floccose, becoming farinaceous to felty, white to cream. Reverse unchanged to slightly darker. Odour absent. Marginal hyphae with inequivalently much-branched laterals. Cells comperatively short, 25-80 µm.

27,0$ a. (26) Growth rate 15-30 mm. Basidiocarps not formed within 6 weeks. Marginal and aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-4(-5.5) µm wide, thin-walled. Some submerged hyphae Seem to have a meandering lumen, but the walls [p. 154] are thin and the agar is not tough. Chlamydospores 7.511 µm diam (in the agar up to 14 µm) or 7.510 x 58 µm, wall up to 1 µm thick. N.America. ///Code: (8), (9), (11), (12), (13), 14, (15), 18, (19), 21, (22), 25, 30, (31), (38), 39, (42), 44, 45, (49), 50, (51), 52, 53, (54), 61, 83, 85, 89.   †$Poria oleracea Davidson & Lombard
27,0$ b. Growth rate 20-55 mm. Basidiocarps sometimes formed within 6 weeks. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-6.5 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled. Hyphae with meandering lumen absent. Chlamydospores 6-11 x (5-)6-9 µm, wall up to 1.5 µm thick. North-temperate. Ref.: Nobles and Nordin, 1955; Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: (1), (2), (3), (7), (8), (9), (11), 12, 14, (15), 18, (19), 21, 22, (23), (25), 30, 31, (38), 39, (42), 44, 45, 50, 52, 53, 54, (61), (80), 83, 85, (88), 89, (94). $ Hypochnicium vellereum (Ell. & Cragin) Parm. 

28,29$ a. (19) Colony becoming yellowish or orange
