37,38$ a (36) Sclerotia present, becoming brown to black ///Growth rate 12-35 mm. Advancing zone raised, even, hyphae rather distant to distant. Hyphal strands present, consisting of narrow anastomosing hyphae (2.5-5 µm wide), surrounded by wider ones (6-12 µm). Marginal hyphae 2-6.5(-8) µm wide, thin- [p. 157] to firm-walled. Branches nearly always arising from clamps. Aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-8(-12) µm wide, hyaline to yellowish, thin- to thick-walled, some with resinous content. (Leucogyrophana)
37,40$ b. Sclerotia absent

38,0$ a. (37) Sclerotia more or less spindle-shaped, developing from fusiform swellings within strands. Medulla with long, rather narrow elements: textura epidermoidea. On gymnosperms. ///Mat at first cottony, white, becoming densely cottony to floccose-cottony, sometimes somewhat velvety or cottony-silky, Marguerite Yellow. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Siepmann, 1971; Ginns and Weresub, 1976. ///Code: (2), (8), (9), (11), 12, 14, (19), (20), 21, (26), 30, 31, (34), 35, (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), (48), (51), 52, 53, 54, (55), (58), 60, 66, (67), 80, 81, 90, 95, 97. $ Leucogyrophana pinastri (Fr.) Ginns & Weresub 
38,39$ b. Sclerotia more or less globose or irregular, developing as clusters of intertwined swollen cells. Medulla consisting of short, wide cells: textura angularis. On gymnosperms, more rarely angiosperms

39,0$ a. (38) Hyphal strands abundant, soon becoming dark brown to black. Sclerotia typically about 2 mm diam ///Mat at first cottony to cottony-woolly, soon with scattered white dots (sclerotium-initials), white to cream, becoming densely cottony-woolly, to (sub)felty, sometimes somewhat silky, Warm Buff to Naples Yellow, sometimes with an olivaceous tinge. North-temperate. Ref.: Harmsen, 1953; Boidin, 1958a; Ginns and Weresub, 1976. ///Code: (2), (8), (9), (11), 12, (14), (19), (20), 21, 22, (24), (25), 30, 31, (34), 35, (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), (48), (51), 52, 53, 54, (55), (58), 60, 66, 67, 80, 81, (89), (90), 95, 97. $ Leucogyrophana mollusca (Fr.) Pouzar 
39,0$ b. Hyphal strands typically restricted to the area surrounding the sclerotia, only rarely becoming dark brown to black. Sclerotia typically 3-4.5 mm diam. ///Mat downy to cottony, white, becoming cottony-woolly [p. 158] to felty, cream to Cartridge Buff or Warm Buff, locally Naples Yellow. N.-America. Ref.: Lentz and McKay, 1970; Ginns and Weresub, 1976. ///Code: (2), (8), (11), 12, 14, 17, (19), 21, (22), 25, 30, 31, (34), 35, (38), 39, 42, 44, (45), (48), 52, 53, 54, (55), (58), (60), 66, 67, 80, 81, (89), (90). $ Leucogyrophana olivascens (Berk. & Curt.) Ginns & Weresub 

40,0$ a. (37) Hyphal strands present, consisting of a medulla of narrow anastomosing hyphae surrounded by wider hyphae.///Growth rate 25-55 mm. Advancing zone raised, even or bayed, hyphae distant. Mat cottony to plumose, whitish to cream, becoming Barium Yellow, Strontium Yellow to Amber Yellow, turning reddish in KOH. Mat remaining cottony or becoming cottony-woolly, rarely somewhat silky. Reverse unchanged to slightly yellow. Odour absent or distinct but undefinable. Marginal and aerial hyphae 3-9 µm wide, branches typically arising from clamps. Some aerial hyphae thick-walled (up to 1.2 µm thick), 2-4.5 µm wide, without septa, some encrusted with yellow crystals. On gymnosperms. Temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Harmsen, 1960. ///Code: (2), (3), 4, (7), (8), (11), 12, 14, (15), (20), 21, (22), 23, 30, 31, 35, (38), 39, 42, 45, (46), 48, 52, 53, 54, 55, (57), (80), 81, 82, 83, 90. $ Serpula lacrymans (Pers. ex S. F. Gray) Schroet.  
40,0,n$ The thickwalled, aseptate hyphae can be regarded as skeletal hyphae, although they are not straight, as true skeletals should be.  /// * The only well developed strain available of Serpula tignicola (Harmsen), M. P. Christ. could not be distinguished from S. lacrymans. 
40,41$ b. Hyphal strands absent

41,42$ a †(40) Colony after 6 weeks brownish, Hazel, Ochraceous Tawny, Avellaneous. Brownish hyphae with thickened walls present
41,43$ b. Colony after 6 weeks yellowish. Coloured thick-walled hyphae absent

42,0$ a. (41) Aerial hyphae becoming very thick-walled, up to 3-4 µm, sometimes with meandering lumen. Hyphae covered with knob-like protuberances may be present. [p. 159] Ellipsoid swellings and chlamydospores typically present. $ See Veluticeps berkeleyi, under 15b. 
42,0$ b. Aerial hyphae becoming thickened (up to 0.5 µm), never with meandering lumen. Hyphae never with knoblike protuberances. Ellipsoid swellings present, but chlamydospores never observed. ///Growth rate 4-20 mm. Advancing zone raised, even to lobate, hyphae dense. Mat at first downy to floccose-woolly, soon becoming densely woolly or rarely felty, white, around the inoculum often becoming Light Buff to Avellaneous. Marginal and aerial hyphae 1.5-3 µm wide. Clamps of variable shape. Some hyphae twisted. On gymnosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: McKay and Lentz, 1960. ///Code: (9), (10), (11), 12, (15), 17, (19), 22, (25), 30, 31, (34), (35), (36), 38, 39, 42, 45, 48, 52, 53, (67), 80, 83, 90. $ Chaetoderma luna (Romell) Parm. 

43,0$ a. (41) Thick-walled hyphae or intercalary hyphal parts with many short branches present in the aerial mycelium. $ See Poria xantha, under 13a 
43,0$ b. Thick-walled much-branched hyphae or hyphal portions absent. ///Growth rate 3-15(-30) mm. Advancing zone appressed, rarely raised, even to bayed, hyphae rather distant. Mat cottony to cottony-woolly, later sometimes (sub)felty, whitish or yellowish from the beginning, becoming Barium Yellow, Straw Yellow, Pale Olive Buff or Antimony Yellow. Reverse typically darker. Marginal hyphae (1-)1.5-3(-4) µm wide, thin- to sometimes firm-walled. Aerial hyphae hyaline to rarely yellowish, 1.5-4 µm wide, with thin to thickened walls, often covered with yellowish crystals. Globose to ellipsoid swellings may be present in the aerial and submerged mycelium. Mainly on gymnosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Harmsen, 1960; Sen, 1973; Ginns, 1976. ///Code: (9), (10), (11), (12), (13), (14), (15), 21, 22, (24), (25), (30), (31), 35, 38, 39, 42, (44), 45,, (48), (51), 52, 53, 57, (67), (80), 82, 83, (89), 90, (94). $ Serpula Pers. ex S. F. Gray  Merulius aureus Fr.  
43,0,n$ Sen described S. himantioides (Fr.) P. Karst. with a crustose layer, while S. similis (Berk. & Br.) Ginns (as Merulius eurocephalus (Berk. & Br.) Petch) [p. 160] would have considerably wider hyphae (up to 7.5 µm). 

44,0$ a. (36) Colony becoming Light Windsor Blue, Dark Aniline Blue or Urania Blue. $ See Pulcherricium caeruleum, key D under 78b 
44,45$ b. Colony not blue

