4,0$ b. Hyphae up to 9(-11) µm wide. Septa with up to 5 clamps. Marginal hyphae septate, cell length 150-400 µm. ///Mat after 6 weeks downy to floccose or thinly woolly. Marginal hyphae 2.5-8.5(-10) µm wide, with thin to slightly thickened walls. Aerial hyphae 2-9(-11) µm wide, sometimes encrusted, thin to thick-walled, septa with 0-5 clamps. Submerged hyphae often with swellings, up to 16 µm diam. Chlamydospores 15-30 µm diam. On angiosperms. Europe. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: (1),. 3, (5), (6), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, 19, (21), 22, 30, (39), (40), 41, (42), 44, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, (57), 80, 82, 83, (85), (89), 96, 98, 100. $ Peniophora macrospora Bres. ex Bourd. & Galz.  
4,0,n$ Chlamydospores do not always  Seem to be present; Boidin only observed them on Hagem agar. Our cultures (sent by Boidin) showed them in abundance, while clamp connections were rather rare.

5,0$ a. (2) Blastoconidia terminal or on short lateral branches, sometimes slightly oblique, with broad bases, narrowly ellipsoid, (11-)14-23 x 8-13 µm, thick-walled. Colony Cinnamon Buff, Light Vinaceous Cinnamon or Pinkish Cinnamon, after 6 weeks Onion-skin Pink to Vinaceous Tawny. Arthroconidia absent. ///Growth rate 55->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, [p. 169] even, hyphae distant. Mat at first appressed, becoming sparsely downy to farinaceous, whitish to cream, finally wrinkled and with some arachnoid whitish mycelium. Reverse unchanged. Marginal hyphae 3-7.5(-12) µm wide, sometimes with swellings. Septa rarely with 1-4 clamps. Cells typically 25-150 µm long. Aerial hyphae 2.5-8 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, rarely with clamps. In the arachnoid mycelium cylindrical conidia, 8-13 x 1.5-2 µm, are formed terminally on blunt protuberances in small groups of usually 34. Specialized conidiophores absent. Submerged hyphae 3-9(-12) µm wide, often thick-walled (wall up to 4 µm thick). In soil or human or animal skin, rarely wood. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: von Arx, 1971. ///Code: (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, 17, 18, (30), (31), 33, (34), (39), (40), 41, 44, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, 80, (83), 86. $ Sporotrichum dimorphosporum v. Arx 
5,0,n$ The strain QM 806 is this species. It has often been used for the production of exoﬂD1,3 glucanase (Reese and Mandels, 1959) which causes almost complete solubilization of cell walls of yeasts (Bauer et al., 1972).
5,6$ b. Blastoconidia absent. Colony white or yellowish. Arthroconidia present

6,0$ a. (6) Mat at first appressed to downy, soon becoming farinaceous to floccose, white. Clamp connections rare, multiple clamps very rare. ///Basidiocarps sometimes formed. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)2-6(-11) µm wide. Septa rarely with 1-2(-3) clamps. Aerial hyphae 2-6 µm wide, with thin to thickened walls, sometimes encrusted. Arthroconidia abundant, 24.5 µm diam. Submerged hyphae thin- to thick-walled (wall up to 1 µm thick), sometimes with swellings. On gymnosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Biggs, 1938; Nobles, 1948; Ka‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: (1), (2), 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, (16), 17, 18, (19), (21), 30, 37, (39), (40), (41), (42), 44, (45), 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, (57), (80), 82, 83, 84, (88), (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Peniophora gigantea (Fr. ex Fr.) Mas See ????
6,0$ b. Mat cottony or cottony-woolly, deep, later often appressed and subfelty, typically with hyphal strands or rhizomorphs, cream to Colonial Buff, Tawny Olive and rarely Sayall Brown. Multiple clamps abundant. [p. 170] ///Growth rate 50->70 mm. Advancing zone raised, even or somewhat fan-shaped, hyphae rather. distant to distant. Reverse yellowish. Marginal hyphae 2.5-14 µm wide. Septa with 0-8 clamps. Clamps often sprouting. Some narrow hyphae much-branched, witchesí broom-like; wide hyphae often somewhat constricted at- the septa. Aerial hyphae hyaline to yellowish, 2.5-8(-15) µm wide, sometimes with slightly thickened walls, often somewhat irregular, some covered with yellowish material, others with yellowish contents. Hyphal strands consisting of broad, thinwalled and narrow firm-walled hyphae. Arthroconidia typically abundant, 3-6.5 µm diam. On gymnosperms, rarely angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Robak, 1936; Kemper, 1937; Davidson et al., 1942; Nobles, 1948; Boidin, 1958a; van der Westhuizen, 1958. ///Code: (6), (7), (11), 12, (14), (15), (17), 21, (22), (23),24, (30), 31, (34), 35, 38, (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (48), 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, 58, 67, 78, (80), 81, 83, (84), (89), (90), (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Coniophora DC. ex MÈrat 

6,0,n$ It is not possible to differentiate Coniophora at the species level in culture. This description not only includes C. arida (Fr.) P. Karst., C. puteana (Schum. ex Fr.) P. Karst., C. suffocata (Peck) Mas See, species which are distinguished in nature by unimportant and variable characters, but also the cystidiate C. olivacea (Fr.) P. Karst. /// * The "brown hyphae with knoblike or bulbous protuberances" described for C. puteana by Nobles (1948) are probably hyphae with oil drops or resinous material. The wall itself is not rough.

7,8$ a. Growth rate typically 45->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed, even, hyphae rather distant to distant. Mat after 2 weeks appressed to downy, rarely farinaceous, remaining so or becoming thinly woolly to subfelty. Colony white or cream to ochraceous. Reverse typically bleached. Marginal hyphae thin-walled, (1.5-)2.5-8(-12) µm wide. Septa with 0-4(-5) clamps. Aerial hyphae thin- to thick-walled, sometimes encrusted with small crystals. Submerged hyphae thin- to thick-walled. (Phanerochaete)
7,9$ b. Broad hyphae typically thin- or only slightly thick-walled; [p. 171] when thick-walled the thickening is regular and does not exceed 1 µm. Narrow, thick-walled hyphae, (0.7-2) µm wide, may be present. Hyphal strands absent

8,0$ a. (7) Growth rate 45->70 mm. Mat at first downy, white, becoming thinly cottony to plumose or fan-shaped, cream to Warm Buff or Ochraceous Buff. After 6 weeks often woolly. Marginal hyphae 3-8(-12) µm wide. Septa with 0-4 clamps. Aerial hyphae (1.5-)3-8 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, some hyphae with brownish content, some encrusted. Cystidium-like structures sometimes present, thick-walled, with apical swelling(s). Submerged hyphae (2-)4-8(-12) µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, some with narrow, much branched lateral hyphae, 2-3.5 µm wide. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), (16), 17, 21, 22, 23, (24), (25), 30, 31, 35, (39), (40),-41, 42, 44, 48, 50, (52), 53, 54, 55, (57), (61), (67), (72), (80), 82, 83, 89, 96, 97, 100. $ Phanerochaete affinis (Burt) Parm.
8,0$ b.* Growth rate 45-60 mm. Mat at first appressed, later thinly woolly to floccose, white. Marginal hyphae 3.5-5.5 µm wide. Septa with 0-3(4) clamps. Aerial and submerged hyphae thin- to thick-walled, 3-7.5 µm wide, some with narrow, much branched laterals. Cystidia absent. N.-America. Ref.: Burdsall & Gilbertson, 1974. ///Code: 1, (7), 13, 14, (17), 19, 22, 30, 37, (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (48), 50, (52), 5 3, 54, 61, 89. $ Phanerochaete arizonica Burdsall & Gilbertson
8,0$ c.* Plate covered in 1-2 weeks. Mat appressed at the margin, becoming downy or thinly cottony, to subfelty, locally densely cottony, often with hyphal strands. Colony cream to Cinnamon Buff, rarely whitish. Marginal hyphae 3-8 µm wide. Septa with 0-4 clamps. Aerial hyphae 3.5-6 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, often covered with yellowish resinous material or small crystals. Cystidia absent. Submerged hyphae 3-8 µm wide, thin- to firm-walled, sometimes with irregular much-branched laterals or knob-like protuberances. Europe ///Code: (1), 2, 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, 21,24, (30), 31, 35, 37, (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, 50, (52), 53, 54, 55, (57) 58, (61), 82, 83, 89. $ Peniophora ericina Bourd. [p. 172]
8,0$ d.* Plate covered in 1-2 weeks. Mat at first appressed, becoming downy to floccose or thinly woolly, white. Marginal hyphae 2.5-8.5(-10) µm wide. Septa with 0-5 clamps. Aerial hyphae 2-9(-11) µm wide, sometimes encrusted, thin- to thick-walled. Cystidia absent. Submerged hyphae thin- to thick-walled, often with swellings up to 16 µm diam. $ See Peniophora macrospora under 4b
8,0$ e.* Growth rate 20-30 mm on MEA, on ChA only 10-15 mm. Mat appressed at the extreme margin, downy elsewhere, rarely becoming thinly cottony or plumose, white; hyphal strands sometimes present. Marginal hyphae 2.5-5(-8) µm wide. Septa with 0-2(-4) clamps. Aerial hyphae (2.5-)4-10 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled, some with resinous contents, some encrusted with crystals. Cystidium-like structures sometimes present, thick-walled, with apical swelling(s), often encrusted. Submerged hyphae thin- to thick-walled, sometimes with narrow much-branched laterals. North-temperate ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (8), (9), 13, (14), (15), 17, (21), (23), 30, (31), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, 57, (60), (72), (78), (81), 82, 83, 89. $ Peniophora roumeguerii (Bres.) Hˆhn. & Litsch.
8,0$ f.* Growth rate 45-70 mm. Mat appressed to downy, becoming thinly woolly, white. Marginal hyphae 2.5-6.5(-12) µm wide. Septa with 0-4(-5) clamps. Aerial and submerged hyphae (1.5-)2.5-8 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: (1), (2), 3, (6), (7), (11), 13, (14), 17, (21), 22, 30, (37), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, 83, 90, (97), (98). $ Phanerochaete sanguinea (Fr.) Hjortstam
8,0$ g.* Plate covered in 2 weeks. Mat after 2 weeks appressed, rarely locally farinaceous, or locally slightly downy or thinly woolly or cottony, white. Marginal hyphae 1.5-10 µm wide. Septa with 0-4(-5) clamps. Aerial hyphae (when present) 2-6 µm wide, often encrusted with needle-like crystals, thin- to thick-walled. Cystidia absent. Submerged hyphae 2-8 µm wide, sometimes with narrow much-branched hyphae, often constricted at the septa. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: (1), (2), (3), (6), (11), 13, (14), 16, (18), (21), (22), (24), (30), 37, (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (45), 48, 50, 52, 53, 54, 55, (57), 61, 78, (82), 83, 89, 96, 98, 100. $ Phanerochaete tuberculata (P. Karst.) Parm. [p. 173]
8,0$ h.* Growth rate 50-65 mm on MEA (on ChA 35-45 mm). Mat appressed at the margin, becoming long-cottony, thin but rather high. Hyphal strands often present. Colony cream to Light Buff or Light Ochraceous Buff. Marginal hyphae (3-)4-12 µm wide, thin- to thick-walled. Septa with 0-5 clamps. Aerial hyphae hyaline to yellowish, 4-15 µm wide, often encrusted with crystals. Hyphal strands with thick-walled anastomosing hyphae. Submerged hyphae up to 15 µm wide, often constricted at the septa. On gymnosperms and angiosperms. North-temperate. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, (7), 11, 13, 14, 17, 21, (24), (30), 31, 35, 37, (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, 48, 50, 53, 54, 55, 57, (58), 78, (80), (81), 82, 83, (89), (90), 96, 97, 100. $ Phanerochaete velutina (DC. ex Fr.) P. Karst.

9,10$ a. (7) Mat after 4 weeks hyaline or white (if coloured, then only against the side of the dish). Narrow (0.7-1.5 µm), thick-walled aerial hyphae absent. Clamps 0-2 per septum, typically rare and only found in an approx. 10 mm wide marginal region. ///Growth rate 50->70 mm. Advancing zone appressed or minutely downy, even, rarely bayed, hyphae rather distant or distant. Reverse unchanged or slightly bleached. (Meruliopsis)
9,11$ b. Mat after 4 weeks cream to ochraceous. Narrow (0.7-1.5 µm), thick-walled aerial hyphae typically present. Clamps 0-4(-6) per septum, typically abundant everywhere in the colony

10,0$ a †(9) On angiosperms. Plate covered in 2 weeks. Mat at first downy, becoming floccose to woolly or cottony-woolly. Odour absent. Submerged hyphae often irregular and with swellings (up to 11.5 µm diam). Marginal and aerial hyphae 2-7(-8.5) µm wide. Swellings absent. North-temperate. Ref.: Lombard and Gilbertson, 1965. ///Code: (1), (3), (6), (7), (11), 13, 14, 17, 19, (21), 22, (24), 30, (37), (39), (40), 41, 44, (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), (78), 80, 82, 83, 89. $ Ceriporia excelsa (Lundell) Parm.  /Syn.: Ceriporia viridans (Berk. & Br.) Donk Misappl.: Poria rhodella (Fr.) Cooke.
10,0$ b.* Mainly on angiosperms. Plate covered in 2 weeks. Mat cottony-floccose to woolly-floccose, whitish; sometimes [p. 174] tan-coloured towards the side of the dish. Odour fruity. Hyphae 2-5.5(-9)m wide, some wavy or nearly moniliform. Hyphae in tan-coloured areas pale yellowish. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Ginns, 1976. ///Code: (1), (6), (12), (13), 14, 19, 21, 22, 30, (31), (35), (36), 37, (39), (40), 41, 44, (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), (61), (67), (79), 80, (82), 83, 89, (90). $ Meruliopsis ambiguus (Berk.) Ginns
10,0$ l0c.* Mainly on angiosperms. Growth rate 45-65 mm. Mat downy to farinaceous-granular, sometimes floccose or thinly cottony. Odour absent. Hyphae (1.5-)2-6(-8.5) µm wide. Hyphal knots often present. Swellings very rare or absent. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Ginns, 1976. ///Code: (1), (2), 3, (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), (16), 17, 18, (19), (21), 30, (37), (39), (40), (41), 42, 44, (45), (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), (65), (80), (82), 83, (88), 89, (90), (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Meruliopsis corium (Fr.) Ginns  /Syn.: Merulius confluens Schw.  
10,0,n$ According to Ginns (1976), the growth rate of M. ambiguus at 35∞C is 70 mm, while that of M. corium is about 15 mm.
10,0$ d.* On angiosperms and gymnosperms. Plate covered in (1-)2 weeks. Mat at first appressed, chamois-like, later downy to thinly cottony-woolly. Odour absent. Hyphae 2-8 µm wide. Clavate hyphal swellings uncommon to rare, 20-50 x 6.5m. North-temperate. Ref.: Ginns, 1976. ///Code: (5), (6), 13, 14, (16), 17, (21), 22, 30, (39), (40), 41, 44, (50), 52, 53, 54, (55), 75, (89), (90). $ Meruliopsis hirtellus (Burt) Ginns
10,0$ e.* On gymnosperms. Growth rate 60->70 mm. Mat downy to thinly cottony or cottony-floccose. Odour absent. Hyphae 2-6(-8) µm wide, rarely much-branched to form a witchesí broom. Coiled hyphae and terminal swellings sometimes present. Temperate. Ref.: Nobles, 1948; Lombard and Gilbertson, 1965; Ginns, 1976. ///Code: 3, (6), (7), 11, (12), (13), 14, (15), 17, (18), (19), 21, (24), 30, (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, 50, 52, 53, 54, (55), (75), 83, 90, (92). $ Meruliopsis taxicola (Pers.) Bond.

11,0$ a. (9) Laccase and peroxidase absent. Mat just behind the advancing zone deeply cottony or cottony-woolly, often [p. 175] with hyphal strands. $ See Coniophora under 6b
11,12$ b. Laccase and peroxidase present, when absent colonies downy or low thinly woolly

12,13$ a. (11) Mat immediately behind the advancing zone dense and deeply cottony or cottony-woolly or plumose, later often felty. ///Growth rate 50->70 mm. Advancing zone raised, appressed, even, hyphae rather distant to distant. Colony at first white, later cream or ochraceous, the yellowish parts often turning red with KOH. Widest marginal hyphae typically exceeding 7.5 µm in width. Septa with 04(-6) clamps. Aerial mycelium sometimes with firm or thickened walls and often covered with yellowish resinous drops. Narrow hyphae (0.7-1.5 µm) with firm to thickened walls typically present. Coiled hyphae often present. On angiosperms (Stereum s. str.).
12,14$ b. Mat downy, thinly woolly or floccose, not deeply cottony or plumose

13,0$ a. (12) Colony cream, Light Ochraceous Buff or Maize Yellow. Marginal hyphae 2-7(-12) µm wide. Aerial hyphae (0.5-)1-8 µm wide. Mainly on Quercus. Europe Ref.: Boidin, 1958a. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (4), (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 21, (22), 23, (24), 25, 30, 31, 35, (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (48), 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 58, (82), 83, 89, (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Stereum gausapatum (Fr.) Fr.  
13,0,n$ This description covers the European strains of S. gausapatum; strains originating from N.America are different (See under 19a). This difference is also reflected in the spore dimensions, those of European specimens (610 x 3.54.5 µm) being larger than those of American specimens (57.5(8.5) x 23 µm).
13,0$ b.* Colony cream to Buff Yellow to Light Antimony Yellow. Marginal hyphae (1.5-)3-10 µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1-10(-12) µm wide. On various angiosperms. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Cartwright & Findlay, 1958; van der Westhuizen, 1958; Siepmann, 1969. ///Code: 1, 2, 3, 4, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), (19), 21, (22), (23), 24, 25, 30, 31, 35, (38), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, 48, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 58, (60), 83, 89, (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Stereum hirsutum (Willd. ex Fr.) S. F. Gray [p. 176]
13,0,n$  This description also fits: S. subtomentosum Pouzar; S. complicatum (Fr.) Fr.; S. rameale (Schw.) Burt.
13,0$ c.* Colony cream to Light Ochraceous Buff, rarely Warm Buff, becoming Light Buff, sometimes locally Mummy Brown. Marginal hyphae 1.5-6(-8) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1-10 µm wide, sometimes with resinous content. Some hyphae irregular, with knob-like projections. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Boidin, 1958a; Siepmann, 1969. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, 4, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), 14, 21, (22), 23, (24), 25, 30, 31, (34), 35, (37), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, 48, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, (58), (60), 80, 83, 89, (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Stereum rugosum (Pers. ex Fr.) Fr.
13,0$ d.* Colony cream to Pale Ochraceous Buff to Cream Color. Marginal hyphae 2-9(-11) µm wide. Aerial hyphae 1-8 µm wide, some with resinous content. Australia, New Zealand. Ref.: Refshauge and Proctor, 1935. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 21, 22, 23, (24), 30, 31, 35, (38), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (48), 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, (58), 60, 83, 89, (90), (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Stereum vellereum Berk.

14,0$ a. (12) Gloeocystidia 110-300 x 3.5-4 µm, sometimes with inflations up to 12 µm diam. Mat after 3 weeks with white ridges which later become pinkish ///Growth rate 45-65 mm. Advancing zone appressed. Colony thinly woolly, with drops of exudate around the inoculum, cream to Light Ochraceous Buff, somewhat darker at the inoculum. Basidiocarp-initial salmon. Reverse unchanged. Hyphae hyaline, thinwalled, 2-8 µm wide, often inequivalently branched. Septa with 0-4 clamps. Dichohyphidia with long, narrow branches, 1-2 µm wide, with thickened walls. Central Africa. Code: 1, (2), (7), 13, (21), 22, (24), (27), 29, 30, 31, (33), 35, (39), (40), 41, 44, (47), (48), 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, 55, 73, (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Vararia insolita Boidin & Lanquetin
14,15$ b. Gloeocystidia absent. Mat without white ridges. Advancing zone typically appressed, even, hyphae rather distant. Colony at first appressed, downy, thinly cottony or thinly woolly, later often floccose or (sub)felty. Yellowish parts (when present) often [p. 177] red with KOH. Septa with 0-2(-6) clamps. Aerial hyphae hyaline, rarely slightly yellowish, with thin- to thickened walls, some covered with yellowish resinous drops. Narrow hyphae with thickened walls often present, sometimes dichotomously branched.

15,0$ a. (14) On gymnosperms. Odour sweet. Cystidia (conducting cells) often present, hyaline to brown, thick-walled, expanded at the apex, cyanophilous, often with somewhat granular contents, 4.5-6.5(-7) µm diam. ///Growth rate 30-50 mm. Advancing zone even or bayed, hyphae distant. Reverse unchanged or darker. Colony white to cream to Buff Yellow, some places may be chamois or darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-7 µm wide. Septa rarely with more than 2 clamps. Aerial hyphae (1-)1.5-6(-6.5) µm wide, septa occasionally with up to 3(-5) clamps. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Robak, 1936, 1942; Nobles, 1948; Ka‰rik and Rennerfelt, 1957; Boidin, 1958a; van der Westhuizen, 1958; Siepmann & Zycha, 1968. ///Code: (1), 3, (7), (8), (11), (12), (13), 14, (15), 17, (18), (19), 21, (22), 24, (25), 30, 31, (34), (35), (36), (38), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (48), 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, (60), (67), (73), (82), 83, 90, (92), 96, 98,100. $ Stereum sanguinolentum (Alb. & Schw. ex Fr.) Fr.
15,16$ b. On angiosperms. Odour absent. Cystidia absent (but $ See note under Stereum ostrea)16 

16,17$ a. (15) Growth rate 15-40 mm (Gloeocystidiellum)
16,18$ b. Growth rate at least 40 mm

17,0$ a. (16) Marginal hyphae 2.5-8(-9.5) µm wide. Colony white to cream to Light Buff or Salmon Buff. Reverse unchanged. ///Septa with 0-6 clamps. Aerial hyphae (1-)2-6 µm wide, some with yellowish contents; wall thin to thickened. Central Africa. Ref.: Boidin, 1966. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), (19), 21, 22, 30, 31, (33),(35), (39), (40), 41, 44, 48, 50, (51), 52, 53, 54, 55, (60), (67), (78), 83, 89, (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Gloeocystidiellum flammeum Boidin
17,0$ b. Marginal hyphae 1.5-5 µm wide. Colony cream to Chamois or Yellow Ocher, locally Buckthorn Brown to Cinnamon. Reverse darker. [p. 178]///Septa with 0-3 clamps. Some marginal hyphae with many short laterals branching at right angles. Aerial hyphae 1-6(-9) µm wide. Brown hyphae thick-walled. Swellings often present. Central Africa. Ref.: Boidin, 1966. ///Code: 1, 3, (8), (9), (11), 13, (14), 17, 18, (22), (24), 30, 31, (34), 35, 38, (39), (40), 41, 44, 48, (50), 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), 58, (61), 67, 80, 83, 89, (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Gloeocystidiellum heimii Boidin

18,0$ a. (16) Aerial hyphae thin- to firm-walled, 3.5-6 µm wide. $ See Peniophora ericina, under 8c
18,19$ b At least some aerial hyphae much narrower than 3 µm

19,0$ a. (18) Growth rate 40->70 mm. Mat at first appressed to downy or floccose, soon becoming low woolly to felty, whitish, Light Buff, Ochraceous Buff, Cinnamon Buff to Ochraceous Tawny. Reverse unchanged or darker. Marginal hyphae 1.5-8 µm wide. Septa with 0-4(-6) clamps. Aerial mycelium 1-4(-5.5) µm wide, with thin to slightly thickened walls, some with yellowish contents, some covered with resinous material or rarely with small crystals, some with monilioid swellings which are not separated by septa. N.-America. Ref.: Davidson et al., 1942. ///Code: 1, (2), 3, 4, (6), (7), (11), (12), (13), (14), 17, (19), (21), 22, 24, (25), (30), 31, 35, (38), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (48), 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, (55), (57), 58, (60), (67), 79, 80, (82), 83, 89, (92), 96, 98, 100. $ Stereum gausapatum (Fr.) Fr. 
19,0,n$ American strains only,   See note under 11a
19,0$ b. Growth rate 35-50 mm. Mat appressed at the margin, becoming floccose to low cottony-woolly, rarely somewhat farinaceous, finally felty to floccose-felty, white to Colonial Buff, or Antimony Yellow, sometimes Yellow Ocher, after 6 weeks Primuline Yellow to Ochraceous Buff, turning red in KOH. Reverse unchanged. Marginal hyphae 2-7 µm wide. Septa with 0-4 short and broad clamps. Aerial hyphae with thin to thickened walls, 1-4.5(-6.5) µm wide, sometimes much branched, often covered with yellow resinous material. Narrow hyphae often thick-walled. Cosmopolitan. Ref.: Refshauge & Proctor, 1935; Boidin, 1958. Code: 1, 3, 4, (7), (8), (11), 13, (14), (17), (18), 19, 21, 22, 24, 25, 30, 31, 35, (38), (39), (40), 41, 42, 44, (48), 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 57, (58), 82, 83, 89, (92), 96, 98, 100. [p. 179] $ Stereum ostrea (Blume & Nees) Fr.  Incl.: Stereum fasciatum (Schw.) Fr.; Stereum lobatum (Kuntze ex Fr.) Fr.; Stereum insignitum QuÈl. 
19,0,n$ Boidin (1958), mentioned cystidioid structures (terminal, thickwalled, 30-400 x 2.55 µm) in S. insignitum. These were neither found in the cultures studied, nor mentioned by other authors

